<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The first author is supported by NSF DMS-2404843</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The second author is supported by NSF DMS-2345030, NSF DMS-2506403, and Simons Foundation’s Travel Support for Mathematicians.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shaoyun Bai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>MIT, 77 Massachusetts Avenue Cambridge, MA 02139, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:shaoyunb@mit.edu">shaoyunb@mit.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guangbo Xu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics, Rutgers University, Hill Center–Busch Campus, 110 Frelinghuysen Road, Piscataway, NJ 08854-8019, USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:guangbo.xu@rutgers.edu">guangbo.xu@rutgers.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Continuing our previous work on the integral Arnold conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we establish the foundation of Hamiltonian Floer theory over integer coefficients on any compact symplectic manifold. This package produces a well-defined chain homotopy class of Floer complexes, chain-level continuation maps, the Piunikhin–Salamon–Schwarz (PSS) isomorphism, and an associative pair-of-pants product. When the Hamiltonian is the <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration for a prime number <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also establish the <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Floer theory in characteristic <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>, including the equivariant Floer complex and the <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant pair-of-pants product. Moreover, we define quantum Steenrod operations for any compact symplectic manifold and compare them with the equivariant pair-of-pants products via the <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant PSS map. We also obtain chain-level invariants such as spectral numbers and barcodes and prove quantitative properties of the equivariant pair-of-pants product.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">The counting theory is based on Fukaya–Ono’s normally polynomial perturbation scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and its realization by the authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, termed FOP perturbations, adapted in the abstract setting of flow categories, flow multimodules, and homotopies of flow bimodules. Along the way, we construct global Kuranishi charts for the relevant moduli spaces following Abouzaid–McLean–Smith’s framework and its adaptation to the Hamiltonian Floer setting due to the authors, which may be of independent interest.</p>
  
</div>
