<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Floer theory is one of the most influential discoveries in geometry and topology. In symplectic topology, the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian Floer homology</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> provides a powerful framework for studying Hamiltonian dynamics. Most notably, it leads to proofs of various versions of the famous Arnold conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Several variations, such as quantitative Floer theory and local Floer theory, played important roles in many other applications in Hamiltonian dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The development of Floer homology was accompanied by the advancement of the understanding of pseudoholomorphic curves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and regularizations of their moduli spaces. In earlier works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> where certain vanishing or positivity conditions were assumed, when defining the Floer complexes, one only needs to consider moduli spaces of virtual dimensions at most one; one can achieve transversality of such moduli spaces by perturbing geometrically and can obtain Floer complexes with coefficients in <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When the semipositivity condition was removed, there is no known way to achieve transversality via geometric perturbations without imposing further conditions (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>). The abstract perturbation methods used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> require one to regularize the moduli spaces via <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kuranishi models</span> or <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">polyfolds</span> and to apply virtual perturbations or integrations. One then has to deal with moduli spaces of negative virtual dimensions and to organize all the constructions in a compatible way which can be formalized using Cohen–Jones–Segal’s notion of <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">flow categories</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. The lack of geometric transversality is closely intertwined with the orbispace structure of the moduli spaces, which naturally leads to fractional counts of curves and chain-level structures only defined over <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main purpose of the current work is to extend Floer theory from rational to integer coefficients for general closed symplectic manifolds. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> we have already established a portion of the integral Floer theory which provides a proof of the Arnold conjecture over <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The motivation to go beyond the scope of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, besides a complete package of the integral Hamiltonian Floer theory, resides in the potential applications in Hamiltonian dynamics. For this purpose, we will construct quantum Steenrod operations for general symplectic manifolds (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> under semipositivity or stronger assumptions). In an upcoming joint work with Shelukhin and Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we will extend many dynamical applications (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>) of quantum Steenrod operations to greater generality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The integral extension is possible due to the recent developments in the virtual technique. The most crucial idea was given by Fukaya–Ono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. Roughly speaking, the moduli spaces carry natural stable complex structures, which allow a specific type of single-valued perturbations (normally polynomial perturbations) resulting in well-defined integer counts. Fukaya–Ono discussed the original idea for a local Kuranishi model, while B. Parker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> further explored strategies to globalize. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> we provide a rigorous treatment of Fukaya–Ono’s proposal and define the so-called “FOP transversality condition” for normally polynomial perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Another crucial concurrent development is the Abouzaid–McLean–Smith construction of global Kuranishi charts (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>). This new technique helps avoid the complicated inductive arguments involving multiple charts (such as having good coordinate systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>). Meanwhile, the smoothing argument of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> replaces the hard analytic approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> by a soft topological approach. These novel ideas helped significantly in the case of Hamiltonian Floer theory. One of the main challenges in Floer theory is to carry out AMS construction for infinitely many moduli spaces compatibly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We hope that the foundational work laid in this paper can serve as a useful package for applications of Floer theory to Hamiltonian dynamics on general compact symplectic manifolds. Our joint paper with Shelukhin and Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> showcases how the technical work done here can efficiently lead to interesting statements for Hamiltonian maps on general compact symplectic manifolds. Meanwhile, we hope the technical procedures can serve as a model to be adopted in other situations such as in Lagrangian Floer theory or contact topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results fall into three parts:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integral Hamiltonian Floer theory at the chain level (Theorems <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>—<a href="#Thmthma7" title="Theorem G. ‣ 1.1.5. Comparison with known construction ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equivariant Floer theory and quantum Steenrod operations (Theorems <a href="#Thmthma8" title="Theorem H (Quantum Steenrod operation on general symplectic manifold). ‣ 1.2.1. The quantum Steenrod operations ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>—<a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantitative refinements, including spectral invariants and barcodes (Theorems <a href="#Thmthma11" title="Theorem K (Filtered Floer complex and continuation map). ‣ 1.3.1. Action filtration and spectral invariants ‣ 1.3. Main results on quantitative Floer theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a>—<a href="#Thmthma13" title="Theorem M (Barcode stretching under prime iteration). ‣ 1.3.3. Quantitative features of equivariant Floer theory ‣ 1.3. Main results on quantitative Floer theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>) as well as results about Floer complexes of homologically nontrivial orbits (Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm11" title="Theorem 1.11 (Filtered Floer chain complex for noncontractible periodic orbits). ‣ 1.3.4. Quantitative Hamiltonian Floer theory for noncontractible orbits ‣ 1.3. Main results on quantitative Floer theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.11</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm12" title="Theorem 1.12. ‣ 1.3.4. Quantitative Hamiltonian Floer theory for noncontractible orbits ‣ 1.3. Main results on quantitative Floer theory ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.12</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Below we state these main results in a way that readers can directly cite.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Main results on Floer complexes</h4>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.1. </span>Floer complexes and Floer homology</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First and foremost, by applying the FOP perturbation scheme to the moduli spaces of Floer trajectories, we construct a Floer complex with integer coefficients, which is well-defined up to chain homotopy equivalence. Under certain restrictions of the Hamiltonian, such a complex was constructed by the authors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> without proving the homotopy equivalence between different choices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start by recalling the basic setup. Let <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic manifold. Let</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}(\omega)\cap{\rm ker}(c_{1})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}(\omega)\cap{\rm ker}(c_{1})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is a finitely generated free abelian group. Let <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Novikov ring with integer coefficients:</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}=\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{i}T^{A_{i}}\ |\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ A_{i}\in\Pi,\ \lim_{i\to\infty}\omega(A_{i})=+\infty\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}=\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{i}T^{A_{i}}\ |\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ A_{i}\in\Pi,\ \lim_{i\to\infty}\omega(A_{i})=+\infty\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate 1-periodic Hamiltonian generating a 1-periodic family of Hamiltonian vector fields <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{H_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{H_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a 1-periodic orbit is a map <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x:S^{1}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x:S^{1}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> solving</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}(t)=X_{H_{t}}(x(t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}(t)=X_{H_{t}}(x(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">capped 1-periodic orbit</span> is a pair <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(x,\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(x,\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a 1-periodic orbit and <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence class of extensions of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to the disk <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where two extensions are equivalent if their difference, regarded as a map from <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, has zero symplectic area and zero Chern number. Each capped 1-periodic orbit <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> has a well-defined symplectic action</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p)=-\int_{\mathbb{D}}\tilde{x}^{*}\omega-\int_{S^{1}}H_{t}(x(t))dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p)=-\int_{\mathbb{D}}\tilde{x}^{*}\omega-\int_{S^{1}}H_{t}(x(t))dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and a well-defined Conley–Zehnder index</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm deg}p:={\rm CZ}(p)\in{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>CZ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm deg}p:={\rm CZ}(p)\in{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Denote the corresponding cohomological grading of capped periodic orbits by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|p|:=\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}X-{\rm CZ}(p)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>CZ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p|:=\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}X-{\rm CZ}(p).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all contractible 1-periodic orbits and <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all capped 1-periodic orbits.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmthma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Floer chain complex)<span id="Thmthma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic manifold. Let <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a nondegenerate 1-periodic Hamiltonian. Then for each <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic family of <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structures <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and a certain choice <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear differential on the chain group</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="CF_{*}(H):=\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{i}p_{i}\ |\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ p_{i}\in\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(H),\ \lim_{i\to\infty}{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p_{i})=-\infty\Big\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H):=\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}a_{i}p_{i}\ |\ a_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\ p_{i}\in\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(H),\ \lim_{i\to\infty}{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p_{i})=-\infty\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(which has the structure of a module over <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>), making it a chain complex of finitely generated free <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with a <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading defined by the Conley–Zehnder index. Moreover, the chain homotopy type of <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the choice <math id="Thmthma1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next we establish the continuation map with expected properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmthma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Continuation Map)<span id="Thmthma2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be two triples such that the Floer chain complexes</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi}),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi}),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are defined.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-linear chain homotopy equivalence, called the continuation map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{12}(\Theta):CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{12}(\Theta):CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whose definition further depends on a system of choices </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The chain homotopy class of the continuation map does not depend on the system of choices </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})=(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})=(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, any continuation map is homotopic to the identity map of </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                        
                        
                        
                      However we do not know if the identity map on the Floer chain complex can be realized as a continuation map.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is another triple for which the complex </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is defined, and</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{23}(\Theta^{\prime}):CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{23}(\Theta^{\prime}):CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a continuation map. Then the composition </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{23}(\Theta^{\prime})\circ\Phi^{12}(\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{23}(\Theta^{\prime})\circ\Phi^{12}(\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is homotopic to a continuation map from </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S1.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B (Continuation Map). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> lead to the definition of a canonical object</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which we call the <span id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">integral Floer homology</span> of <math id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.2. </span>Pair-of-pants product</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next we consider the pair-of-pants product.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem C</span></span><span id="Thmthma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Pair-of-pants product)<span id="Thmthma3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{i},J_{i},\Xi_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{i},J_{i},\Xi_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math> be triples for which the Floer chain complexes are defined as stated in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Then there is a <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear chain map</span></p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1};\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2};\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3};\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of degree <math id="Thmthma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dim</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}X</annotation></semantics></math> whose definition depends on a system of choices. Moreover, the chain map satisfies the following properties.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The homotopy class of (</span><a href="#S1.E4" title="In Theorem C (Pair-of-pants product). ‣ 1.1.2. Pair-of-pants product ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) is independent of the choices.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This chain map is compatible with continuation maps. Hence it induces a canonical linear map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">called the pair-of-pants product.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map (</span><a href="#S1.E4" title="In Theorem C (Pair-of-pants product). ‣ 1.1.2. Pair-of-pants product ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) is associative up to homotopy. Hence </span><math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a graded ring.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a multiplicative identity</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf 1}\in HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf 1}\in HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of cohomological degree zero called the Floer fundamental class.</span><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                        
                        
                        
                      We do not know if it coincides with the image of the classical fundamental class under the PSS map.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.3. </span>Poincaré duality</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then discuss Poincaré-type pairings on Hamiltonian Floer homology.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem D</span></span><span id="Thmthma4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Poincaré duality)<span id="Thmthma4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be Floer chain complexes provided by Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Then up to a choice <math id="Thmthma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="Thmthma4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-bilinear pairing</span></p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PD}(\Theta):CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PD}(\Theta):CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">called the </span><span id="Thmthma4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">chain-level Poincaré pairing</span><span id="Thmthma4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which satisfies the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The homotopy type of the pairing is well-defined.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pairing is compatible with continuation maps. Namely, suppose </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is another Floer chain complex and </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{31}:CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})\to CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>31</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{31}:CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3})\to CF_{*}(H_{1},J_{1},\Xi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a chain-level continuation map. Then the pairing</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PD}(\Phi^{31}(\cdot),\cdot):CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>31</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PD}(\Phi^{31}(\cdot),\cdot):CF_{*}(H_{3},J_{3},\Xi_{3}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}CF_{*}(H_{2},J_{2},\Xi_{2})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is homotopic to a chain-level Poincaré pairing.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In field coefficients, the induced Poincaré pairing on homology is nondegenerate. More precisely, let </span><math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf K}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a field and </span><math id="S1.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>𝐊</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the Novikov field. Then the induced bilinear pairing</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PD}:\left(HF_{*}(X)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X)\right)\underset{\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}\to\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℤ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℤ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>𝐊</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>𝐊</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PD}:\left(HF_{*}(X)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X)\right)\underset{\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}\to\Lambda_{\bf K}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I4.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is nondegenerate.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Poincaré duality is compatible with the pair-of-pants product. More precisely, the following diagram commutes.</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="79.90" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-62.01355985475474px" version="1.1" viewBox="112.51 62.01 449.95 79.90" width="449.95"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 112.50725125019459 79.90001922856563) translate(0,3.54) translate(112.51,0)"><g transform="translate(-112.51,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:15.66em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="216.71"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{HF_{*}(X,\omega)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X,\omega)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X,\omega)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{HF_{*}(X,\omega)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X,\omega)\widehat{\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}HF_{*}(X,\omega)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 112.50725125019459 -0.27673578005007266 L 112.50725125019459 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(120.85,0) translate(0,8.94) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.64)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.59em;--ltx-fo-height:0.61em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.58" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.43)" width="49.74"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\Phi^{\rm pants}\otimes{\rm Id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">pants</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\Phi^{\rm pants}\otimes{\rm Id}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(187.23,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 112.5072