</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.4. </span>PSS map and isomorphism</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we consider the Piunikhin–Salamon–Schwarz (PSS) map (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>) from the Morse complex to the Floer complex and a map in the reversed direction called the SSP map.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E</span></span><span id="Thmthma5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(PSS and SSP maps)<span id="Thmthma5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Morse–Smale pair on <math id="Thmthma5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with the associated Morse–Smale–Witten complex <math id="Thmthma5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CM_{*}(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CM_{*}(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a Floer chain complex provided by Theorem </span><a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then there exists a </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-linear chain map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PSS}:CM_{*}(f,g)\underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes}\Lambda^{\Pi}\to CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">ℤ</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PSS}:CM_{*}(f,g)\underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes}\Lambda^{\Pi}\to CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I5.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of degree </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-n:=-\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>dim</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-n:=-\frac{1}{2}{\rm dim}X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
and a </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-linear chain map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm SSP}:CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CM_{*}(f,g)\underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes}\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">ℤ</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm SSP}:CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to CM_{*}(f,g)\underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes}\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I5.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of degree </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> whose definitions depend on various choices.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The chain homotopy classes of </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PSS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PSS}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm SSP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm SSP}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are independent of these choices.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The PSS and SSP maps are compatible with continuation maps, hence induce well-defined maps</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm PSS}:H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to HF_{*-n}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm PSS}:H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to HF_{*-n}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm SSP}:HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to H_{*+n}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>SSP</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm SSP}:HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to H_{*+n}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem F</span></span><span id="Thmthma6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The PSS map on homology is an isomorphism of graded <math id="Thmthma6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We point out that we do not know if the PSS and the SSP maps are inverses to each other up to homotopy. In particular, the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma6" title="Theorem F. ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> involves new moduli spaces beyond those appearing in the constructions of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma5" title="Theorem E (PSS and SSP maps). ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, the Arnold conjecture over integer coefficients now follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmthma6" title="Theorem F. ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> as a corollary, although in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> we proved it by only considering the Floer chain complex for special <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and proving only the injectivity part of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma6" title="Theorem F. ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Integral Arnold conjecture, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:X\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:X\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a nondegenerate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism. Let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal Chern number of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the number of fixed points of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is no less than the minimal number of generators of a <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>-graded complex of free abelian groups whose homology is isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(X;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(X;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> viewed as a <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}/2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}/2N</annotation></semantics></math>-graded abelian group.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If we compose the Floer-theoretic Poincaré pairing with the PSS map, then it induces a nondegenerate bilinear pairing on the homology <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FOP Poincaré pairing</span> on <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math> is the bilinear pairing</p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\Phi^{\rm PSS}(\cdot),\Phi^{\rm PSS}(\cdot)\rangle:H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>PSS</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Phi^{\rm PSS}(\cdot),\Phi^{\rm PSS}(\cdot)\rangle:H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi}){\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}}H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to\Lambda^{\Pi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If we use the PSS map defined using the technology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, then over rational coefficients, such a pairing coincides with the classical Poincaré pairing on homology. This is because the orbifold Euler classes satisfy properties needed for <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-localization, so there is no contribution from moduli spaces of curves with nonzero energy. However, the FOP Euler characteristic classes arising in our perturbation scheme do not necessarily satisfy these properties and we do not know if the pairing is classical or not. This phenomenon is similar to the case of quantum <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-theory (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>) or quantum generalized cohomology developed by Abouzaid–McLean–Smith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The point of introducing the quantum-corrected Poincaré pairing is to define an associative product analogous to the quantum product on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math> in our context, where we use the pairing to turn three-pointed Gromov–Witten type counts into a bilinear operation on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math> to dualize a marked point into an output marked point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Another corollary is the invertibility of the Seidel map with integer coefficients defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Recall that for each loop of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:S^{1}\to{\rm Ham}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:S^{1}\to{\rm Ham}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has a Hamiltonian fibration <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\phi}\to S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\phi}\to S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with fibres being <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. By counting holomorphic sections <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:S^{2}\to\widetilde{M}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:S^{2}\to\widetilde{M}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> (via FOP perturbation), one can define a map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi):H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi):H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})\to H(X;\Lambda^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which only depends on the homotopy class <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi]\in\pi_{1}({\rm Ham}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi]\in\pi_{1}({\rm Ham}(X))</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              One uses the classical Poincaré duality in the definition. Hence we do not expect that <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)S(\psi)=S(\phi\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)S(\psi)=S(\phi\psi)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the main use in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> of the Seidel map is its invertibility.</span></span></span> In the semipositive case, using classical perturbation method, one can prove that <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible by showing <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi\psi)=S(\phi)S(\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi\psi)=S(\phi)S(\psi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(1)={\rm Id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(1)={\rm Id}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the invertibility of the Seidel maps can only be shown using the Hamiltonian model for the quantum cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Seidel representation)<span id="S1.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Seidel map <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix an almost complex structure <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a nondegenerate Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the pullback by the loop <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. On <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2}={\mathbb{R}}\cup S^{1}\cup\{\pm\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2}={\mathbb{R}}\cup S^{1}\cup\{\pm\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> one can turn on a family of Hamiltonian connections parametrized by <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}dt</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-S,S]\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-S,S]\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and which vanishes near <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. We regard this as a family of Hamiltonian connections <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}_{\phi}\to S^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}_{\phi}\to S^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> written with respect to the negative trivialization <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then with respect to the positive trivialization, the connection reads <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}dt</annotation></semantics></math>. Then consider the family of moduli spaces of holomorphic sections with respect to this family of Hamiltonian connections. When <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=0</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a bimodule defining the Seidel map <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, given as a chain map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}(\phi):CM(f)\to CM(f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}(\phi):CM(f)\to CM(f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">The induced map on homology is the Seidel map <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a triple concatenation of a bimodule for PSS map, a bimodule for continuation map, and a bimodule for SSP map. Therefore, <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is chain homotopic to the composition of these three maps. By Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B (Continuation Map). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmthma5" title="Theorem E (PSS and SSP maps). ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, on cohomology level <math id="S1.SS1.SSS4.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.1.5. </span>Comparison with known construction</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly monotone (also called semi-positive), then by perturbing either the almost complex structure <math id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> or the Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, Hofer–Salamon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and Ono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> defined the Floer chain complex over integers. Such constructions can also be carried out to define continuation maps, pair-of-pants products, and Künneth isomorphisms with integer coefficients for weakly monotone <math id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is natural to wonder if our constructions agree with the classical definitions in the weakly monotone setting. As a proof of principle, we demonstrate that this is the case for the Floer homology.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem G</span></span><span id="Thmthma7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="Thmthma7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly monotone, our Floer chain complex <math id="Thmthma7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi)</annotation></semantics></math> is chain homotopy equivalent to the one defined using the classical method.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We expect that the proof methods can be generalized to establish the identification between our Floer-theoretic invariants and the classical versions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, for a general compact <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, Floer chain complex <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)</annotation></semantics></math> over rational coefficients is constructed using the classical virtual technique (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>). Using the setup similar to here, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)</annotation></semantics></math> is a module over the rational Novikov field <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although there is an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}\cong HF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(X,\omega)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{*}(X,\omega;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}\cong HF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">via the PSS maps in the two setups and the universal coefficient theorem for ordinary homology, one does not have an obvious relation on the chain level, between</p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}\ \ \ {\rm and}\ \ \ CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace><mi>and</mi><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{*}(H,J,\Xi;\Lambda^{\Pi})\underset{\Lambda^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\Pi}\ \ \ {\rm and}\ \ \ CF_{*}^{\mathbb{Q}}(H,J)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">without going through Morse theory.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To discuss Steenrod operations and other aspects of equivariant Floer theory, we switch to the cohomological convention, partially to match with the fact that classical Steenrod operations are cohomological operations. In this way, by considering ascending flows rather than descending flows, one obtains the Floer cochain complex <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(H,J,\Xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(H,J,\Xi)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a Floer flow category whose cochains are formal infinite linear combinations of capped 1-periodic orbits of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> whose actions go to <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime number. Abbreviate <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}:={\mathbb{Z}}/{\sf p}{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}:={\mathbb{Z}}/{\sf p}{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. When we view it as a field serving as the coefficient field of cohomology, we use <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> instead. In characteristic <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>, on any topological space, there are the Steenrod power operations which can be organized as a single nonlinear map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will primarily use the “Tate” version of equivariant theory. Recall that the more familiar “Borel” version of the <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant cohomology of a point is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\rm Borel}_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}({\rm pt};{\bf F}_{\sf p})={\bf F}_{\sf p}[\mathtt{U}]\langle\uptheta\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>Borel</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\rm Borel}_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}({\rm pt};{\bf F}_{\sf p})={\bf F}_{\sf p}[\mathtt{U}]\langle\uptheta\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">over <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by a degree <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> variable <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and a degree <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> variable <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptheta</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.EGx2.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.EGx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \mathtt{U}\uptheta=\uptheta\mathtt{U},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚄</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \mathtt{U}\uptheta=\uptheta\mathtt{U},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.EGx2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \uptheta^{2}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathtt{U},&{\sf p}=2,\\
0,&{\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝚄</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \uptheta^{2}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathtt{U},&amp;{\sf p}=2,\\
0,&amp;{\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">It is defined as the ordinary cohomology of the classifying space <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and admits a nice Morse model. We often need to take the completion by allowing formal power series.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Tate version of the equivariant cohomology of a point differs from the Borel one by inverting the variable <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptheta</annotation></semantics></math> (in characteristic <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>) resp. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math> (in odd characteristics), i.e. (after completion)</p>
<table id="S1.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\rm Tate}({\rm pt};{\bf F}_{\sf p})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf F}_{2}{(\!(\uptheta)\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf F}_{\sf p}{(\!(\mathtt{U})\!)}\langle\uptheta\rangle,&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>Tate</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>pt</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\rm Tate}({\rm pt};{\bf F}_{\sf p})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf F}_{2}{(\!(\uptheta)\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf F}_{\sf p}{(\!(\mathtt{U})\!)}\langle\uptheta\rangle,&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are two main reasons why we prefer the Tate version. First, all the nice properties of the equivariant Floer theory hold for the Tate version but not the Borel one, for example, localization. Second, the inversion of the variable <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math> follows more naturally from our geometric construction. The Borel version, instead, is regarded as a truncation of the Tate version. From now on, all equivariant cochain complexes or cohomology, without further declaration, will be the Tate version.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the following notions. Let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf K}_{\sf p}:=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf F}_{2}{(\!(\uptheta)\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf F}_{\sf p}{(\!(\mathtt{U})\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf K}_{\sf p}:=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf F}_{2}{(\!(\uptheta)\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf F}_{\sf p}{(\!(\mathtt{U})\!)},&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The chain-level construction we use will always produce algebraic structures linear over <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf K}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf K}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, denote</p>
<table id="S1.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}:=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf K}_{\sf p},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf K}_{\sf p}\langle\uptheta\rangle,&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2\end{array}\right.." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}:=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}{\bf K}_{\sf p},&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
{\bf K}_{\sf p}\langle\uptheta\rangle,&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2\end{array}\right..</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The resulting cohomology groups (not complexes) will be modules over <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, such module structure is not an algebraic consequence, but needs to be constructed geometrically.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.1. </span>The quantum Steenrod operations</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The classical Steenrod square resp. power operations are cohomological operations which can be formulated as a single map (for <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}=2</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S1.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="St_{2}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{2})\to H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{2}[\uptheta])\subseteq H^{*}(X;\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">St_{2}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{2})\to H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{2}[\uptheta])\subseteq H^{*}(X;\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">resp. (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S1.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="St_{\sf p}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p}[\mathtt{U}]\langle\uptheta\rangle)\subseteq H^{*}(X;\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">St_{\sf p}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p}[\mathtt{U}]\langle\uptheta\rangle)\subseteq H^{*}(X;\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="St_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">St_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> is additive and linear over <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if we regard <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptheta</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="St_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">St_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> multiplies the grading by <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fukaya proposed the idea of defining quantum Steenrod operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. It has been adapted in the Floer setting by Seidel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and Shelukhin–Zhao <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> and in the quantum cohomology setting by Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> and Seidel–Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. A key point in the construction is the integrality of curve counts, which then admit well-defined mod <math id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> reduction. Hence the existing constructions are all restricted to monotone or semipositive settings. The FOP perturbation method developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> allows one to extend to the general setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem H</span></span><span id="Thmthma8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantum Steenrod operation on general symplectic manifold)<span id="Thmthma8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any compact symplectic manifold <math id="Thmthma8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, for each prime <math id="Thmthma8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are well-defined additive maps</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}QSt_{2}:&amp;\ H^{k}(X;{\bf F}_{2})\to H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{2}{[\![\uptheta]\!]}}^{\Pi})\subseteq H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{\tilde{\bf K}_{2}}^{\Pi}),\ ({\sf p}=2)\\
QSt_{\sf p}:&amp;\ H^{k}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}{[\![\mathtt{U}]\!]}}^{\Pi}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\subseteq H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\ ({\sf p}&gt;2)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.158em;" width="0.158em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mpadded style="width:0.158em;" width="0.158em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="1.000em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}QSt_{2}:&amp;\ H^{k}(X;{\bf F}_{2})\to H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{2}{[\![\uptheta]\!]}}^{\Pi})\subseteq H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{\tilde{\bf K}_{2}}^{\Pi}),\ ({\sf p}=2)\\
QSt_{\sf p}:&amp;\ H^{k}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}{[\![\mathtt{U}]\!]}}^{\Pi}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\subseteq H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\ ({\sf p}&gt;2)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The classical part (i.e., setting the Novikov parameter to be zero) of </span><math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> coincides with the classical Steenrod operation.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When </span><math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is semipositive, </span><math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> coincides with the quantum Steenrod operation defined by Seidel–Wilkins </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (which, in </span><math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and monotone case, agrees with Wilkins’ definition of quantum Steenrod square in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.2. </span>Equivariant Floer cohomology</h5>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Floer-theoretic version of the Steenrod operation has played important roles in many recent dynamical applications of Floer theory, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> etc. In this paper, we also generalize the construction of the Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation to general compact symplectic manifolds. Let <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Floer chain complex associated to a nondegenerate Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, a compatible almost complex structure <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, and a system of choices <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>; to simplify notation, we suppress <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> from the notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem I</span></span><span id="Thmthma9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equivariant Floer cohomology)<span id="Thmthma9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic manifold and <math id="Thmthma9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a 1-periodic Hamiltonian such that its </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-th iteration </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nondegenerate. Then there exists a cochain complex (the Tate </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant Floer complex)</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I7.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of finitely generated </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-modules whose definition depends on a choice </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Hamiltonians </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with both </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> nondegenerate and choices </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to define their equivariant Floer cochain complex, there is a chain homotopy equivalence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I7.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">called the equivariant continuation map.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The equivariant continuation maps are well-defined up to chain homotopy. Moreover, the composition of two equivariant continuation maps is homotopic to an equivariant continuation map. Hence there is a well-defined symplectic invariant of </span><math id="S1.I7.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, denoted by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I7.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">called the (Tate) </span><math id="S1.I7.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-equivariant Hamiltonian Floer cohomology</span><span id="S1.I7.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a natural equivariant PSS map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{MF}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{MF}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I7.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and an equivariant SSP map</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{FM}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{FM}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I7.i4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which are isomorphisms of </span><math id="S1.I7.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i4.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-modules.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The equivariant cohomology <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally a module over the field <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, this is the same as the ring <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure on the complex <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> as “multiplying by <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptheta</annotation></semantics></math>” is not a chain map. However, one could define a <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure on the cohomology <math id="S1.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we do not consider this additional structure in the current paper.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is an algebraically defined <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant chain complex</p>
<table id="S1.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">whose underlying cochain group is a suitable completion of</p>
<table id="S1.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Big(CF(H)\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\cdots\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}CF(H)\Big)\otimes\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup></munder><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(CF(H)\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}\cdots\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}}{\otimes}CF(H)\Big)\otimes\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The differential is linear over <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quasi-Frobenius map is an <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="qF:HF(H;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\to H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qF:HF(H;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})\to H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which is, roughly speaking, induced by the chain-level map <math id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\mapsto x\otimes\cdots\otimes x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto x\otimes\cdots\otimes x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.3. </span>Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation</h5>

<div id="Thmthma10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem J</span></span><span id="Thmthma10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation)<span id="Thmthma10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthma10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic manifold and <math id="Thmthma10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmthma10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structure.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any pair of Hamiltonians </span><math id="S1.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I8.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S1.I8.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> nondegenerate and </span><math id="S1.I8.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> nondegenerate, there exists a cochain map called the </span><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equivariant pair-of-pants product</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{P}:C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H_{-})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to CF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{P}:C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H_{-})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to CF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}(H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I8.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I8.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is well-defined up to chain homotopy.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map on cohomology, denoted by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H_{-})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H_{-})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{+}^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I8.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I8.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an isomorphism of </span><math id="S1.I8.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I8.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-modules.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I8.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The composition defining the </span><span id="S1.I8.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation</span><span id="S1.I8.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

