<li id="S8.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(FOP perturbation for equivariant multimodule homotopies)</span><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1},\ldots,\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1},\ldots,\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be as in the above situation. Let </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be two </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant multimodules over </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant homotopy from </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Let </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be FOP perturbations on </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively as </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant multimodules over </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then there exists a </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant FOP perturbation </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as a homotopy from </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S8.I2.i3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.27" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We construct the FOP perturbations inductively. In general, the Novikov conditions of Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Definition 7.2. ‣ 7.1.2. Convergence ‣ 7.1. Unobstructedness, local finiteness, grading, and orientations ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>, Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm5" title="Definition 7.5. ‣ 7.1.2. Convergence ‣ 7.1. Unobstructedness, local finiteness, grading, and orientations ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>, and Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm24" title="Definition 7.24. ‣ 7.3.3. Symmetries on flow categories ‣ 7.3. Symmetries on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a> allow us to choose a beginning of the induction process. The freeness assumed in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a> allows us to achieve equivariant transversality.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">—<span id="S8.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The case of flow categories</span>— We say a pair of objects <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal if <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and there does not exist a third object <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> such that both <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pr}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pr}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{rq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{rq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are nonempty. By (2) of Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm24" title="Definition 7.24. ‣ 7.3.3. Symmetries on flow categories ‣ 7.3. Symmetries on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a>, unless all morphism spaces are empty, minimal pairs exist. Moreover, the <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariance condition implies that minimal pairs form a union of <math id="S8.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we perturb <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}=({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}=({\mathcal{U}}_{pq},{\mathcal{E}}_{pq},{\mathcal{S}}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> for minimal pairs <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly contained in the union of top strata of <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. If it is not the case, then there exists a codimension one stratum which intersects <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the unobstructedness assumption, such a stratum comes from the product of two other morphism spaces. This contradicts the assumption that <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimal pair. Hence our claim is correct.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then by Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8.1. FOP perturbations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>, there exists an FOP transverse perturbation <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using the free <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, we translate this perturbation to charts <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with all <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p^{\prime},q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the same <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply such perturbation constructions independently for all <math id="S8.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of minimal pairs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we set up the induction. Recall that the Novikov flow category <math id="S8.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> carries an <math id="S8.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued action <math id="S8.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}=({\mathcal{A}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}=({\mathcal{A}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|={\mathcal{A}}_{1}+\cdots+{\mathcal{A}}_{m}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{R}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|={\mathcal{A}}_{1}+\cdots+{\mathcal{A}}_{m}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Choose an increasing sequence <math id="S8.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}&lt;c_{2}&lt;\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}&lt;c_{2}&lt;\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each pair <math id="S8.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l-1}\leq|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l-1}\leq|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S8.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;r&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;r&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S8.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(r)&lt;c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(r)&lt;c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(r)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(r)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S8.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S8.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)\geq c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)\geq c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we state the following induction hypothesis: suppose for <math id="S8.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in this sequence, we have constructed FOP transverse perturbations</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all pairs <math id="S8.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)&lt;c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S8.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfy the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbations are <math id="S8.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whenever <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;r&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;r&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{prq}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{prq}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the stabilization of the product of <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the compositions of <math id="S8.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S8.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, then <math id="S8.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one we have already constructed.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our choice of <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the base case is established. Suppose the induction hypothesis holds for <math id="S8.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a pair <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(p)-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q)\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>. If the pair <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, then we take <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the one we constructed before the induction setup. Suppose it is not minimal. By the induction hypothesis, for each <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;r&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;r&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, one has constructed FOP transverse perturbations</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pr}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pr},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{pr}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pr},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{rq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{rq}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{rq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{rq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">As the structural map</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pr}^{\mathbb{F}}\times M_{rq}^{\mathbb{F}}\to\partial^{prq}M_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pr}^{\mathbb{F}}\times M_{rq}^{\mathbb{F}}\to\partial^{prq}M_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a rigidified embedding of normally complex derived orbifolds, there is a canonical extension of the product <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}\times{\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pr}^{\prime}\times{\mathcal{S}}_{rq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{prq}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{prq}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. The induction hypothesis also implies that for all such <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, these canonical extensions agree on strata of the form <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{pr_{1}r_{2}q}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{pr_{1}r_{2}q}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence one obtains an object in <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}</annotation></semantics></math> stratified by the true boundary of <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pq}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 8.4. ‣ 8.1. FOP perturbations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>, there exists an FOP transverse perturbation <math id="S8.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{\alpha}:\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{\alpha}:\partial^{\alpha}{\mathcal{U}}_{pq}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with the existing ones.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">As the <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is free, one can translate <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to all pairs on the same <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. Apply the same procedure for all <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of pairs <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime},q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime},q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{A}}|(p^{\prime})-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q^{\prime})\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{A}}|(p^{\prime})-|{\mathcal{A}}|(q^{\prime})\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that the induction can be carried out. Hence the case of flow categories of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a> is established.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">—<span id="S8.SS2.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The case of multimodules—</span> We follow a similar inductive process. Consider the set <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m}\times{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m}\times{\rm Ob}\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose objects are denoted by <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on this set via the morphism <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; in the situation of item (3), the group <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> also acts on this set and the <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-action commutes with <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. We say a tuple <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal if 1) there does not exist <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}&lt;q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}&lt;q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and 2) there does not exist <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, similar to the flow category case, the unobstructedness assumption implies that one can construct an FOP transverse perturbation</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">which can be translated to orbits of <math id="S8.SS2.p11.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}\times\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}\times\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we set up an induction scheme. For each tuple <math id="S8.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|=|{\mathcal{A}}_{1}|(p_{1})+\cdots+|{\mathcal{A}}_{m}|(p_{m})-|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(p^{\prime})\in{\mathbb{R}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|=|{\mathcal{A}}_{1}|(p_{1})+\cdots+|{\mathcal{A}}_{m}|(p_{m})-|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(p^{\prime})\in{\mathbb{R}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.5</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a sequences of real numbers</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}&lt;c_{2}&lt;\cdots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}&lt;c_{2}&lt;\cdots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfying the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> goes to infinity.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S8.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S8.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}&lt;q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}&lt;q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S8.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S8.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S8.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S8.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S8.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S8.I5.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S8.I5.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S8.I5.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S8.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S8.I5.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S8.I5.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\geq c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\geq c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I5.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from the definition of equivariant Novikov flow categories (Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm24" title="Definition 7.24. ‣ 7.3.3. Symmetries on flow categories ‣ 7.3. Symmetries on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a>) that for each <math id="S8.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf\Big\{|{\mathcal{A}}_{i}|(p_{i})-|{\mathcal{A}}_{i}|(q_{i})\ |\ M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\neq\emptyset\Big\}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf\Big\{|{\mathcal{A}}_{i}|(p_{i})-|{\mathcal{A}}_{i}|(q_{i})\ |\ M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\neq\emptyset\Big\}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf\Big\{|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(p^{\prime})-|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(q^{\prime})\ |\ M_{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}\neq\emptyset\Big\}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf\Big\{|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(p^{\prime})-|{\mathcal{A}}^{\prime}|(q^{\prime})\ |\ M_{p^{\prime}q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}\neq\emptyset\Big\}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum of these lower bounds. Then we can take <math id="S8.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}=l\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}=l\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we state the induction hypothesis: for a <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in this sequence, we have constructed FOP transverse perturbations</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all tuples <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S8.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|&lt;c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfy the following conditions.</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbations are <math id="S8.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whenever <math id="S8.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}&lt;q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}&lt;q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism</p>
<table id="S8.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\iota_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}(p_{i}q_{i})p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}:M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}(p_{i}q_{i})p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}:M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.I6.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is also a morphism in <math id="S8.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whenever <math id="S8.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}&lt;p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism</p>
<table id="S8.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\iota_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}:M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\times M_{q^{\prime}p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}:M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};q^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\times M_{q^{\prime}p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.I6.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is also a morphism in <math id="S8.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S8.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S8.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, <math id="S8.I6.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one we have already constructed before the induction.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our choice of <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the base case is established. Suppose the induction hypothesis holds for <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a tuple <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m};p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}|\in[c_{l-1},c_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>. If it is minimal, then the perturbation has been constructed. If not, then the induction hypothesis and the conditions on the sequence <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that an FOP transverse perturbation has been constructed compatibly on each lower stratum of <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can extend it using the collar structure to a neighborhood of the boundary. Then by (3) of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8.1. FOP perturbations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>, one can extend to an FOP transverse perturbation <math id="S8.SS2.p15.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}^{\prime}:{\mathcal{U}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{E}}_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using the free <math id="S8.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{1}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Pi}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, one can translate this perturbation to all tuples on the same orbit. Apply the same procedure to all orbits of tuples within this energy window. Therefore the induction can be carried out. Hence the multimodule case of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a> is established.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The homotopy case of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a> is almost identical to the case of multimodules, hence is omitted.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.3. </span>FOP perturbation and multimodule concatenations</h4>

<div id="S8.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that although multimodule concatenation can be canonically done for multimodules enriched in <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}</annotation></semantics></math>, in practice we will have non-canonical concatenations (involving extra stabilizations).</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.6</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{n},\mathring{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{n},\mathring{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be Novikov flow categories enriched in <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Novikov multimodule over <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{m};\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Novikov multimodule over <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{n};\mathring{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\mathring{\mathbb{G}}_{n};\mathring{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be their descents to <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a concatenation of <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{G}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{G}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then this concatenation has a lift to <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> as a concatenation <math id="S8.Thmthm6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the concatenation respects the functor</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}\to{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}\to{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Definition <a href="#S6.Thmthm13" title="Definition 6.13. ‣ 6.5.2. General concatenations ‣ 6.5. Multimodule concatenations ‣ 6. Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.13</span></a>, the concatenation <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains codimension zero morphisms</span></p>
<table id="S8.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};r_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\times M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}\to M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{i-1}p_{1}\cdots p_{m}r_{i+1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};r_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}\times M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}\to M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{i-1}p_{1}\cdots p_{m}r_{i+1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the three items above are objects of <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> and the morphism is a collared rigidified embedding of NC derived orbifolds. Then by the <span id="S8.SS3.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Product Property)</span> of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8.1. FOP perturbations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>, the product of existing FOP perturbations on <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};r_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};r_{i}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and on <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r_{1}\cdots r_{n};r^{\prime}}^{\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an FOP perturbation on the source of the morphism (<a href="#S8.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 8.3. FOP perturbation and multimodule concatenations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>). Then after certain stabilization by a rigid orbifold vector bundle, by the <span id="S8.SS3.p2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Stabilization Property)</span> of Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 8.2. ‣ 8.1. FOP perturbations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>, the product has a canonical germ of FOP transverse extensions to the (corresponding open stratum) of the target of (<a href="#S8.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 8.3. FOP perturbation and multimodule concatenations ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a>). The condition that the FOP perturbations are collared and respect the rigidifications guarantees that the extensions agree on adjacent strata. Therefore, one obtains a concatenation <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\mathring{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> lifting <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\tilde{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to verify that the concatenation is compatible with the concatenation of multimodules enriched in <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the forgetful functor <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}\to{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>FOP</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm FOP}\to{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Pt2" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_part">Part II </span>GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES</h2>

<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Outline of Part 2</h3>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we describe flow categories, bimodules, and homotopies that will appear in (most of) the settings of Hamiltonian Floer theory. We then state the main theorems about the construction of their lifts to the category of normally complex Kuranishi spaces, which induce lifts to normally complex derived orbifolds. Using the the counting scheme described in Section <a href="#S8" title="8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we prove the main theorems except for those in the <math id="Sx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant setting. The technical constructions of the regularization of the moduli spaces are deferred to the next Part.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Geometric Flow Categories</h3>

<section id="S9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.1. </span>Morse flow category</h4>

<div id="S9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a closed oriented Riemannian manifold <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,g)</annotation></semantics></math> and a Morse function <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Morse flow category, denoted by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}(f,g)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and usually abbreviated as <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, has critical points of <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as its objects and the moduli spaces of unparametrized (broken) downward gradient trajectories as its morphism spaces. We always assume that <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> is Morse–Smale and that near critical points <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a quadratic function and <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean metric with respect to a certain coordinate system near each critical point. In this way, for any two critical points <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\rm crit}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\rm crit}f</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold with corners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The partial order of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}\mathbb{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}\mathbb{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S9.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}\neq\emptyset" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the poset stratifying <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S9.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}=\{p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{k}&lt;q\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}=\{p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{k}&lt;q\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an object of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also define composition maps as the natural inclusion</p>
<table id="S9.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pr}^{\mathbb{M}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{rq}^{\mathbb{M}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pr}^{\mathbb{M}}\times\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{rq}^{\mathbb{M}}\to\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is a morphism of <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we obtained a flow category enriched in <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, the standard exponential decay estimate implies that the moduli spaces are indeed objects of <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}_{\rm adm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>adm</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SMan}}_{\rm adm}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, its outercollaring still lives in the same category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The flow category <math id="S9.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a grading which assigns to each critical point <math id="S9.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> the its Morse index. By assigning orientations on unstable manifolds and counting elements of zero-dimensional moduli spaces, one obtains a Morse–Smale–Witten complex <math id="S9.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CM_{*}(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CM_{*}(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> over integers freely generated by critical points, whose homology is isomorphic to the singular homology of <math id="S9.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2. </span>Floer flow category</h4>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe a central object considered in this paper. We will assign to each compact symplectic manifold together with a nondegenerate Hamiltonian and an almost complex structure a flow category, called the <span id="S9.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Floer flow category</span>, typically denoted by <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, let <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact symplectic manifold. Recall</p>
<table id="S9.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}c_{1}\cap{\rm ker}\omega}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}c_{1}\cap{\rm ker}\omega}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is an abelian group (monoid). We make it a Novikov monoid (Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm21" title="Definition 7.21 (Novikov groups). ‣ 7.3.1. Twisted Novikov groups ‣ 7.3. Symmetries on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.21</span></a>) by defining</p>
<table id="S9.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:\Pi\to{\mathbb{R}},\ \lambda(a)=\omega(a)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:\Pi\to{\mathbb{R}},\ \lambda(a)=\omega(a),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S9.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}},\ \mu(a)=2c_{1}(a)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:\Pi\to{\mathbb{Z}},\ \mu(a)=2c_{1}(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:M\times S^{1}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:M\times S^{1}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be a (time-dependent) <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic family of Hamiltonians which induces a 1-parameter family of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms</p>
<table id="S9.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}^{t}\in{\rm Ham}(X,\omega)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}^{t}\in{\rm Ham}(X,\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We assume that <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate. Let <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{P}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{P}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of pairs <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,x)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{D}}^{2}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{D}}^{2}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth map and <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=u|_{\partial\mathbb{D}^{2}}:S^{1}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=u|_{\partial\mathbb{D}^{2}}:S^{1}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a 1-periodic orbit of <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S9.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,x)\sim(u^{\prime},x^{\prime})\Longleftrightarrow x=x^{\prime},\ \int_{{\mathbb{D}}^{2}}u^{*}\omega=\int_{{\mathbb{D}}^{2}}(u^{\prime})^{*}\omega,\ \langle c_{1}(TX),u({\mathbb{D}}^{2})\cup(-u^{\prime}({\mathbb{D}}^{2}))\rangle=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,x)\sim(u^{\prime},x^{\prime})\Longleftrightarrow x=x^{\prime},\ \int_{{\mathbb{D}}^{2}}u^{*}\omega=\int_{{\mathbb{D}}^{2}}(u^{\prime})^{*}\omega,\ \langle c_{1}(TX),u({\mathbb{D}}^{2})\cup(-u^{\prime}({\mathbb{D}}^{2}))\rangle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S9.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(H):=\tilde{\mathcal{P}}(H)/\sim" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(H):=\tilde{\mathcal{P}}(H)/\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">whose elements are called <span id="S9.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">capped 1-periodic orbits</span>. Notice that there are well-defined action</p>
<table id="S9.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{H}:{\mathcal{P}}(H)\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{H}:{\mathcal{P}}(H)\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and well-defined Conley–Zehnder index</p>
<table id="S9.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm deg}:{\mathcal{P}}(H)\to{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm deg}:{\mathcal{P}}(H)\to{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Moreover, <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> freely with a finite quotient set, satisfying</p>
<table id="S9.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{H}(ap)={\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p)-\lambda(a),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{H}(ap)={\mathcal{A}}_{H}(p)-\lambda(a),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm deg}(ap)={\rm deg}(p)-\mu(a)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm deg}(ap)={\rm deg}(p)-\mu(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Floer flow category <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> has object set</p>
<table id="S9.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}={\mathcal{P}}(H)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}={\mathcal{P}}(H).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Choose an <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structure <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pair <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\mathcal{P}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has the moduli space <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> of stable Floer trajectories. A partial order on <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by:</p>
<table id="S9.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}\neq\emptyset." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq q\Longleftrightarrow\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}\neq\emptyset.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The moduli space <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified by the regular poset <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is (when <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\neq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\neq q</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S9.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}:=\{p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{k}&lt;q\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}:=\{p&lt;r_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;r_{k}&lt;q\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">This provides a flow category which is denoted by <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}(X,\omega,H,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}(X,\omega,H,J)</annotation></semantics></math>, often abbreviated by <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. At this moment this flow category is only enriched in <math id="S9.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the above setup in the following situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsituationf1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_situationf">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsituationf1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation Flow(i)</span></span><span id="Thmsituationf1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsituationf1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsituationf1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmsituationf1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmsituationf1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a compact symplectic manifold. <math id="Thmsituationf1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Floer flow category associated to a nondegenerate <math id="Thmsituationf1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic Hamiltonian and a 1-periodic family of <math id="Thmsituationf1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structure <math id="Thmsituationf1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the usual properties of the Floer flow category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationf1" title="Situation Flow(i). ‣ 9.2. Floer flow category ‣ 9. Geometric Flow Categories ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Flow(i)</span></a>, the Floer flow category <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the symplectic action <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Conley–Zehnder index, is a <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded <math id="S9.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov flow category.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.3. </span>The pearly flow category</h4>

<div id="S9.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compare Floer homology and Morse homology, we introduce a version of the pearly flow category. <span id="S9.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A priori</span>, this flow category induces a chain complex which is not quasi-isomorphic to the Morse chain complex, as least not in the most naive way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact almost Kähler manifold. For each <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space</p>
<table id="S9.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is a variant of the moduli space <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic maps with two marked points in degree <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. It is defined to be the moduli space of unparametrized Floer trajectories for the Hamiltonian <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>. By singularity removal, <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> can be alternatively described as the moduli space of <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic spheres of class <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> marked points such that the <math id="S9.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> marked points are equipped with asymptotic markers with the same angular coordinate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural compactification</p>
<table id="S9.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is naturally stratified by (ordered) decompositions <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a_{1}+\cdots+a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a_{1}+\cdots+a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(a_{i})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(a_{i})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The strata of the above moduli space <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> can be labelled by the so-called <span id="S9.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear trees</span>. These are oriented trees whose vertices have valence at most 2, hence have a unique incoming vertex (the input) and a unique outgoing vertex (the output). The degrees <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\ldots,a_{k}\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\ldots,a_{k}\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> are viewed as extra decorations on the vertices. We then can allow the edges of the tree to acquire length, allowing the lengths to vary, and allowing the edges to break when the lengths approach infinity. Choose a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We then add additional variables which are negative gradient segments of <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the edges of varying length. For the input sphere resp. output sphere we also add a semi-infinite edge on which is supported a negative gradient ray. The natural matching conditions at nodes are required. By choosing an incoming resp. outgoing critical point <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, one can consider the pearly moduli space and its compactification</p>
<table id="S9.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{P}}(a)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{P}}(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The underlying poset is not as simple as the Morse or Floer case. For <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(\underline{\smash{x}},b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(\underline{\smash{x}},b)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm crit}f\times\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm crit}f\times\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{xy}^{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{xy}^{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of (possibly broken) linear trees whose breakings are labelled by critical points of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, whose vertices are labelled by elements of <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> (with sum being <math id="S9.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-b</annotation></semantics></math>), and whose finite edges have either zero or positive lengths. This is a regular poset, whose maximal elements are those unbroken trees whose edges all have positive length. The depth function is given by the number of breakings plus the number of edges with length zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pearly flow category</span> <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,J,f,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,J,f,g</annotation></semantics></math> as above, is the flow category enriched in <math id="S9.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined as follows.</p>
<ol id="S9.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set of objects is <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}\mathbb{P}={\rm crit}f\times\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}\mathbb{P}={\rm crit}f\times\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any pair of objects <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(\underline{\smash{y}},b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(\underline{\smash{y}},b)</annotation></semantics></math>, the morphism space is the moduli space</p>
<table id="S9.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{P}}(a-b)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{P}}(a-b).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The composition map is given by the concatenation of pearly solutions.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pearly flow category <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> should be distinguished from the pearly bimodule introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Section 4.4]</cite>, which is a flow bimodule between Morse flow categories. <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> here is used to set up the Morse–Bott homology of the constant Hamiltonian <math id="S9.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.4. </span>Coupling of geometric flow categories</h4>

<div id="S9.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given symplectic manifolds <math id="S9.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{j},\omega^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{j},\omega^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> and Floer flow categories <math id="S9.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to pairs <math id="S9.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{j},J^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{j},J^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, their coupling is defined to be the Floer flow category of the product manifold with the product Hamiltonians and almost complex structure. However, it is difficult to describe this construction on the abstract level for a collection of flow categories. The more abstract packaging would require building a symmetric monoidal structure on the (infinity) category of flow categories, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and its follow-up work. Here we describe certain “coupling” that arises geometrically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Running Assumption.</span> Let <math id="S9.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of flow categories. Each of them is either a Floer, Morse, or pearly flow category on a compact (symplectic) manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define their <span id="S9.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">coupling</span>, denoted as</p>
<table id="S9.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}:={\mathbb{F}}^{1}\ast\cdots\ast{\mathbb{F}}^{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}:={\mathbb{F}}^{1}\ast\cdots\ast{\mathbb{F}}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">as follows. Its set of objects is</p>
<table id="S9.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{1}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{1}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">whose elements are written as <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf p}=(p^{1},\ldots,p^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf p}=(p^{1},\ldots,p^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. The morphism space <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\bf p}{\bf q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>𝐩𝐪</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\bf p}{\bf q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of solutions <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf u}=(u^{1},\ldots,u^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf u}=(u^{1},\ldots,u^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (stable) Floer/Morse/pearly trajectory connecting <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the geometric origin of <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We take the quotient by the “synchronized” <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, i.e., for any <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify tuples of parametrized trajectories</p>
<table id="S9.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(u^{1}(\cdot),\dots,u^{k}(\cdot))\sim(u^{1}(\cdot+s),\dots,u^{k}(\cdot+s))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{1}(\cdot),\dots,u^{k}(\cdot))\sim(u^{1}(\cdot+s),\dots,u^{k}(\cdot+s)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">and compactify by allowing sphere bubbling and breaking of trajectories. Notice that this formulation means that breakings in each factor happen simultaneously. For example, when all <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are Floer flow categories, this coupling is the same as the Floer flow category of the product manifold with respect to the product Hamiltonian and product almost complex structure. Notice that if <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivariant with respect to an abelian group <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivariant with respect to the natural action by the product <math id="S9.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi=\Pi^{1}\times\cdots\times\Pi^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi=\Pi^{1}\times\cdots\times\Pi^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the discussion of equivariant Floer theory, one often needs to take self-coupling. For any <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, denote</p>
<table id="S9.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{\ast k}:=\underbrace{{\mathbb{F}}\ast\cdots\ast{\mathbb{F}}}_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>k</mi></munder></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{\ast k}:=\underbrace{{\mathbb{F}}\ast\cdots\ast{\mathbb{F}}}_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which is equivariant with respect to the action by the product <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi\times\cdots\times\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\times\cdots\times\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on this flow category by cyclically permuting the factors. This <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-action commutes with the action by the diagonal <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence this coupled flow category is equivariant with respect to an action by <math id="S9.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>Geometric Multimodules</h3>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we describe bimodules and multimodules that lead to continuation maps, PSS and SSP maps, pair-of-pants products, and Künneth maps.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.1. </span>Floer data and Floer domains</h4>

<div id="S10.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10.1</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">punctured surface</span> is a compact oriented surface (not necessarily connected) with finitely many points removed, usually denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We also include as part of data a decomposition of the punctures into <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">negative ends</span> and <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive ends</span>. The negative ends are usually denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{-}^{1},\ldots,z_{-}^{k_{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{-}^{1},\ldots,z_{-}^{k_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the positive ends are usually denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{+}^{1},\ldots,z_{+}^{k_{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{+}^{1},\ldots,z_{+}^{k_{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cylindrical end</span> on a punctured surface <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> around a negative resp. positive puncture <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism between <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>±</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and a punctured neighborhood of <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>; two cylindrical ends are regarded as equivalent if they differ by a translation. An equivalence class is called a <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">germ of cylindrical ends</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S10.Thmthm1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cylindrical surface</span> is a punctured surface equipped with germs of cylindrical ends around all punctures. To save notations such an object is still denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10.2</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic manifold. A <span id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Floer datum</span> over a cylindrical surface <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=(j,J,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=(j,J,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of a complex structure <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, a smooth family <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structures on <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and a smooth 1-form (called the Hamiltonian connection)</p>
<table id="S10.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\Gamma(X\times\Sigma,T^{*}\Sigma)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\Gamma(X\times\Sigma,T^{*}\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that</p>
<ol id="S10.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any cylindrical end <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\pm}^{j}:{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}_{t}\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>±</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\pm}^{j}:{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}_{t}\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is holomorphic with respect to <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> near infinity where <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>±</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{\pm}\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with the standard complex structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Near the infinity of the cylindrical end around <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{\pm}^{j})^{*}\alpha=H_{\pm}^{j}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{\pm}^{j})^{*}\alpha=H_{\pm}^{j}dt</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a nondegenerate 1-periodic Hamiltonian or zero. In the former resp. latter case we say this end is of <span id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Floer type</span> resp. <span id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Morse type</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Near the infinity of the cylindrical end around <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the almost complex structure <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{z}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with a fixed almost complex structure <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\pm}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\pm}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A cylindrical surface <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a Floer datum <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S10.Thmthm2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Floer domain</span>, which we still denote by <math id="S10.Thmthm2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for brevity.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Floer domain <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,\sigma=(j,J,\alpha))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,\sigma=(j,J,\alpha))</annotation></semantics></math> and a smooth map <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, one can take the form</p>
<table id="S10.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{\sigma}u:=(du+X_{\sigma}(u))^{0,1}\in\Omega^{0,1}(\Sigma,u^{*}TX)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{\sigma}u:=(du+X_{\sigma}(u))^{0,1}\in\Omega^{0,1}(\Sigma,u^{*}TX)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined via</p>
<table id="S10.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(X_{\sigma},-)=d_{X}\alpha," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(X_{\sigma},-)=d_{X}\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the differentiation along the <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> direction, and <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection onto the <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-part determined by the domain complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and the target almost complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.2. </span>Multimodules from smooth domains</h4>

<div id="S10.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can define a particular kind of multimodules coming from Floer domains. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected Floer domain with <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> negative ends and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> positive ends such that all cylindrical ends are of Floer type (see Definition <a href="#S10.Thmthm2" title="Definition 10.2. ‣ 10.1. Floer data and Floer domains ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>), with limiting Hamiltonians <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1},\ldots,H_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1},\ldots,H_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> negative ends and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,H_{n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,H_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the positive ends. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1},\ldots,J_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1},\ldots,J_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the limiting almost complex structures on the negative ends and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,J_{n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,J_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the limiting almost complex structures on the positive ends. Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be Floer flow category associated to <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1}^{\prime},J_{1}^{\prime}),\ldots,(H_{n}^{\prime},J_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1}^{\prime},J_{1}^{\prime}),\ldots,(H_{n}^{\prime},J_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Floer domain <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> defines a multimodule <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, given any tuple <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{p}}=(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{p}}=(p_{1},\ldots,p_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> of objects of <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}=(p_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,p_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}=(p_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,p_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of objects of <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the space of solutions to</p>
<table id="S10.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma}u=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma}u=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which are asymptotic to <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> near the <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th negative end and to <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> near the <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th positive end. We compactify this space by allowing sphere bubbles and Floer trajectory breakings, and denote the compactified space by</p>
<table id="S10.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then there are natural structural maps</p>
<table id="S10.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝔭</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{i}q_{i}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{i-1}q_{i}p_{i+1}\cdots p_{m};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S10.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{j-1}^{\prime}q_{j}^{\prime}p_{j+1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}\times M_{q_{j}^{\prime}p_{j}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{j-1}^{\prime}q_{j}^{\prime}p_{j+1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}\times M_{q_{j}^{\prime}p_{j}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">It is straightforward to see that the collection of moduli spaces <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}};{\mathfrak{p}}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the above maps satisfy the requirement for a multimodule over <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, both <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have a free <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-action, hence <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod{\mathbb{F}}_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has a free action by <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it is easy to see that the multimodule <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivariant with respect to the diagonal embeddings <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\to\Pi^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\to\Pi^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\to\Pi^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\to\Pi^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We summarize the properties of this multimodule.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsituationm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_situationm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsituationm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation Module(i)</span></span><span id="Thmsituationm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsituationm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsituationm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmsituationm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a compact symplectic manifold. <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote16" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">16</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">16</span></span>
                    
                    
                    
                  <span id="footnote16.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Here </span><math id="footnote16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote16.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> is allowed to be </span><math id="footnote16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote16.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">, the situation of the cigar bimodule described in Subsubsection </span><a href="#S10.SS3.SSS6" title="10.3.6. Cigar module ‣ 10.3. PSS type bimodules ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3.6</span></a><span id="footnote16.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span> are Floer flow categories associated to pairs <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m});(H^{\prime},J^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m});(H^{\prime},J^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the multimodule over <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a smooth Floer domain <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> negative cylindrical ends and one positive cylindrical end. Moreover, the Floer datum on <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{j}dt,J_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{j}dt,J_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th negative end and <math id="Thmsituationm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{\prime}dt,J^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{\prime}dt,J^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> on the positive end.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.3</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationm1" title="Situation Module(i). ‣ 10.2. Multimodules from smooth domains ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Module(i)</span></a>, the multimodule <math id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov multimodule over <math id="S10.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We give examples of a few concrete cases which appear naturally in applications.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.2.1. </span>Bimodules for continuation maps</h5>

<div id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first one is the bimodule coming from the continuation map construction. Given Floer flow categories <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>±</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a pair <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\pm},J_{\pm})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\pm},J_{\pm})</annotation></semantics></math> on the same symplectic manifold <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, a homotopy of the Floer data</p>
<table id="S10.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(j_{0},H_{s}dt,J_{s,t}),\quad\quad\quad\lim_{s\to\pm\infty}H_{s}=H_{\pm},\ \lim_{s\to\pm}J_{s,t}=J_{\pm}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.889em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mo>±</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j_{0},H_{s}dt,J_{s,t}),\quad\quad\quad\lim_{s\to\pm\infty}H_{s}=H_{\pm},\ \lim_{s\to\pm}J_{s,t}=J_{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">over the infinite cylinder, where <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard complex structure on <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a special case of Floer data over an arbitrary punctured sphere. Hence one obtains an <math id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}^{-},{\mathbb{F}}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}^{-},{\mathbb{F}}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>-module, usually called the bimodule of continuation maps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is a special one corresponding to the constant Floer datum on the infinite cylinder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10.4</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">diagonal bimodule</span> of a Floer flow category <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,J)</annotation></semantics></math> is the bimodule for continuation maps associated to the <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-independent Floer data <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j_{0},Hdt,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j_{0},Hdt,J)</annotation></semantics></math> on the infinite cylinder. It is denoted by <math id="S10.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.2.2. </span>Poincaré duality multimodule</h5>

<div id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a surface <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\rm PD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\rm PD}</annotation></semantics></math> with two negative cylindrical ends and no positive ends. Let <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\rm PD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\rm PD}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Floer datum on <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\rm PD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\rm PD}</annotation></semantics></math> which converges to <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}dt</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}dt</annotation></semantics></math> on the two negative ends. Let <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hamiltonian Floer flow categories associated to <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, consider the trivial flow category <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are elements of <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> and the only morphisms are identities. Then we describe a multimodule <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2};{\mathbb{O}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2};{\mathbb{O}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given objects <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi\cong{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi\cong{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli spaces of maps <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma^{\rm PD}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma^{\rm PD}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> solving <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\rm PD}}u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\rm PD}}u=0</annotation></semantics></math> which converges to the (uncapped) orbits <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{p}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{p}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{p}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{p}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at the two negative ends; moreover, the spherical class obtained by adding the cappings has symplectic area <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. We compactify the space by allowing sphere bubbles as well as Floer breakings at the two negative ends, and denote the compactified space by</p>
<table id="S10.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then there are natural maps</p>
<table id="S10.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p_{1}q_{1}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}\times M_{q_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}\to M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}q_{1}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}\times M_{q_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}\to M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S10.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p_{2}q_{2}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{2}}\times M_{p_{1}q_{2};a}^{\mathbb{X}^{\rm PD}}\to M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>PD</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{2}q_{2}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{2}}\times M_{p_{1}q_{2};a}^{\mathbb{X}^{\rm PD}}\to M_{p_{1}p_{2};a}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm PD}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">making it a multimodule over <math id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2};{\mathbb{O}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2};{\mathbb{O}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call it a <span id="S10.SS2.SSS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Poincaré duality multimodule</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.3. </span>PSS type bimodules</h4>

<div id="S10.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to establish the relation between the Floer homology and the integral homology of the symplectic manifold, one typically considers the PSS (Piunikhin–Salamon–Schwarz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>) maps. In the current situation, we need to consider a few similar constructions. In terms of the formal terms, we describe bimodules among the three flow categories: Floer, Morse, and Pearly flow categories.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.1. </span>Deformed Morse-to-Morse bimodule</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Morse–Smale pair on the almost Kähler manifold <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Morse flow category associated to <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are critical points of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the trivial product of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of the identity bimodule from <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself, we consider a bimodule involving holomorphic spheres. Indeed, for each <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the space of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic maps <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:S^{2}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:S^{2}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> representing the class <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with two fixed marked points <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{-},z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{-},z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote its Gromov compactification by <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>parametrized</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of genus zero stable <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic spheres with two marked points with energy <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with a rigid component. Let the two markings be <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. There are two evaluation maps</p>
<table id="S10.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm ev}_{\pm}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a)\to X." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ev</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>parametrized</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}_{\pm}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a)\to X.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then for each pair of ojects <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}}),y=(b_{y},\underline{\smash{y}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}}),y=(b_{y},\underline{\smash{y}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S10.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}:={\rm ev}_{-}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}})\cap{\rm ev}_{+}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}})\cap\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a_{x}-b_{y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>ev</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>ev</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>st</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>parametrized</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}:={\rm ev}_{-}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}})\cap{\rm ev}_{+}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}})\cap\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\rm parametrized}(X,J,a_{x}-b_{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">There are obvious structural maps coming from breaking of Morse trajectories:</p>
<table id="S10.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{xz}^{\mathbb{M}}\times M_{z;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{xz}^{\mathbb{M}}\times M_{z;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{x;w}^{\mathbb{MM}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{M}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{x;w}^{\mathbb{MM}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{M}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">forming a bimodule over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call it the <span id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">deformed Morse-to-Morse bimodule</span>, denoted by <math id="S10.SS3.SSS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.2. </span>Floer-to-Morse and Morse-to-Floer bimodules</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe two bimodules relating the Morse flow category <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Floer flow category <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be denoted by <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a compact almost complex manifold <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Morse flow category associated to a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are critical points of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the trivial product of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Floer flow category associated to a nondegenerate Hamiltonian <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> viewed as a positive end with canonical cylindrical coordinates. Choose a Hamiltonian connection <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is equal to <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and which is zero near <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for each object <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can consider the equation</p>
<table id="S10.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotic to the 1-periodic orbit underlying <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and the map <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> represents the capped orbit <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Compactify this space by allowing sphere bubblings and Floer trajectory breakings on the positive ends. Denote the compactified moduli space by <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{p}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{p}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an evaluation map</p>
<table id="S10.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm ev}_{0}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{p}^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ev</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}_{0}:\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{p}^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">at the point <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for each pair of objects <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{x}\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{x}\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in{\rm crit}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in{\rm crit}f</annotation></semantics></math>, then define</p>
<table id="S10.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}:={\rm ev}_{0}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}})\cap\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{(-a_{x})\cdot p}^{\mathbb{MF}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>ev</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}:={\rm ev}_{0}^{-1}(\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}})\cap\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{(-a_{x})\cdot p}^{\mathbb{MF}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unstable manifold of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}}</annotation></semantics></math> is its compactification. Then there are natural structural maps</p>
<table id="S10.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{xy}^{\mathbb{M}}\times M_{y;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{xy}^{\mathbb{M}}\times M_{y;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{x;q}^{\mathbb{MF}}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{x;q}^{\mathbb{MF}}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">defining a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}};{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}};{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, if we let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> viewed as a negative end and if we choose a Hamiltonian connection <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> on this domain which is equal to <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math> on the cylindrical end, then we can define moduli spaces <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p;x}^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p;x}^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> similarly, giving a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.3. </span>The Pearly-to-Floer and Floer-to-Pearly bimodules</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose we have fixed data for the Pearly flow category <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Floer flow category on the almost Kähler manifold <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, one has a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for the moduli space of solutions to</p>
<table id="S10.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0,\ u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0,\ u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we can take the “pearly compactification” in the same way as defining the pearly flow category. Indeed, we treat <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a special marked point around which there is the standard cylindrical coordinates <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. When compactifying the moduli space, sphere bubbles at <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> are taken only modulo translation in <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one can insert gradient segments to obtain the corresponding pearly compactification. For each pair of objects <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{P}}={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{P}}={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is the moduli space <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with structural maps</p>
<table id="S10.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{xy}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{y;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{xy}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{y;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{x;q}^{\mathbb{PF}}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{x;q}^{\mathbb{PF}}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{x;p}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">giving a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, one also has the Floer-to-Pearly bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.4. </span>Morse-to-Pearly and Pearly-to-Morse bimodules</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> and a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{P}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{P}})</annotation></semantics></math>. For simplicity, we assume <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same Morse–Smale pair, though we can certainly take different ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a pair of objects <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(a_{x},\underline{\smash{x}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(b_{y},\underline{\smash{y}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(b_{y},\underline{\smash{y}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic maps <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of symplectic area <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{x}-b_{y}\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{x}-b_{y}\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S10.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(-\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us},\ u(+\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>st</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(-\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us},\ u(+\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">When compactifying these moduli spaces, the bubbling at negative ends are taken modulo only the translation in <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> while bubbling at positive ends are taken modulo both translation and rotations; moreover, we also insert gradient segments at nodes connecting <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and the principal component. In this way, one obtains a compact stratified space</p>
<table id="S10.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with structural maps</p>
<table id="S10.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{xw}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{w;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{xw}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{w;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{x;w}^{\mathbb{PM}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{M}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{x;w}^{\mathbb{PM}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{M}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">giving a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{P}},{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly one has a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{P}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}},{\mathbb{P}})</annotation></semantics></math> by taking “half pearly” compactifications at the positive end instead of the negative one.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.5. </span>Pearly-to-Pearly bimodule</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a pair of critical points <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}},\underline{\smash{y}}\in{\rm crit}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}},\underline{\smash{y}}\in{\rm crit}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the space of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic maps <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> with symplectic area <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(-\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(-\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{x}}}^{\rm us}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(+\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>st</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(+\infty)\in W_{\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\rm st}</annotation></semantics></math>. We take the “pearly compactification,” namely, when sphere bubbles at <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the bubbles are taken modulo only the <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>-translation. Moreover, we insert (possibly broken) gradient segments at nodes connecting the distinguished marked points <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the compactified moduli space by</p>
<table id="S10.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}};\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{PP}}(X,J,a)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\underline{\smash{x}};\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{PP}}(X,J,a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A typical configuration of this moduli space is shown in Figure <a href="#S10.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 10.3.5. Pearly-to-Pearly bimodule ‣ 10.3. PSS type bimodules ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S10.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.15847v1/PP_bimodule.svg" id="S10.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:522/118;" width="522" height="118"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Configurations in <math id="S10.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then for each pair of objects <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x}),y=(\underline{\smash{y}},a_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x}),y=(\underline{\smash{y}},a_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S10.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}}:=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{PP}}(X,J,a_{x}-a_{y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}}:=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}\underline{\smash{y}}}^{\mathbb{PP}}(X,J,a_{x}-a_{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then there are natural maps</p>
<table id="S10.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{xx^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{x^{\prime};y}^{\mathbb{PP}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{xx^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{P}}\times M_{x^{\prime};y}^{\mathbb{PP}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ M_{x;y^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{PP}}\times M_{y^{\prime}y}^{\mathbb{P}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ M_{x;y^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{PP}}\times M_{y^{\prime}y}^{\mathbb{P}}\to M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">defining the structural maps of a bimodule over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{P},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this bimoulde the <span id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pearly-to-Pearly bimodule</span>, denoted by <math id="S10.SS3.SSS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that there is a “classical part” which corresponds to the diagonal bimodule of the Morse flow category.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">10.3.6. </span>Cigar module</h5>

<div id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct a multiplicative identity of the Floer homology, we consider an analogue of the PSS moduli space where we do not have interior markings or Morse trajectories. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\rm cigar}\cong{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>cigar</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\rm cigar}\cong{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open Riemann surface with a cylindrical end. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Floer flow category on <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a nondegenerate Hamiltonian <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the “trivial” flow category consisting of a single object <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> with self-morphism space being a single point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a Floer datum on <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\rm cigar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>cigar</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\rm cigar}</annotation></semantics></math> which coincides with <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> near the infinity. Let <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Floer flow category associated to <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. The cigar module is a bimodule <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\rm cigar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>cigar</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\rm cigar}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{O}},{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{O}},{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> whose morphism spaces consist of solutions to the Floer equation on <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, for each object <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the space of solutions to</p>
<table id="S10.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma^{\rm cigar}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\rm cigar}}u=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>cigar</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>cigar</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma^{\rm cigar}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\rm cigar}}u=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the 1-periodic orbit <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> underlying <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> represents the capped orbit <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, compactify this moduli space by allowing sphere bubbling and Floer trajectory breakings. Let the compactified space be <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{o;p}^{\rm cigar}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>cigar</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{o;p}^{\rm cigar}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there are structural maps</p>
<table id="S10.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{o;q}^{\rm cigar}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{o;p}^{\rm cigar}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>cigar</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>cigar</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{o;q}^{\rm cigar}\times M_{qp}^{\mathbb{F}}\to M_{o;p}^{\rm cigar},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">making a bimodule over <math id="S10.SS3.SSS6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{O}};{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{O}};{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.4. </span>Concatenations</h4>

<div id="S10.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the situation corresponding to concatenating Floer data on different domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsituationc1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_situationc">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsituationc1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation Concatenation(i)</span></span><span id="Thmsituationc1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsituationc1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsituationc1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmsituationc1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a compact symplectic manifold. <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n},{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n},{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are Floer flow categories associated to pairs <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m}),(G_{1},I_{1}),\ldots,(G_{n},I_{n}),(G^{\prime},I^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{1},J_{1}),\ldots,(H_{m},J_{m}),(G_{1},I_{1}),\ldots,(G_{n},I_{n}),(G^{\prime},I^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multimodule over <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{G}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{G}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> resp. over <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n};{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n};{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to smooth Floer domain <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="Thmsituationc1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> as described in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationm1" title="Situation Module(i). ‣ 10.2. Multimodules from smooth domains ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Module(i)</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.5</span></span><span id="S10.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationc1" title="Situation Concatenation(i). ‣ 10.4. Concatenations ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Concatenation(i)</span></a>, the concatenation <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov multimodule over <math id="S10.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{i-1},{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{G}}_{i+1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n};{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{i-1},{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{G}}_{i+1},\ldots,{\mathbb{G}}_{n};{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Straightforward.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11. </span>Homotopies of Multimodules</h3>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we descibe several geometrically defined bimodule homotopies. As we only use bimodule homotopies to show certain chain maps are canonically defined up to homotopy, we will not provide the most general treatment. Hence, we only give a minimal descriptions of bimodules which will be used in this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S11.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.1. </span>Homotopies from varying Floer data</h4>

<div id="S11.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the notions of Floer data and Floer domains (Definition <a href="#S10.Thmthm2" title="Definition 10.2. ‣ 10.1. Floer data and Floer domains ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11.1</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth 1-parameter family of Floer domain consists of the following objects.</p>
<ol id="S11.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S11.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth genus zero surface <math id="S11.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth 1-parameter family of domain complex structures <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that there exists an open and precompact region <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{0}\subseteq\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{0}\subseteq\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and diffeomorphisms</p>
<table id="S11.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{i}^{\pm}:{\mathbb{R}}_{\pm}\times S^{1}\to U_{i}^{\pm}\subseteq\Sigma\setminus\Sigma_{0},\ i=1,\ldots,l_{\pm}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>±</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>±</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{\pm}:{\mathbb{R}}_{\pm}\times S^{1}\to U_{i}^{\pm}\subseteq\Sigma\setminus\Sigma_{0},\ i=1,\ldots,l_{\pm},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> are all open subsets of <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\setminus\Sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\setminus\Sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic with respect to all <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\tau}|_{U_{i}^{\pm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\tau}|_{U_{i}^{\pm}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a positive/negative cylindrical end. Two cylindrical ends are germ-equivalent if they differ by a translation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth 1-parameter family of Hamiltonian connections <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> such that on each <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the cylindrical coordinates provided by <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\tau}=H_{i}^{\pm}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\tau}=H_{i}^{\pm}dt</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate 1-periodic Hamiltonian on <math id="S11.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth 1-parameter family of domain-dependent, <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structures <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that on each <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the cylindrical coordinates, <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\tau}=J_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\tau}=J_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed almost complex structure on <math id="S11.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Denote such a 1-parameter family of Floer domains by <math id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for homotopy.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S11.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the family of incoming Floer flow categories</p>
<table id="S11.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the collection of outgoing Floer flow categories</p>
<table id="S11.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For each pair of collections of objects</p>
<table id="S11.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S11.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ p_{j}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{j},\ j=1,\ldots,m,\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ p_{j}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{j},\ j=1,\ldots,m,\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S11.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ p_{i}^{\prime}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{i}^{\prime},\ i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ p_{i}^{\prime}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}_{i}^{\prime},\ i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">we consider the moduli space</p>
<table id="S11.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">which is the Gromov–Floer compactification of</p>
<table id="S11.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{(\tau,u)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{l}\tau\in[0,1],u:\Sigma\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u|_{U_{j}}(s,t)=p_{j},j=1,\ldots,m,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to+\infty}u|_{U_{i}^{\prime}}=p_{i}^{\prime},\ i=1,\ldots,n\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{(\tau,u)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{l}\tau\in[0,1],u:\Sigma\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u|_{U_{j}}(s,t)=p_{j},j=1,\ldots,m,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to+\infty}u|_{U_{i}^{\prime}}=p_{i}^{\prime},\ i=1,\ldots,n\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">This is a compact topological space stratified by a regular poset <math id="S11.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that the subset of pairs <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,u)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> defines a multimodule <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\prime},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}}_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there are natural structural maps</p>
<table id="S11.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{j}q_{j}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{j-1}q_{j}p_{j+1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{i-1}^{\prime}q_{i}^{\prime}p_{i+1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}\times M_{q_{i}^{\prime}p_{i}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}^{\prime}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{j}q_{j}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}}\times M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{j-1}q_{j}p_{j+1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{i-1}^{\prime}q_{i}^{\prime}p_{i+1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}\times M_{q_{i}^{\prime}p_{i}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}^{\prime}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S11.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}_{0}}\sqcup M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}_{1}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>⊔</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}_{0}}\sqcup M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{X}}_{1}}\to M_{p_{1}\cdots p_{m};p_{1}^{\prime}\cdots p_{n}^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{H}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">One can then check that <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with these structural maps defines a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, enriched in <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will mainly consider the case when <math id="S11.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We summarize the setting in the following situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsituationh1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_situationh">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsituationh1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation Homotopy(i)</span></span><span id="Thmsituationh1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsituationh1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsituationh1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmsituationh1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a compact symplectic manifold. <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}},t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}},t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth 1-parameter family of Floer domains with <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> negative cylindrical ends and <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> positive cylindrical end. <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy from the multimodule <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{0}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{0}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the multimodule <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="Thmsituationh1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.2. </span>Homotopies from gluing</h4>

<div id="S11.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the notion of multimodule concatenations. Let <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be Floer domains defining Floer multimodules <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> which can be composed to a multimodule <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that there is only one breaking on the surface <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S11.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 11.2. Homotopies from gluing ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Then depending on choosing the cylindrical ends on both sides of that breaking, there is a canonical way to construct a 1-parameter family of Floer domains <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S}{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S}{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S\in[S_{0},+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in[S_{0},+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes the neck-length, such that when <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the Floer domain degenerates to the nodal <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote this family by <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a smooth function <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\mapsto S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\mapsto S</annotation></semantics></math> which sends <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding Floer domain. For <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S(\tau)}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S(\tau)}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the multimodule coming from the Floer domain <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any collection of incoming objects <math id="S11.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and outgoing objects <math id="S11.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has the moduli space <math id="S11.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the natural compactification of</p>
<table id="S11.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Big\{(\tau,u)\ |\ \tau\in[0,1],\ u:\Sigma^{\tau}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\tau}}u=0,\ \lim_{s\to\pm\infty}u|_{U_{\pm}^{i}}(s,t)=p_{i}^{\pm}\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>u</mi><msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big\{(\tau,u)\ |\ \tau\in[0,1],\ u:\Sigma^{\tau}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\tau}}u=0,\ \lim_{s\to\pm\infty}u|_{U_{\pm}^{i}}(s,t)=p_{i}^{\pm}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S11.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.15847v1/homotopy_gluing.svg" id="S11.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:290/166;" width="290" height="166"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>When breaking along the dotted line, the multimodule becomes the concatenation of two.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S11.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As usual, we have the natural structural maps</p>
<table id="S11.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{q}}^{-}}^{{\mathfrak{F}}^{-}}\times M_{{\mathfrak{q}}^{-}{\mathfrak{q}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}\times M_{{\mathfrak{q}}^{+}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathfrak{F}}^{+}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{H}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔮</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔮</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔮</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>𝔉</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{q}}^{-}}^{{\mathfrak{F}}^{-}}\times M_{{\mathfrak{q}}^{-}{\mathfrak{q}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}\times M_{{\mathfrak{q}}^{+}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathfrak{F}}^{+}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{H}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S11.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}\sqcup M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S_{0}}{\mathbb{Y}}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊔</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔭</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}\sqcup M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S_{0}}{\mathbb{Y}}}\to M_{{\mathfrak{p}}^{-}{\mathfrak{p}}^{+}}^{\mathbb{H}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">These structural maps provide a homotopy <math id="S11.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S11.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S_{0}}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{S_{0}}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmsituationg1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_situationg">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmsituationg1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Situation Glue(i)</span></span><span id="Thmsituationg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmsituationg1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmsituationg1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmsituationg1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a compact symplectic manifold. <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are Floer multimodules as described in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationc1" title="Situation Concatenation(i). ‣ 10.4. Concatenations ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Concatenation(i)</span></a>. <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth family of Floer domains such that for <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the concatenation <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\#\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}\#\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Floer multimodule associated to the Floer domain <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>ℍ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the homotopy between <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmsituationg1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.3. </span>Homotopies associated to PSS type moduli spaces</h4>

<div id="S11.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On a symplectic manifold, consider the three geometric flow categories: a Floer flow category <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a Morse flow category <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a pearly flow category <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the following diagram</p>
<table id="S11.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<span id="S11.E1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_align_bottom"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S11.E1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="158.45" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-114.84699558053782px" version="1.1" viewBox="53.22 114.85 288.56 158.45" width="288.56"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 53.215084917832 158.44532739774672) translate(53.22,0)"><g transform="translate(85.21,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\mathbb{F}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\mathbb{F}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(78.85,0) translate(0,35.01) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="21.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(85.92,0) translate(0,10.14)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -0.86 7.4" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -5.73 4.76" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 93.88023282509383 0 C 129.10352268538338 35.22328986028954 58.656942964804294 35.22328986028954 93.88023282509383 0" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 83.29455612011597 -5.017284933290525 L 83.28359816865141 -5.017284933290525" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(16.53,0) translate(0,-28.62) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.64em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="22.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(8.57,0) translate(0,-67.88)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 7.08 2.34" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 3.51 6.57" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 83.29455612011597 -5.017284933290525 L 8.574755372917965 -67.87431146060347" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 8.586536754261363 -67.87431146060347 L 8.574755372917965 -67.87431146060347" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 104.48522846184447 -5.152200751900425 L 104.54115835284567 -5.2242403095055865" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(139.85,0) translate(0,-28.64) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.49em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="20.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(179.21,0) translate(0,-69.15)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -3.45 6.6" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -7.05 2.4" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 104.48522846184447 -5.152200751900425 L 179.2050292090425 -69.14469485496014" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 179.1494371354275 -69.07265529735497 L 179.2050292090425 -69.14469485496014" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-10.49,0) translate(0,-78.99) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\mathbb{M}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\mathbb{M}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(-53.22,0) translate(0,-106.26) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="26.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{MM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{MM}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(-0.46,0) translate(0,-86.59)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -3.66 -6.49" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 1.83 -7.22" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 -78.98618097174658 C -49.813200498132005 -78.98618097174658 0 -128.79938146987857 0 -78.98618097174658" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 12.41138965505872 -72.43201695356822 L 12.42317103640212 -72.43201695356822" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(46.41,0) translate(0,-50.36) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.64em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="22.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(87.13,0) translate(0,-9.58)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -7.08 -2.34" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -3.51 -6.57" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 12.41138965505872 -72.43201695356822 L 87.13119040225673 -9.574990426255276" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 87.12023245079217 -9.574990426255276 L 87.13119040225673 -9.574990426255276" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.493072513988343 -82.24170600887946 L 10.493072513988343 -81.68823444877931" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(77.47,0) translate(0,-90.56) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="22.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MP}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(177.27,0) translate(0,-81.97)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.493072513988343 -82.24170600887946 L 177.26739313619933 -81.68823444877931" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 177.26739313619933 -82.24170600887946 L 177.26739313619933 -81.68823444877931" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(177.27,0) translate(0,-78.99) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\mathbb{P}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\mathbb{P}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(204.81,0) translate(0,-106.26) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.61em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="22.24"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{PP}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{PP}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(194.98,0) translate(0,-79.64)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 6.38 -3.85" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 7.28 1.61" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 186.12692856567602 -78.98618097174658 C 186.12692856567602 -128.79938146987857 235.94012906380803 -78.98618097174658 186.12692856567602 -78.98618097174658" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 175.32975706335617 -73.66958983429112 L 175.27416498974117 -73.59755027668595" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(111.19,0) translate(0,-50.35) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.49em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="20.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(100.61,0) translate(0,-9.68)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 3.45 -6.6" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 7.05 -2.4" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 175.32975706335617 -73.66958983429112 L 100.60995631615816 -9.677095731231407" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 100.66588620715935 -9.749135288836568 L 100.60995631615816 -9.677095731231407" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 177.26739313619933 -76.28412749471383 L 177.26739313619933 -75.7306559346137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(77.47,0) translate(0,-67.42) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.88)" width="22.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S11.E1.m1.pic1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PM}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔹</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PM}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(10.49,0) translate(0,-76.01)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.493072513988343 -76.28412749471383 L 177.26739313619933 -75.7306559346137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.493072513988343 -76.28412749471383 L 10.493072513988343 -75.7306559346137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g></svg></span>
</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the arrows are bimodules. Notice that we can compose bimodules in this diagram following the arrows. We say a triangle in this diagram is <span id="S11.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">commutative</span> if the composed bimodule from two edges is homotopic to the third bimodule. We will explain 1) why the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose construction actually can show all triangle whose concatenation happens at <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> commutes, 2) why the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and 3) why the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{FM}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not (at least not obviously) homotopic to <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S11.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S11.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">11.3.1. </span>Floer-to-Pearly, Pearly-to-Floer bimodules, and their concatenations</h5>

<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a 1-parameter family of Floer data on the infinite cylinder, parametrized by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, given by a Hamiltonian connection <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> supported in a region of length about <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-1/\log\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1/\log\tau</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and converges to zero when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, over the long cylinder it is equal to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a nondegenerate 1-periodic Hamiltonian. Given a pair <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x}),y=(\underline{\smash{y}},a_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x}),y=(\underline{\smash{y}},a_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> of objects of <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space</p>
<table id="S11.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{(\tau,u)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}\tau\in[0,1),\ u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u(s,t)\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{y}}},\ \omega(u)=a_{x}-a_{y}\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>us</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>st</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{(\tau,u)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}\tau\in[0,1),\ u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u(s,t)\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{y}}},\ \omega(u)=a_{x}-a_{y}\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We consider the pearly compactification at <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and meanwhile allowing <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which means the map <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> should break along Hamiltonian orbits as <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-1/\log\tau\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1/\log\tau\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S11.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 11.3.1. Floer-to-Pearly, Pearly-to-Floer bimodules, and their concatenations ‣ 11.3. Homotopies associated to PSS type moduli spaces ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, where the neck length <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-1/\log\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1/\log\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S11.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.15847v1/PP_to_PFFP.svg" id="S11.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:464/344;" width="464" height="344"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>The homotopy from <math id="S11.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then the restriction to the <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> slice gives moduli spaces of the bimodule concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> (concatenation at Floer flow category). On the other hand, the restriction to the <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> slice gives moduli spaces of the bimodule <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{PP}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote this homotopy by</p>
<table id="S11.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{PF}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{FP}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{PF}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{FP}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The concatenation at the Pearly flow category and its homotopy to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bit more complicated. It is the concatenation of two homotopies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">First, consider the infinite cylinder equipped with a 1-parameter family of Floer data, parametrized by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hamiltonian connection is the flat one <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives rise to the diagonal bimodule <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while as <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases, there is a long cylinder of length growing to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> on which the Hamiltonian connection is trivial. Then for each pair of objects <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,p^{\prime}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,p^{\prime}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the space</p>
<table id="S11.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{(\tau,u)\left|\begin{array}[]{cc}\tau\in[0,1),\ u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u(s,t)=p,\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)=p^{\prime}\end{array}\right.\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{(\tau,u)\left|\begin{array}[]{cc}\tau\in[0,1),\ u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma_{\tau}}u=0,\\
\displaystyle\lim_{s\to-\infty}u(s,t)=p,\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)=p^{\prime}\end{array}\right.\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We compactify it naturally by adding sphere bubbles, Floer trajectory breakings at both negative and positive ends, and allowing cylindrical breakings when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. When the cylinder breaks off, we only quotient by the translation symmetry but not the rotation symmetry. This provides a compact moduli space</p>
<table id="S11.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pp^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pp^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and gives a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> to an intermediate bimodule, denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Figure <a href="#S11.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 11.3.1. Floer-to-Pearly, Pearly-to-Floer bimodules, and their concatenations ‣ 11.3. Homotopies associated to PSS type moduli spaces ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S11.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.15847v1/FF_to_FPPF.svg" id="S11.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:464/617;" width="464" height="617"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>The homotopy between <math id="S11.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we introduce a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}\circ\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}\circ\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">First, given a nonnegative real number <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we can consider the notion of <span id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">telephone</span> (see Figure <a href="#S11.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 11.3.1. Floer-to-Pearly, Pearly-to-Floer bimodules, and their concatenations ‣ 11.3. Homotopies associated to PSS type moduli spaces ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) of length <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a pearly-type configuration with two ends being solutions to a Floer equation while the surfaces in between are holomorphic cylinders connected by Morse flow segments of total length <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. By compactifying the moduli space while allowing <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (meaning the Morse trajectories will break one or more times), one obtains moduli spaces</p>
<table id="S11.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pp^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pp^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">providing a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{F}\bullet\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then define</p>
<table id="S11.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}=\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}\cup\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}=\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}\cup\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">which is a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call it the <span id="S11.SS3.SSS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">telephone homotopy</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 11.2</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The homotopy <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},2}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to relate two different constructions of the Morse–Bott theory for the constant Hamiltonian <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The pearl flow category includes choosing a Morse function over the critical submanifolds and corresponds to the “cascades model” (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>) of Morse–Bott theory. In contrast, the natural output of the bimodule <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF},1}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the “fiber product model” (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for an early incarnation). The point of our construction is to compare the “Morse version” of Floer theory, coming from the flow category <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> that is associated with a nondegenerate Hamiltonian, with the Morse–Bott version, which uses pearly trajectories, using the fiber product model as an intermediate gadget.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">11.3.2. </span>Other concatenations along Pearly flow categories</h5>

<div id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can also construct a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{MP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{MP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a homotopy from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∘</mo><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{FP}\tilde{\circ}\mathbb{PM}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the two cases are dual to each other, we only explain the definition of the first homotopy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the bimodule <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the collection of moduli spaces</p>
<table id="S11.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{xp}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{xp}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is the natural compactification of the space of solutions to the Floer equation on the infinite cylinder with a Hamiltonian connection <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which is zero near <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and which is <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. We deform <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to a 1-parameter family <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that when <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the support of <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is pushed to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a bimodule consisting of moduli spaces whose configurations are similar to the third picture of Figure <a href="#S11.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 11.3.1. Floer-to-Pearly, Pearly-to-Floer bimodules, and their concatenations ‣ 11.3. Homotopies associated to PSS type moduli spaces ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Then one can continue the homotopy by inserting gradient segments of lengths which vary from <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. The very end of this homotopy is the concatenation <math id="S11.SS3.SSS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MP}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{PF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">11.3.3. </span>Remarks on concatenation at the Morse flow category</h5>

<div id="S11.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can also form the concatenation at the Morse flow category, for example, <math id="S11.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM}}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the category <math id="S11.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is homotopic to the bimodule <math id="S11.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, firstly by gluing broken Morse trajectories and secondly by gluing holomorphic curves. However, it is hard to regularize such a homotopy. In particular, when gluing holomorphic curves at a node, unlike the Pearly case, the gluing has a complex gluing parameter. This does not fit very well with our framework. This is the reason why we need to go through the Pearly flow category in order to prove the invertibility of the PSS and SSP maps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12. </span>Main Theorems on AMS Lifts</h3>

<div id="S12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we state the main results regarding regularizations of moduli spaces introduced in the previous section. The proof will be presented in the next part.</p>
</div>
<section id="S12.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.1. </span>Main statements for Floer flow categories</h4>

<div id="S12.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We state the main theorem about global charts of Floer flow categories and other types of flow categories. The constructions depend on many choices. Most of the dependence of the choices can be absorbed via the global chart construction of bimodules; however, the dependence of the outercollaring width and the choice of the so-called integral action (Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm1" title="Definition 15.1. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></a>) cannot be absorbed easily. Hence in the statement, we emphasize the dependence of these two choices.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem O</span></span><span id="Thmthma15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthma15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationf1" title="Situation Flow(i). ‣ 9.2. Floer flow category ‣ 9. Geometric Flow Categories ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Flow(i)</span></a>, given any outercollaring width <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a choice of integral action on <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm1" title="Definition 15.1. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></a>) on <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-outercollaring of <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a set of lifts <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm12" title="Definition 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Rigidified embeddings ‣ 3. Kuranishi Spaces ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a>) as a <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov flow category. Each individual lift in this set is called an </span><span id="Thmthma15.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMS lift</span><span id="Thmthma15.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> subject to the outercollaring width and the integral action. The AMS lifts admit coherent orientations in the sense of Definition <a href="#S7.Thmthm12" title="Definition 7.12. ‣ 7.1.4. Orientations ‣ 7.1. Unobstructedness, local finiteness, grading, and orientations ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.12</span></a>. Moreover, for any <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-outercollaring of <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an AMS lift of <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the outercollaring width <math id="Thmthma15.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon+\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon+\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the same integral action.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Section <a href="#S18" title="18. Proofs of the Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> except for the last statement, which is a straightforward consequence of the constructions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 12.1</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In fact what we mean by an AMS lift does not only mean the resulting flow category <math id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also all the choices made in the process of construction. When an follow-up construction depends on a given AMS lift, it means the construction <span id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a priori</span> depends on the choices made for the AMS lift.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The (in)dependence on the choice of the integral action is characterized by the following theorem. Recall that for the Floer flow category <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a bimodule <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the continuation map from <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J,H)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J,H)</annotation></semantics></math> with the trivial Floer data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 12.2</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationf1" title="Situation Flow(i). ‣ 9.2. Floer flow category ‣ 9. Geometric Flow Categories ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Flow(i)</span></a>, let <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two AMS lifts of <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the same outercollaring width and two different integral actions, provided by Theorem <a href="#Thmthma15" title="Theorem O. ‣ 12.1. Main statements for Floer flow categories ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">O</span></a>. Then there exists a class of lifts of the corresponding outercollaring of <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> as a bimodule over <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, with respect to the coherent orientations on <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the lift is coherently oriented.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Section <a href="#S20" title="20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The (in)dependence on other choices for the AMS lift is included in the theorem on multimodules (Theorem <a href="#Thmthma16" title="Theorem P. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">P</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.2. </span>Multimodules and homotopies</h4>

<div id="S12.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we state the main theorems concerning regularizing flow multimodules and homotopies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we formulate the theorem on lifts for multimodules. Due to technical reasons, we must consider the dependence on a specific choice, namely, that of integral actions (Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm1" title="Definition 15.1. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></a> and Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm2" title="Definition 15.2. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.2</span></a>) in the construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthma16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem P</span></span><span id="Thmthma16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthma16.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multimodule associated a smooth Floer domain <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> negative ends and <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> positive ends satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S12.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{0,1\},\ n=0\Longrightarrow m=2\ {\rm and}\ m=0\Longrightarrow n=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>and</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{0,1\},\ n=0\Longrightarrow m=2\ {\rm and}\ m=0\Longrightarrow n=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthma16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is a multimodule over <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are Floer flow categories and <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Floer flow category when <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the trivial flow category when <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a cigar bimodule over <math id="Thmthma16.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{O}};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕆</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{O}};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma16.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmthma16.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthma16.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Choose a common outercollaring width and compatible integral actions on the involved Floer flow categories (see Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm2" title="Definition 15.2. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.2</span></a>). Let <math id="Thmthma16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be AMS lifts to <math id="Thmthma16.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthma16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m},{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the fixed outercollaring width and these integral actions provided by Theorem <a href="#Thmthma15" title="Theorem O. ‣ 12.1. Main statements for Floer flow categories ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">O</span></a> (when <math id="Thmthma16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}={\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}={\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math>, take <math id="Thmthma16.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}={\mathbb{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}={\mathbb{O}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthma16.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S12.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S12.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a set of lifts of </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, denoted by </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (called an AMS lift subject to the chosen integral actions), which is a multimodule over </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{k};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{k};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Moreover, with respect to the coherent orientations on </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{m},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the lift </span><math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is coherently oriented.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any two AMS lifts of </span><math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (subject to the same set of integral actions) are homotopic as multimodules enriched in </span><math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> via a lift of the the trivial homotopy from the outercollaring of </span><math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to itself and the homotopy is coherently oriented.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Section <a href="#S18" title="18. Proofs of the Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that Theorem <a href="#Thmthma16" title="Theorem P. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">P</span></a> depends on choosing an integral action. For comparing different choices, we consider a particular homotopy. Recall that <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}\mathbb{F}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}\mathbb{F}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the diagonal bimodules. There is an obvious homotopy <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the concatenation (as concatenations of weak bimodules)</p>
<table id="S12.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Big(\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}\mathbb{F}_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}\mathbb{F}_{m}}\Big)\circ\mathbb{X}\circ\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}\mathbb{F}^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝕏</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}\mathbb{F}_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}\mathbb{F}_{m}}\Big)\circ\mathbb{X}\circ\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}\mathbb{F}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">to <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> by simultaneously gluing at both positive and negative ends. When <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace the above concatenation by</p>
<table id="S12.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
