<section id="S19.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">19.1.2. </span>Concatenations of domains</h5>

<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we consider the concatenation of two monotone multimodules associated to smooth curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S19.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 19.2</span></span><span id="S19.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S19.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationc1" title="Situation Concatenation(i). ‣ 10.4. Concatenations ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Concatenation(i)</span></a>, let</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{n}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{n}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the monotone flow categories enriched in <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the same outercollaring width. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be monotone multimodules over <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup><mo>;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> resp. over <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{n}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo>;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{n}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> constructed in Subsection <a href="#S14.SS4" title="14.4. Multimodules and homotopies for smooth domains ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.4</span></a>. Then there exists a concatenation <math id="S19.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S19.Thmthm1" title="Definition 19.1. ‣ 19.1.1. Concatenations of monotone multimodules ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The underlying monotone multimodules enriched in <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math> have a canonical concatenation. Indeed, let <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the singular curve with two components <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> glued along the cylindrical end of the concatenation. Notice that the lateral lines in <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> glue together to lateral lines in the singular domain. Then the same construction of <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> can be applied to <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, providing a monotone multimodule <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is straightforward to check that the outercollaring of <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a concatenation of the corresponding outercollarings of <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The remaining is to construct the rigidifications and the stable complex structures. For simplicity, we assume that <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bimodule <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bimodule <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}^{\prime};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}^{\prime};{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>; the general case is essentially the same with only additional notational complexities.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first consider the stable complex structure on the concatenation. Recall the case of smooth domains is obtained in Proposition <a href="#S14.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 14.19. ‣ 14.3.6. Stable complex structure ‣ 14.3. Construction of the domain flow categories ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.19</span></a> where the stable complex structure is naturally induced. The same fact holds for the concatenated domain. Therefore, the natural concatenation <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\circ\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\circ\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact in <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>ℂ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we consider rigidifications. We first rigidify the embeddings</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that this is a codimension zero embedding and there is a target top stratum</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{d;d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{d;d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\subseteq{\mathcal{C}}_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">such that</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{d+d^{\prime}}\times_{{\mathcal{G}}_{d}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{\prime}}}({\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}})\cong{\mathcal{C}}_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{d+d^{\prime}}\times_{{\mathcal{G}}_{d}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{\prime}}}({\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}})\cong{\mathcal{C}}_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that a rigidification of the above codimension zero embedding (see Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm9" title="Definition 14.9. ‣ 14.2.2. Rigidifications ‣ 14.2. The category of curves ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.9</span></a>) is a map</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{d;d^{\prime}}:B_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}\times Q_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\epsilon}\to B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{d;d^{\prime}}:B_{d}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}\times Q_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\epsilon}\to B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">These maps have to incorporate the existing one defining rigidifications of <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔹</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can construct these maps inductively, from the deepest strata up.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, if <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has no lower strata, then there is no constraint coming from rigidifications of <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔹</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and one can choose the rigidification arbitrarily.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In general one needs to construct <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{d;d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{d;d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> inductively. We only explain how to construct it when <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d;d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has only a codimension one stratum given by a decomposition <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{1}+d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{1}+d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This codimension one stratum is shared with another open stratum <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d_{1};d_{2}+d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d_{1};d_{2}+d^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{B}\circ\mathbb{B}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The rigidifications for these two open strata (with different symmetry groups) must be compatible along the shared fake boundary. We first choose rigidification of the codimension 1 embedding</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\to\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\to\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that the embedding can be factorized in two ways</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S19.Ex14.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="119.86" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-106.12999797444695px" version="1.1" viewBox="52.71 106.13 377.95 119.86" width="377.95"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 52.70994235754376 119.86254919972413) translate(0,3.46) translate(52.71,0)"><g transform="translate(95.64,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.94em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:10pt;" height="16.29" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 13.04)" width="87.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S19.Ex14.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\partial^{d_{1}d_{2}}B_{d}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\partial^{d_{1}d_{2}}B_{d}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 174.90974448438797 -10.863637073052875 L 174.93643205789704 -10.938146920294555" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(239.79,0) translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -5.64 4.87" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -7.44 -0.36" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 174.90974448438797 -10.863637073052875 L 239.78970597455722 -33.20877921850137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 239.76303951463478 -33.14092015105032 L 239.78970597455722 -33.20877921850137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-52.71,0) translate(0,-46.94) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0.94em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="16.5" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 13.04)" width="97.12"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S19.Ex14.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 41.94864283892002 -33.20877921850137 L 41.94864283892002 -33.18302064280433" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(110.2,0) translate(0,-10.86)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -7.44 0.48" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -5.72 -4.78" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 41.94864283892002 -33.20877921850137 L 110.19474344661296 -10.863637073052875" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 110.19474344661296 -10.891295871547236 L 110.19474344661296 -10.863637073052875" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 33.80335893178359 -58.01093055447454 L 33.837858532348314 -58.141940359556614" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(101.45,0) translate(0,-80.15)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -5.72 4.78" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -7.44 -0.48" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 33.80335893178359 -58.01093055447454 L 101.44451305515558 -80.1501096622799" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 101.40999234100423 -80.02965665051629 L 101.44451305515558 -80.1501096622799" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(234.05,0) translate(0,-46.94) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.94em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.32em;font-size:10pt;" height="17.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 13.04)" width="82.89"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S19.Ex14.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(85.92,0) translate(0,-93.88) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.94em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.34em;font-size:10pt;" height="17.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 13.04)" width="106.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S19.Ex14.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{B_{d_{1}}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\times\partial^{d_{2}d^{\prime}}B_{d_{2}+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{B_{d_{1}}^{{\mathbb{B}}}\times\partial^{d_{2}d^{\prime}}B_{d_{2}+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 183.24973788749006 -80.1501096622799 L 183.24973788749006 -80.1501096622799" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(244.76,0) translate(0,-58.96)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -7.44 0.36" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -5.64 -4.87" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 183.24973788749006 -80.1501096622799 L 244.75785959174362 -58.958064937420005" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 244.75785959174362 -58.958064937420005 L 244.75785959174362 -58.958064937420005" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the two arrows on the left are already rigidified. Consider</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}=Q_{d_{1}+d_{2}+d^{\prime}}/Q_{d_{1}}\oplus Q_{d_{2}}\oplus Q_{d^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}=Q_{d_{1}+d_{2}+d^{\prime}}/Q_{d_{1}}\oplus Q_{d_{2}}\oplus Q_{d^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">The rigidification on this lower stratum should be a germ of maps</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}:\Big(B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\Big)\times Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}\to\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝔹</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔹</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}:\Big(B_{d_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}}\times B_{d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}\Big)\times Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}\to\partial^{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}B_{d+d^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{B}}\circ{\mathbb{B}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that there is a natural inclusion</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{d_{1}d_{2}}\oplus Q_{d_{2}d^{\prime}}\subseteq Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{d_{1}d_{2}}\oplus Q_{d_{2}d^{\prime}}\subseteq Q_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.6.1" class="ltx_text">where the value of the rigidification is already determined. One then only has to extend it to the complement. Choosing such an extension equivariantly, one hence obtains the rigidification <math id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{d_{1};d_{2};d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> on this lower stratum.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that this lower stratum is shared by two top strata. One uses the collar regions in both directions to extend to a rigidification for both separately.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S19.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">19.1.3. </span>Gluing of domains</h5>

<div id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose we are in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationh1" title="Situation Homotopy(i). ‣ 11.1. Homotopies from varying Floer data ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Homotopy(i)</span></a>. Suppose on the family of domains <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) one chooses a smooth family of lateral lines. Here <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the broken domain. Then without making further choices, one has the monotone flow categories</p>
<table id="S19.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}},\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{n}},\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}},\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{G}}_{1}},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{n}},\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">monotone multimodules</p>
<table id="S19.EGx66" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S19.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As demonstrated above, the concatenation <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> within the category <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically defined. Moreover, by varying <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, one also obtains a monotone homotopy <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>ℍ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the concatenation to <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the multimodule associated to <math id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">What we do below is the construction of the further choices such as rigidifications and stable complex structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S19.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 19.3</span></span><span id="S19.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S19.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix an outercollaring width. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{G}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in Proposition <a href="#S19.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 19.2. ‣ 19.1.2. Concatenations of domains ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.2</span></a>. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a monotone multimodule associated to the smooth domain <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for the outercollaring <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℍ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a lift to <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a monotone homotopy from <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕐</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}\circ_{i}{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Sigma^{\mathbb{Y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Again, it only remains to construct the rigidifications and stable complex structures. The construction of rigidification is still based on the same induction principle. Then stable complex structure can be chosen after choosing a rigidification.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S19.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">19.2. </span>Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing</h4>

<div id="S19.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we treat the topological AMS construction for concatenations of Floer multimodules associated to smooth Floer domains.</p>
</div>
<section id="S19.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">19.2.1. </span>Concatenation</h5>

<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume we are in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationc1" title="Situation Concatenation(i). ‣ 10.4. Concatenations ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Concatenation(i)</span></a>. We have constructed relatively smooth AMS lifts <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. The purpose of this subsection is to demonstrate how to extend the constructions of <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a lift of the concatenation <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S19.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 19.4</span></span><span id="S19.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S19.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationc1" title="Situation Concatenation(i). ‣ 10.4. Concatenations ‣ 10. Geometric Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Concatenation(i)</span></a>, let <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be relatively smooth AMS lifts of the outercollarings of <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, subject to the same outercollaring width and integral actions which induce the same integral symplectic form on <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a set of concatenations <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S19.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">As in Subsection <a href="#S19.SS1.SSS1" title="19.1.1. Concatenations of monotone multimodules ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.1.1</span></a>, we only describe the construction in the case of concatenations of bimodules. The case of multimodules differs only in notation. Denote <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}={\mathbb{B}}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}={\mathbb{B}}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}={\mathbb{B}}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}={\mathbb{B}}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bimodule over <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{0};{\mathbb{F}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{0};{\mathbb{F}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bimodule over <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1};\mathbb{F}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1};\mathbb{F}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Floer flow category associated to Hamiltonian <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the broken domain with two cylinders equipped with Floer datum <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> from the concatenation of the two Floer data for <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">First consider the concatenation of domains. The AMS constructions of <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{0},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{0},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>01</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> start with choosing monotone flow categories <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{0},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{1},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{0},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{1},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> as considered in Section <a href="#S14" title="14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, as well as monotone bimodules <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{01},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>01</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{01},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Then Proposition <a href="#S19.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 19.2. ‣ 19.1.2. Concatenations of domains ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.2</span></a> allows us to choose a concatenation <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{01}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{01}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> as a monotone bimodule over <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{0};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msub><mi>𝕞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msub><mi>𝕞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{0};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now using the chosen integral actions, one can obtain pullbacks</span></p>
<table id="S19.EGx67" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S19.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}},i=0,1,2;\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}},i=0,1,2;\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{12}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}},\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{12}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">as ordinary flow categories and bimodules. The corresponding bimodules of framed maps are also obtained</span></p>
<table id="S19.EGx68" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S19.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{01}}^{\rm fr},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{01}}^{\rm fr},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S19.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{12}}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{12}}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">with a concatenation</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{01}}^{\rm fr}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{12}}^{\rm fr}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{01}}^{\rm fr}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}_{12}}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">canonically determined by the concatenation <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}}\circ{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We need to show that the two further systems of choices, i.e., group reductions and thickening data, can be extended to the concatenation.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first explain why the group reduction can be naturally extended. Indeed, given a triple of objects <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0},p_{1},p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0},p_{1},p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the three involved flow categories, one has the morphism</span></p>
<table id="S19.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{{\rm fr}}\times{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}\to{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{{\rm fr}}\times{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}\to{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">in the category of framed maps. One can see that if we choose any strict embedding representing the conjugacy class, then the strict embedding induces an open embedding onto the closure of a top stratum</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{2}}\times_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{1}}\times\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{1};p_{2}}}\Big({}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{{\rm fr}}\times{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}\Big)\to{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Map</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mi>fr</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Map</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>fr</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Map</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>fr</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{2}}\times_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{1}}\times\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{1};p_{2}}}\Big({}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{{\rm fr}}\times{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}\Big)\to{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{{\rm fr}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">(recall <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}={\mathcal{G}}_{L}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}={\mathcal{G}}_{L}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by reparametrizing the maps and frames while <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by linearly rearranging the frames). As the group reduction is invariant under the first group action and equivariant under the second action, the open embedding naturally induces an extension of the group reduction. One can check that the extension is well-defined on fake boundaries of <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math> (which are adjacent to two such top strata).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The extension of the thickening datum is also canonical in a neighborhood of the morphism (<a href="#S19.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 19.2.1. Concatenation ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.2</span></a>), because the embedding is rigidified. Using the rigidification of this embedding, we can extend the thickening datum in a way which is constant in each normal fiber specified by the rigidification. As one can always shrink the resulting charts, up to shrinking, the extension of the thickening datum is canonically extended.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then one can write down the thickenings in the same way as for a multimodule with a smooth domain. We can verify easily that one obtains a topological Kuranishi lift <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is also a concatenation <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{01}\circ\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{01}\circ\hat{\mathbb{B}}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> within the category <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. The relative smooth structure is canonically given and one can also easily see that the concatenation is indeed in <math id="S19.SS2.SSS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S19.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">19.2.2. </span>Gluing</h5>

<div id="S19.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S19.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 19.5</span></span><span id="S19.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S19.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In Situation <a href="#Thmsituationg1" title="Situation Glue(i). ‣ 11.2. Homotopies from gluing ‣ 11. Homotopies of Multimodules ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Glue(i)</span></a>, fix an outercollaring width and integral actions on the involved flow categories compatible with respect to <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be relatively smooth AMS lifts of <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to the outercollaring width and the integral actions. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a concatenation provided by Theorem <a href="#S19.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 19.4. ‣ 19.2.1. Concatenation ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.4</span></a>. Let <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an AMS lift of the multimodule <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a set of lifts of the outercollaring of the homotopy <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> as a homotopy from <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">At this stage, it follows from the same strategy as the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma17" title="Theorem Q. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Q</span></a>. The choices of rigidifications of domains are provided by Proposition <a href="#S19.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 19.3. ‣ 19.1.3. Gluing of domains ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.3</span></a>. The choices of group reductions and thickening datum can also be interpolated over the homotopy. The relative smooth structures are canonically obtained.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S19.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">19.3. </span>Normal complex structure and stable smoothing</h4>

<div id="S19.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we sketch the discussion on normal complex structures and stable smoothing of the objects arising in concatenation and gluing, which will finish the proof of Theorem <a href="#S12.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 12.4. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.4</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmthma18" title="Theorem R. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">R</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S12.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 12.4. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We continue after Theorem <a href="#S19.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 19.4. ‣ 19.2.1. Concatenation ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.4</span></a>. Suppose the relatively smooth AMS lifts <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the multimodules <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> using the construction of Section <a href="#S16" title="16. Vertical Normal Complex Structures and orientations ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. We would like to see if the composition <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> provided by Theorem <a href="#S19.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 19.4. ‣ 19.2.1. Concatenation ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.4</span></a> can also be lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> as a composition. In fact, the vertical tangent bundles contained in the composition <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are canonically determined by the corresponding vertical tangent bundles. Hence the vertical NC structures are canonically induced. Therefore, we see that <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The stable smoothing of the concatenation is also automatically induced. We briefly explain for the bimodule case. Let <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{0},{\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{0},{\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S19.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 19.4. ‣ 19.2.1. Concatenation ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.4</span></a>. For each triple of objects <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0},p_{1},p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0},p_{1},p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has the embedding</span></p>
<table id="S19.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S19.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}}\times K_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}\to K_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}\circ\mathbb{B}_{12}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}}\times K_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}\to K_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}\circ\mathbb{B}_{12}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S19.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The difference essentially lies in the difference between the complex Lie groups <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{1}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{1};p_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{1}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{1};p_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{p_{0};p_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In terms of Kuranishi charts, the unitary part of the difference is realized by changing the symmetry groups while the non-unitary part of the difference is realized by having a further stabilization. Therefore, if both <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p_{0};p_{1}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p_{1};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{12}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with a (stable) smoothing, then the corresponding top stratum of <math id="S19.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}\circ\mathbb{B}_{12}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p_{0};p_{2}}^{\mathbb{B}_{01}\circ\mathbb{B}_{12}}</annotation></semantics></math> has an induced stable smoothing (possibly after doing further stabilizations).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Lastly, we combine the stable complex structures of the domain moduli space discussed in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S19.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 19.2. ‣ 19.1.2. Concatenations of domains ‣ 19.1. Concatenation and gluing of domains ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.2</span></a>, the NC structures on the vertical tangent bundles to obtain an NC structure on the quotient derived orbifolds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma18" title="Theorem R. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">R</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S19.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We continue after Theorem <a href="#S19.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 19.5. ‣ 19.2.2. Gluing ‣ 19.2. Relatively smooth AMS lifts for concatenations and gluing ‣ 19. Concatenation and Gluing—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19.5</span></a>. Suppose the relatively smooth AMS lifts <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> are lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose we also have a concatenation <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}\circ_{i}\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. The homotopy <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. We first can lift <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S19.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be done by using the same argument as using a family of Cauchy–Riemann operators interpolating between the linearization of the Floer equation and a complex-linear one. We omit the details, which would be a tedious repetition of the flow category and multimodule case already considered.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S19.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S19.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, suppose <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{1},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{G}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{G}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> are also lifted to <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> after further stabilization and smoothing. The stable smoothing and NC structures of <math id="S19.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be constructed using the same inductive argument as we always did. The result is obtained by passing to the orbifold quotient.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S20" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">20. </span>Comparing Non-homotopic Choices</h3>

<div id="S20.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The comparison between different choices which are not homotopic requires more setup. Choices in this category include</p>
<ol id="S20.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S20.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S20.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of integral actions (see Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm1" title="Definition 15.1. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.1</span></a>) in construction the AMS lifts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S20.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S20.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Different choices of lateral lines in Floer domains.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S20.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remove the dependence on these two choices uniformly via a doubling construction (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Section 6.10]</cite>). Here, different integral actions give rise to different moduli spaces of curves of projective spaces; as for different choices of lateral lines under the same choice of integral actions, they induce different composition laws. Despite the different effects, we can handle them in the same way.</p>
</div>
<section id="S20.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">20.1. </span>Curves in products of projective spaces</h4>

<div id="S20.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can generalize the notion of monotone flow categories/multimodules/homotopies (see Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm1" title="Definition 14.1. ‣ 14.1.1. Monotone flow category ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.1</span></a>, Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm4" title="Definition 14.4. ‣ 14.1.2. Monotone flow multimodules and homotopies ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.4</span></a>, Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm5" title="Definition 14.5. ‣ 14.1.2. Monotone flow multimodules and homotopies ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.5</span></a>) in the following way. Fix <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the monoid <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for each <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf d}=(d^{1},\ldots,d^{k})\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf d}=(d^{1},\ldots,d^{k})\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a natural poset</p>
<table id="S20.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{\bf d}=\Big\{({\bf d}_{0},\ldots,{\bf d}_{l})\ |\ {\bf d}_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\setminus\{0\},\ {\bf d}={\bf d}_{0}+\cdots+{\bf d}_{l}\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\bf d}=\Big\{({\bf d}_{0},\ldots,{\bf d}_{l})\ |\ {\bf d}_{i}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}\setminus\{0\},\ {\bf d}={\bf d}_{0}+\cdots+{\bf d}_{l}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S20.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">monotone flow category of rank <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> enriched in <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{f}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of a collection of <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math>-stratified objects</p>
<table id="S20.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\bf d}^{\mathbb{f}},\ \forall{\bf d}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>𝕗</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝐝</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\bf d}^{\mathbb{f}},\ \forall{\bf d}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and a collection of morphisms</p>
<table id="S20.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{{\bf d}_{1}}^{\mathbb{f}}\times M_{{\bf d}_{2}}^{\mathbb{f}}\to\partial^{{\bf d}_{1},{\bf d}_{2}}M_{{\bf d}_{1}+{\bf d}_{2}}^{\mathbb{f}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>𝕗</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>𝕗</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>𝕗</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{{\bf d}_{1}}^{\mathbb{f}}\times M_{{\bf d}_{2}}^{\mathbb{f}}\to\partial^{{\bf d}_{1},{\bf d}_{2}}M_{{\bf d}_{1}+{\bf d}_{2}}^{\mathbb{f}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which satisfy the natural associativity condition, similar to that imposed in Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm1" title="Definition 14.1. ‣ 14.1.1. Monotone flow category ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.1</span></a>. One can see any monotone flow category <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> induces an ordinary flow category <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S20.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\cong{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\cong{\mathbb{Z}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, one can define monotone multimodules and homotopies of higher ranks as we did in Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm4" title="Definition 14.4. ‣ 14.1.2. Monotone flow multimodules and homotopies ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.4</span></a> and Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm5" title="Definition 14.5. ‣ 14.1.2. Monotone flow multimodules and homotopies ‣ 14.1. Monotone flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.5</span></a>: we leave it to the interested reader to spell out the details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we would like to build rank <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> monotone flow category <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, for <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf d}=(d^{1},\ldots,d^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐝</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf d}=(d^{1},\ldots,d^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S20.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{\bf d}={\mathbb{P}}^{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{k}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐝</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{\bf d}={\mathbb{P}}^{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Each element is an equivalence class of maps with <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> components. Indeed, one can consider the moduli space <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(\mathbb{P}^{\bf d},{\bf d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(\mathbb{P}^{\bf d},{\bf d})</annotation></semantics></math> of cylinders in <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math> of multidegree <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐝</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math> and the open subset</p>
<table id="S20.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{\bf d}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(\mathbb{P}^{\bf d},{\bf d})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐝</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\bf d}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{0,2}^{\mathbb{R}}(\mathbb{P}^{\bf d},{\bf d})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">of cylinders satisfying 1) the evaluations of the <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th component at <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\cdots,0,1]\in\mathbb{P}^{d^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\cdots,0,1]\in\mathbb{P}^{d^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and 2) the image of the <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th component is not contained in any hyperplanes of <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math>-stratified manifold with a smooth action by the complex Lie group</p>
<table id="S20.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\bf d}:={\mathcal{G}}_{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\bf d}:={\mathcal{G}}_{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which contains a maximal compact subgroup</p>
<table id="S20.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G_{\bf d}:=G_{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times G_{d^{k}}\cong U(d^{1})\times\cdots\times U(d^{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\bf d}:=G_{d^{1}}\times\cdots\times G_{d^{k}}\cong U(d^{1})\times\cdots\times U(d^{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Similar to the rank 1 case, one obtains a collection of objects</p>
<table id="S20.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{\bf d}=({\mathcal{G}}_{\bf d},B_{\bf d},C_{\bf d})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{\bf d}=({\mathcal{G}}_{\bf d},B_{\bf d},C_{\bf d})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S20.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\bf d}\to B_{\bf d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>𝐝</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\bf d}\to B_{\bf d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the universal curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One can define similar structural maps to obtain a monotone flow category, denoted by</p>
<table id="S20.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for any smooth Floer domain <math id="S20.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a monotone multimodule <math id="S20.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{k,\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝕏</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{k,\Sigma^{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S20.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Dom}^{k},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{k};\mathbb{Dom}^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Dom}^{k},\ldots,\mathbb{Dom}^{k};\mathbb{Dom}^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A slight difference from the previous rank <math id="S20.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> case is that we choose not to construct rigidifications and stable complex structures at this stage (although we could do so). We would like to do the construction later after pulling back so that certain compatibility holds directly from our construction. At this point, we only take outercollarings and the monotone flow categories/multimodules are enriched in <math id="S20.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S20.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">20.2. </span>Double-framed thickenings</h4>

<div id="S20.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We may carry out the roadmap of the AMS construction for the higher-rank approach which facilitates the comparison of different integral actions. We only need to consider the <math id="S20.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> case and call such a construction a “double-framed” AMS construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly go through the construction for Floer flow categories. Assume one is in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationf1" title="Situation Flow(i). ‣ 9.2. Floer flow category ‣ 9. Geometric Flow Categories ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Flow(i)</span></a>. Fix an outercollaring width. Let</p>
<table id="S20.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}},\ {\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{2}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}},\ {\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{2}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">be two integral actions on <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote their direct sum by</p>
<table id="S20.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Then for each pair <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> of objects of <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S20.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf d}_{pq}=(d_{pq}^{1},d_{pq}^{2})={\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}(p)-{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}(q)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf d}_{pq}=(d_{pq}^{1},d_{pq}^{2})={\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}(p)-{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2}}(q)\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Then for each pair <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S20.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}=({\mathcal{G}}_{pq},B_{pq},C_{pq})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}=({\mathcal{G}}_{pq},B_{pq},C_{pq}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">be the restriction of <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{{\bf d}_{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{{\bf d}_{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the strata whose associated degrees can be realized by integralized actions of broken Floer trajectories of <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. This provides a pullback</p>
<table id="S20.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">which is a flow category enriched in <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are those of <math id="S20.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One can write down the associated flow category of framed maps in the same way as the rank <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> case. More precisely, given objects <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of framed maps consists of tuples <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,F)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth map satisfying the exponential decay condition, and <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=(F_{1},F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=(F_{1},F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of two frames where <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, called an <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-frame along <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, is a frame of the holomorphic line bundle <math id="S20.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{u,\Omega_{i}}\to C_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{u,\Omega_{i}}\to C_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the positive constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following fact is important for the comparison. For each <math id="S20.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="S20.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}={\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{1}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}^{1}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}={\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{1}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}^{1}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S20.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S20.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 20.1</span></span><span id="S20.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S20.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a <math id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant principal <math id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S20.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Abbreviate</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d^{i}={\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}(p)-{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}(q),\ i=1,2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{i}={\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}(p)-{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}(q),\ i=1,2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Each element of <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a holomorphic map <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{1},u^{2}):C_{\phi}\to\mathbb{P}^{d^{1}}\times\mathbb{P}^{d^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{1},u^{2}):C_{\phi}\to\mathbb{P}^{d^{1}}\times\mathbb{P}^{d^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is an obvious free <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{d^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> by linearly transforming the second component. Moreover, fix <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{1}\in B_{pq}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{1}\in B_{pq}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, all holomorphic maps into <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> which have the prescribed degrees on each irreducible component are in the same free <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. They come from projective embeddings of the irreducible components into <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{d^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by a basis of global sections of the line bundle with prescribed degree, with matching condition on the nodes. This amounts to looking at all framings with positive evaluation constraint, thereby producing <math id="S20.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the natural projection</p>
<table id="S20.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{i}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}\to{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}^{i},\ i=1,2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}\to{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}^{i},\ i=1,2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">We will use these projections to pull back all choices made for the rank 1 construction to the rank 2 case and take direct sums.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S20.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 20.2</span></span><span id="S20.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S20.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The lifts of <math id="S20.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{i},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{i},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S20.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> canonically induce a lift of <math id="S20.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm9" title="Definition 14.9. ‣ 14.2.2. Rigidifications ‣ 14.2. The category of curves ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.9</span></a>, the rigidification for <math id="S20.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{i},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{i},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by maps</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\theta_{prq}^{i}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}^{i}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}^{i}\times Q_{prq}^{i}\to{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{prq}^{i}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}^{i}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}^{i}\times Q_{prq}^{i}\to{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">satisfying certain conditions. One can simply pullback <math id="S20.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{prq}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{prq}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S20.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, define</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\theta_{prq}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}\to&amp;\ Q_{prq}\\
(\phi_{pr},\phi_{rq})\mapsto&amp;\ \Big(\theta_{prq}^{1}(\pi_{1}(\phi_{pr}),\pi_{1}(\phi_{rq})),\theta_{prq}^{2}(\pi_{2}(\phi_{pr}),\pi_{2}(\phi_{rq}))\Big).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\theta_{prq}:{}_{\lfloor}B_{pr}\times{}_{\lfloor}B_{rq}\to&amp;\ Q_{prq}\\
(\phi_{pr},\phi_{rq})\mapsto&amp;\ \Big(\theta_{prq}^{1}(\pi_{1}(\phi_{pr}),\pi_{1}(\phi_{rq})),\theta_{prq}^{2}(\pi_{2}(\phi_{pr}),\pi_{2}(\phi_{rq}))\Big).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">These maps define a rigidification of <math id="S20.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, the stable complex structure can also be obtained by using such pullbacks, whose compatibility can be checked easily.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now we assume that <math id="S20.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is enriched in <math id="S20.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Of course, such a lift does not necessarily come from pullbacks, but for our purpose, it suffices to consider this special situation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for a flow multimodule <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{1},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}}_{m};{\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>, if each of the flow categories are equipped with integral actions for <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S20.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}^{\Omega_{i}},\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}}^{\Omega_{i}},{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}^{\Omega_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}^{\Omega_{i}},\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}}^{\Omega_{i}},{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}^{\Omega_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">we obtain a multimodule <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}_{m}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔻</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕠</mi><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}_{1}},\ldots,{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}_{m}};{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S20.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">20.3. </span>Flow category situation</h4>

<div id="S20.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We spell out the comparison between different integral actions on the same flow category in detail.</p>
</div>
<section id="S20.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">20.3.1. </span>Framed maps and group reductions</h5>

<div id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can write down the flow category of framed maps</p>
<table id="S20.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}^{\mathbb{fr}}_{\mathbb{F}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}^{\mathbb{fr}}_{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which assigns to each pair <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> a space <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of triples <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,F)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi\in{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a component-wise smooth map which extends continuously over nodes and which converges exponentially fast to periodic orbits, and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=(F_{1},F_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=(F_{1},F_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is a double-frame where <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a frame of the holomorphic line bundle <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{u,\Omega_{i}}\to C_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{u,\Omega_{i}}\to C_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the pre-chosen positive or negative constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that there are still natural projection maps</p>
<table id="S20.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\pi_{i}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to&amp;\ {}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq,i}^{\rm fr}\\
(\phi,u,F)\mapsto&amp;(\pi_{i}(\phi),u,F_{i}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\pi_{i}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to&amp;\ {}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq,i}^{\rm fr}\\
(\phi,u,F)\mapsto&amp;(\pi_{i}(\phi),u,F_{i}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now denote</p>
<table id="S20.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}={\mathcal{G}}_{L,pq}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{R,pq}\cong{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}={\mathcal{G}}_{L,pq}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{R,pq}\cong{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">It acts on <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math> as before, where <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{L,pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{L,pq}</annotation></semantics></math> reparametrizes the maps and the frames, while <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{R,pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{R,pq}</annotation></semantics></math> linearly transforms the frames. Recall that a group reduction (Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm7" title="Definition 15.7 (The category of families of reduced framed maps). ‣ 15.2.2. Group reductions ‣ 15.2. Framed maps ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.7</span></a>) consists of a compatible system of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant maps</p>
<table id="S20.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Lambda_{pq}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}/G_{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{pq}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}/G_{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">If we have chosen independently group reductions for <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}^{\mathbb{fr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{{\mathbb{F}}_{i}}^{\mathbb{fr}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then using the projection map (<a href="#S20.E1" title="In 20.3.1. Framed maps and group reductions ‣ 20.3. Flow category situation ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.1</span></a>), we can pull them back to obtain a group reduction on the double-framed construction <math id="S20.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\mathbb{fr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\mathbb{fr}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S20.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">20.3.2. </span>Thickening data</h5>

<div id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The last step of constructing the topological AMS lift is to choose transverse thickening data. By slightly generalizing Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm10" title="Definition 15.10. ‣ 15.3.1. Thickening data ‣ 15.3. The topological Kuranishi lifts ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.10</span></a>, a thickening datum on the flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a collection of unitary <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant linear maps</p>
<table id="S20.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{pq}:W_{pq}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{pq}:W_{pq}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying the natural compatibility condition with the structural maps. With or without transversality condition, one can write down the thickening (cf. Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm14" title="Definition 15.14. ‣ 15.3.2. The thickenings ‣ 15.3. The topological Kuranishi lifts ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.14</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S20.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{pq}=\Big\{(\phi,u,e,F)\ |\ (\phi,u,F)\in{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr},e\in W_{pq},\ \phi_{F}=\phi\in{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq},\ \overline{\partial}_{H}u+\nu_{pq}(e)=0\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Map</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>fr</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}=\Big\{(\phi,u,e,F)\ |\ (\phi,u,F)\in{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr},e\in W_{pq},\ \phi_{F}=\phi\in{}_{\lfloor}B_{pq},\ \overline{\partial}_{H}u+\nu_{pq}(e)=0\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The obstruction bundle <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is still the direct sum of the trivial bundles <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{pq}\oplus Q_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{pq}\oplus Q_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Kuranishi section <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{pq}:V_{pq}\to E_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{pq}:V_{pq}\to E_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of the (linearized) group reduction <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and the forgetful map <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,e,F)\mapsto e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,e,F)\mapsto e</annotation></semantics></math>. There is the natural footprint map</p>
<table id="S20.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{pq}^{-1}(0)/G_{pq}\to{}_{\lfloor}M_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{pq}^{-1}(0)/G_{pq}\to{}_{\lfloor}M_{pq}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">sending the <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,e,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,e,F)</annotation></semantics></math> to the equivalence class of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then suppose one has chosen independently thickening datum</p>
<table id="S20.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nu_{pq}^{i}:W_{pq}^{i}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}^{i}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}^{i}/B_{pq,i}}^{0,1}\otimes TX),\ i=1,2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{pq}^{i}:W_{pq}^{i}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}^{i}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}^{i}/B_{pq,i}}^{0,1}\otimes TX),\ i=1,2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">then one can pullback each of them</p>
<table id="S20.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i})^{*}\nu_{pq}^{i}:W_{pq}^{i}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i})^{*}\nu_{pq}^{i}:W_{pq}^{i}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S20.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S20.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 20.3</span></span><span id="S20.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S20.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S20.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{pq}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{pq}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse, so is <math id="S20.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}^{*}\nu_{pq}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}^{*}\nu_{pq}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from the observation that if <math id="S20.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{pq}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{pq}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped surjectively onto the cokernel of the linearized Cauchy–Riemann operator, so is the pullback.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S20.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">20.3.3. </span>Finalizing proof</h5>

<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we would like to compare the two lifts <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a rank 1 construction and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> using a rank 2 construction where the thickening datum is pulled back from the choices made for <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the notion of free quotient of Kuranishi spaces (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Definition 3.11. ‣ 3.1.2. Free quotients ‣ 3.1. Kuranishi spaces ‣ 3. Kuranishi Spaces ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S20.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 20.4</span></span><span id="S20.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S20.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> be a flow category enriched in <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}</annotation></semantics></math> with morphism space</p>
<table id="S20.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{pq}^{\sim}=(G_{pq}^{\sim},V_{pq}^{\sim},E_{pq}^{\sim},S_{pq}^{\sim})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{pq}^{\sim}=(G_{pq}^{\sim},V_{pq}^{\sim},E_{pq}^{\sim},S_{pq}^{\sim}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">free quotient</span> of <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of for each pair of objects <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> a decomposition</p>
<table id="S20.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{\sim}=G_{pq}\times G_{pq}^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{\sim}=G_{pq}\times G_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">of compact Lie groups satisfying 1) the conjugacy class of group embeddings <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pr}^{\sim}\times G_{rq}^{\sim}\to G_{pq}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pr}^{\sim}\times G_{rq}^{\sim}\to G_{pq}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> respect the decomposition and 2) <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{pq}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{pq}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that one obtains a new flow category <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> with morphism spaces <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> being the free quotient of <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{pq}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{pq}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmthm11" title="Definition 3.11. ‣ 3.1.2. Free quotients ‣ 3.1. Kuranishi spaces ‣ 3. Kuranishi Spaces ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>). By abuse of notation, we also call the resulting flow category consisting of these free quotients a free quotient of <math id="S20.Thmthm4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that in the above definition, if <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> is enriched in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the resulting flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by taking the quotient by the full group is isomorphic to the one obtained from the free quotient obtained by taking the residual group action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S20.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 20.5</span></span><span id="S20.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S20.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume we are in Situation <a href="#Thmsituationf1" title="Situation Flow(i). ‣ 9.2. Floer flow category ‣ 9. Geometric Flow Categories ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Flow(i)</span></a>. Fix an outercollaring width. Let <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> be two integral actions. Let <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an AMS lift of <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> (of rank <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to an outercollaring width and <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then there exists a rank 2 AMS lift <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> (subject to the same outercollaring width and the rank 2 integral action) and another rank <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> lift of the outercollaring <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that 1) <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a free quotient of <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and 2) <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a stabilization of <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same applies to an AMS lift <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to an outercollaring width and <math id="S20.Thmthm5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We provide a construction that works equally well for both <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>: their relations with our <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> are completely symmetric. We will focus on explaining the situation for <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall the roadmap of constructing <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, consider the flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S20.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 20.1. ‣ 20.2. Double-framed thickenings ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.1</span></a>, there is another flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose corresponding moduli spaces are principal bundles over those in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1},{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1},{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally induced from it in the following way. The differences between <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in: 1) the compact Lie group associated to objects <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{1}_{pq}\times G^{2}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{1}_{pq}\times G^{2}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>; 2) the space <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{1}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{1}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by a principal <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{2}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{2}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{1}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{1}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a subspace of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> discussed in Lemma <a href="#S20.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 20.3. ‣ 20.3.2. Thickening data ‣ 20.3. Flow category situation ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.3</span></a> where we replace each fiber of the principal <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{2}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{2}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}\to B^{1}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}\to B^{1}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> by the maximal compact subgroup, i.e., looking at the unitary framings on the second factor; 3) the obstruction bundle and Kuranishi section are pulled back to the principal <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{2}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{2}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle constructed in 2). By construction, we see that <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the free quotient of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the other hand, we define <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> taking full advantage of the double framing. This time, along each map <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> there are two sets of frames, one for the line bundle induced from <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other for the line bundle induced from <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The group reduction chosen for <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to this “double-framed” version. This time, we consider the total space <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, acted on by <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}=G^{1}_{pq}\times G^{2}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}=G^{1}_{pq}\times G^{2}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. As for the obstruction bundle, we first pull back the thickening data <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}^{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}^{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}^{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}^{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is still transverse by Lemma <a href="#S20.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 20.3. ‣ 20.3.2. Thickening data ‣ 20.3. Flow category situation ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.3</span></a>, then we take the direct sum with <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the obstruction bundle is</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{W}}_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}\oplus\underline{\smash{W}}_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{2}}\oplus\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{1}\oplus\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{W}}_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}\oplus\underline{\smash{W}}_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{2}}\oplus\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{1}\oplus\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{Q}}_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-component of the Kuranishi section is induced from the group reduction for the <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-frames. Therefore, in the category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>, the statement of Proposition <a href="#S20.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 20.5. ‣ 20.3.3. Finalizing proof ‣ 20.3. Flow category situation ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.5</span></a> holds, where we construct the rigidification by pullback.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">To complete the construction of <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>, one needs to extend the statement to the category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that the pullback is induced from maps between domain moduli. Hence the construction of the relative NC structure for <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be canonically lifted to <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>. To extend the smoothing, notice that for each <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, the ambient space associated with <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a topological <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{pq}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{pq}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-principal bundle over the ambient space associated with <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can always use the group actions to construct smooth bundle structures inductively, hence <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is smoothed. Then using the stabilization structure between <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can induce a canonical smooth structure on <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the statement is also true within the category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. Lastly, the NC structure construction is quite elementary and can also be respected.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S12.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 12.2. ‣ 12.1. Main statements for Floer flow categories ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using the notations introduced in Proposition <a href="#S20.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 20.5. ‣ 20.3.3. Finalizing proof ‣ 20.3. Flow category situation ‣ 20. Comparing Non-homotopic Choices ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20.5</span></a>, recall that the flow categories <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained as quotients of the flow categories <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1},\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can use the intermediate flow category <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\sim}</annotation></semantics></math> to provide the desired construction. Indeed, recall that the Floer flow category requires passing to the quotient under the <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>-translation action if the energy is strictly greater than <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain the lift of the diagonal bimodule <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Delta}^{\mathbb{FF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the normally complex Kuranishi spaces before taking the quotient. By passing to the orbifold quotient, we finish the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In fact we can also prove that given a pair of integral actions <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\mathbb{F}}^{\Omega_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, the double-framed construction together with FOP perturbation produces a chain complex <math id="S20.SS3.SSS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C({\mathbb{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C({\mathbb{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is well-defined up to homotopy given by a continuation map also defined via a double-framed construction. The proof of Proposition <a href="#S12.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 12.2. ‣ 12.1. Main statements for Floer flow categories ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.2</span></a> can be viewed as a special case of this fact.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S20.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">20.4. </span>Multimodules</h4>

<div id="S20.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with flow categories, for multimodules and homotopies, the lateral lines specified in Definition <a href="#S14.Thmthm8" title="Definition 14.8. ‣ 14.2.1. Lateral lines ‣ 14.2. The category of curves ‣ 14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14.8</span></a> may not be homotopically unique. Note that under the double-frame construction, we need to use the lateral lines from two different versions simultaneously to define concatenation maps of the framings. They exactly facilitate the construction of the doubly-thickened moduli spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S12.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 12.3. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S20.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We only present the proof in the first situation; the second situation (the Poincaré duality multimodule) only differs in notations. Fix an outercollaring width. For any involved flow category <math id="S20.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the double-framed construction produces AMS lifts <math id="S20.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\rm double}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>double</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{\rm double}</annotation></semantics></math> (to <math id="S20.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>), which depends on choices made in the construction. Two particular choices as described above are the “pullbacks” from each single-framed versions, denoted by</span></p>
<table id="S20.EGx69" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S20.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S20.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}_{(0)}^{{\rm double}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}_{(0)}^{{\rm double}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S20.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}_{(1)}^{{\rm double}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}_{(1)}^{{\rm double}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now turn to the multimodule <math id="S20.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from <math id="S20.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\Omega_{1}\oplus\Omega_{2},{\mathbb{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any collection of double-framed AMS lifts</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\rm double},\ \ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}^{\rm double},\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mi>double</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\rm double},\ \ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}^{\rm double},\ \hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">then one can construct a double-framed AMS lift</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>double</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">as a multimodule over <math id="S20.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\rm double},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}^{\rm double};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mi>double</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{\rm double},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m}^{\rm double};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the construction is compatible with continuation maps connecting different double-framed AMS lifts of each individual flow category.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S20.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S20.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Notice that one has two particular situations of <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>double</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from pulling back the single-framed construction <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}_{(s)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\rm double}_{(s)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>, which are, respectively, multimodules over <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1,(s)}^{{\rm double}},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m,(s)}^{\rm double};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}}_{(s)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>double</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></mmultiscripts></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{1,(s)}^{{\rm double}},\ldots,\hat{\mathbb{F}}_{m,(s)}^{\rm double};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{}^{\prime}{\rm double}}_{(s)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Now let <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}\mathbb{F}_{j}}_{(01)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>01</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{j}\mathbb{F}_{j}}_{(01)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}_{(10)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}_{(10)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the double-framed AMS lift of the diagonal bimodules. Then the double-framed concatenation/gluing produces a homotopy from</span></p>
<table id="S20.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S20.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left(\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}_{(01)}\times\cdots\times\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}{\mathbb{F}}_{m}}_{(01)}\right)\circ\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{(1)}^{\rm double}\circ\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}_{(10)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>01</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>01</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{1}{\mathbb{F}}_{1}}_{(01)}\times\cdots\times\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{m}{\mathbb{F}}_{m}}_{(01)}\right)\circ\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{(1)}^{\rm double}\circ\hat{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}{\mathbb{F}}^{\prime}}_{(10)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S20.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S20.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">to <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{(1)}^{\rm double}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>double</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{(1)}^{\rm double}</annotation></semantics></math>. This requires extending the proof of Theorem <a href="#S12.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 12.4. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.4</span></a> provided in the last section to the double-framed setting, which is nothing more complicated. Passing to the quotient via the functor <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S20.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a homotopy exactly expected in Proposition <a href="#S12.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 12.3. ‣ 12.2. Multimodules and homotopies ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S21" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">21. </span>AMS Construction for Other Flow categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies</h3>

<div id="S21.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S21.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss the AMS construction for flow categories etc. which are not from smooth Floer domains.</p>
</div>
<section id="S21.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">21.1. </span>Pearly flow category</h4>

<div id="S21.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S21.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the Pearly flow category <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated to an almost Kähler manifold <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math> and a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The object set is</p>
<table id="S21.EGx70" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S21.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S21.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm Ob}\mathbb{P}={\rm crit}f\times\Pi\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm Ob}\mathbb{P}={\rm crit}f\times\Pi\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S21.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm where}\ \Pi=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}c_{1}\cap{\rm ker}\omega}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm where}\ \Pi=\frac{\pi_{2}(X)}{{\rm ker}c_{1}\cap{\rm ker}\omega}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S21.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">An object is written as <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(\underline{\smash{x}},a_{x})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}\in{\rm crit}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}\in{\rm crit}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{x}\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{x}\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. We will construct AMS lifts of outercollarings of <math id="S21.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as declared in Theorem <a href="#S12.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 12.7. ‣ 12.3.1. PSS maps and pearly flow category ‣ 12.3. Bimodules and homotopies associated to PSS type maps ‣ 12. Main Theorems on AMS Lifts ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.7</span></a>. The main difference from the case of the Floer flow category is that the boundary strata of moduli spaces are given by fiber products instead of direct products.</p>
</div>
<section id="S21.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">21.1.1. </span>Fibered topological spaces, Kuranishi spaces, and derived orbifolds</h5>

<div id="S21.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S21.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 21.1</span></span><span id="S21.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S21.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S21.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S21.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth manifold (without boundary and corners).</p>
<ol id="S21.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S21.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S21.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S21.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> be the regular stratification category whose objects are pairs <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,f_{-},f_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,f_{-},f_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\pm}:N\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\pm}:N\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair of continuous maps. A morphism in <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{1},f_{1,\pm})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{1},f_{1,\pm})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{2},f_{2,\pm})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{2},f_{2,\pm})</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism of stratified spaces <math id="S21.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:N_{1}\to N_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:N_{1}\to N_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following diagram commutes.</p>
<table id="S21.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S21.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="78.87" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-61.11336097490314px" version="1.1" viewBox="14.04 61.11 97.25 78.87" width="97.25"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 14.04315319829286 78.87081633436246) translate(0,3.46) translate(14.04,0)"><g transform="translate(-13.57,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="18.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S21.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{N_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{N_{1}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 13.567949703855767 -0.27673578005007266 L 13.567949703855767 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(13.01,0) translate(0,8.88) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.6em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="22.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S21.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{f_{1,\pm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.7