t\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CM(f,g)^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 155.46355pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 155.46355pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(C({\mathbb{M}})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 222.06766pt\raise 6.20831pt\hbox{{}\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise-3.20831pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle{\Phi^{\mathbb{QSt}}}$}}}\kern 3.0pt}}}}}}\ignorespaces{\hbox{\kern 245.97054pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\lx@xy@tip{1}\lx@xy@tip{-1}}}}}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\lx@xy@droprule}}{\hbox{\kern 245.97054pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 0.0pt\raise 0.0pt\hbox{\hbox{\kern 3.0pt\raise-2.5pt\hbox{$\textstyle{H(C({\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}}))}$}}}}}}}\ignorespaces}}}}\ignorespaces.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that the quasi-Frobenius map is compatible with continuation maps. To see that the composition is independent of the choice of the Morse–Smale pair <math id="S24.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can concatenate the multimodule <math id="S24.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> with Morse bimodules interpolating different choices of the Morse–Smale pairs, therefore we obtain the following statement using standard ideas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 24.5</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be another Morse flow category on <math id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a different Morse–Smale pair <math id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\prime},g^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\prime},g^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following diagram commutes.</span></p>
<table id="S24.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="68.97" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-57.332867719629945px" version="1.1" viewBox="41.13 57.33 396.02 68.97" width="396.02"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.12903451926154 68.96525048213297) translate(0,3.54) translate(41.13,0)"><g transform="translate(-36.86,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.73em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="65.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{HM^{*}(f,g)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{HM^{*}(f,g)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 36.862633181126334 -0.27673578005007266 L 36.862633181126334 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(76.28,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 36.862633181126334 -0.27673578005007266 L 76.27887021164125 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 76.27887021164125 -0.27673578005007266 L 76.27887021164125 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-33.82)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.823754656474854 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -33.823754656474854 L 0.27673578005007266 -33.823754656474854" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(76.28,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.51em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.35em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="90.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 174.66579810438503 -0.27673578005007266 L 174.66579810438503 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(253.27,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 174.66579810438503 -0.27673578005007266 L 253.2676484585798 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 253.2676484585798 -0.27673578005007266 L 253.2676484585798 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 125.19558782116975 -12.491705738625553 L 125.74905938126989 -12.491705738625553" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(125.47,0) translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 125.19558782116975 -33.20877921850137 L 125.74905938126989 -12.491705738625553" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 125.19558782116975 -33.20877921850137 L 125.74905938126989 -33.20877921850137" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(253.27,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.6em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="91.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}}))\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}}))\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 302.8339466745797 -11.14645356605438 L 303.38741823467984 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(303.11,0) translate(0,-33.82)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 302.8339466745797 -33.823754656474854 L 303.38741823467984 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 302.8339466745797 -33.823754656474854 L 303.38741823467984 -33.823754656474854" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-41.13,0) translate(0,-44.84) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.34em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="73.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{HM^{*}(f^{\prime},g^{\prime})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{HM^{*}(f^{\prime},g^{\prime})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 41.12903451926154 -45.117897761054465 L 41.12903451926154 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(74.34,0) translate(0,-44.84)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 41.12903451926154 -45.117897761054465 L 74.33783485134954 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 74.33783485134954 -45.117897761054465 L 74.33783485134954 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(74.34,0) translate(0,-44.84) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.35em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="93.97"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}^{\prime})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}^{\prime})^{\otimes{\sf p}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 176.6068123510901 -45.117897761054465 L 176.6068123510901 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(251.33,0) translate(0,-44.84)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 176.6068123510901 -45.117897761054465 L 251.3266130982881 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 251.3266130982881 -45.117897761054465 L 251.3266130982881 -44.56442620095432" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(251.33,0) translate(0,-44.84) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.88em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="95.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S24.Ex17.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M}^{\prime}\#{\mathbb{E}}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H^{*}(C^{*}(\mathbb{M}^{\prime}\#{\mathbb{E}}))}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here vertical arrows are isomorphisms induced from continuation maps.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that</p>
<table id="S24.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}}))\cong H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}}))\cong H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\langle\uptheta\rangle).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Then the above lemma shows that, after tensoring with <math id="S24.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a canonical map</p>
<table id="S24.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{{\sf p}}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{{\sf p}}:H^{*}(X;{\bf F}_{\sf p})\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{\widetilde{\bf K}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">By construction, the chain map <math id="S24.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not decrease the <math id="S24.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading, hence it indeed defines a map for each degree <math id="S24.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S24.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{{\sf p}}:H^{k}(X;{\mathbb{F}}_{2})\to\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{2}{[\![\uptheta]\!]}}^{\Pi}),&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}{[\![\mathtt{U}]\!]}}^{\Pi}\langle\uptheta\rangle),&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.158em;" width="0.158em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝚄</mi><mpadded style="width:0.158em;" width="0.158em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{{\sf p}}:H^{k}(X;{\mathbb{F}}_{2})\to\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}H^{2k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{2}{[\![\uptheta]\!]}}^{\Pi}),&amp;\ {\sf p}=2,\\
H^{{\sf p}k}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}{[\![\mathtt{U}]\!]}}^{\Pi}\langle\uptheta\rangle),&amp;\ {\sf p}&gt;2.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">This is called the <span id="S24.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quantum Steenrod operation</span>. The proof of the claim that the classical part of <math id="S24.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which comes from constant holomorphic spheres) coincides with the classical Steenrod operation is the same as the semi-positive case considered by Seidel–Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. This finishes the proof of part (1) of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma8" title="Theorem H (Quantum Steenrod operation on general symplectic manifold). ‣ 1.2.1. The quantum Steenrod operations ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S24.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">24.3. </span>AMS construction</h4>

<div id="S24.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to construct <math id="S24.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant AMS lifts which is also equivariant with respect to the monoid morphism (<a href="#S24.E1" title="In Proposition 24.1. ‣ 24.1.1. The flow categories and bimodules associated to quantum Steenrod operation ‣ 24.1. Geometric setup ‣ 24. Quantum Steenrod Operations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.1</span></a>). It suffices to construct the lift for the corresponding moduli space of <math id="S24.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}+1</annotation></semantics></math>-marked curves, from which the flow multimodule is constructed by taking fiber products with moduli spaces of Morse trajectories.</p>
</div>
<section id="S24.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">24.3.1. </span>The domain moduli</h5>

<div id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first consider the moduli space of domain curves. For each <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, consider</p>
<table id="S24.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}}(\mathbb{CP}^{d},d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}}(\mathbb{CP}^{d},d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">of holomorphic maps from the special marked curve <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{St}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{St}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S24.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}}(\mathbb{CP}^{d},d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal{M}}{}_{\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}}(\mathbb{CP}^{d},d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">be the open subset whose images are not contained in any hyperplane. Then <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=PGL(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=PGL(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open subset. Moreover, there is an action by <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> which permutes the incoming marked points. Then one has the object</p>
<table id="S24.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}=({{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}=({{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times{\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Since this is not stratified, we do not need to consider outercollaring or rigidifications at this moment. We can equip <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant stable complex structure, hence an object of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>ℂ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the complex analytic structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S24.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">24.3.2. </span>The thickenings</h5>

<div id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Choose an integral symplectic form <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> which is tamed by <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the space of maps and framed maps</p>
<table id="S24.EGx77" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S24.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S24.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm Map}_{d}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Map</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm Map}_{d}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S24.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Map</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>fr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">whose elements are <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u)</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,F)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth map satisfying <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int u^{*}\Omega=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int u^{*}\Omega=d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a framing associated to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and the integral symplectic form <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can choose a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant group reduction scheme</p>
<table id="S24.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:{\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X)\to Q_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=T_{1}({\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}/G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Map</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>fr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:{\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X)\to Q_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=T_{1}({\mathcal{G}}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}/G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for example, using the formula (<a href="#S15.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 15.2.6. Group reductions ‣ 15.2. Framed maps ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.6</span></a>). On the other hand, choose a thickening datum</p>
<table id="S24.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\mathring{C}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}/B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}\otimes TX)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\mathring{C}_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}/B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}\otimes TX)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unitary representation of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\cong PU(d+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\cong PU(d+1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivariant with respect to both the <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-action and the <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then one has the thickened moduli space</p>
<table id="S24.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=\left\{(\phi,u,F,e)\ \left|\begin{array}[]{c}(\phi,u,F)\in{\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X),\ e\in W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},\\
\overline{\partial}u+\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(e)=0,\ \phi=\phi_{F}\in B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\end{array}\right.\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=\left\{(\phi,u,F,e)\ \left|\begin{array}[]{c}(\phi,u,F)\in{\rm Map}_{d}^{\rm fr}(C_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},X),\ e\in W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},\\
\overline{\partial}u+\iota_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(e)=0,\ \phi=\phi_{F}\in B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\end{array}\right.\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The obstruction bundle</p>
<table id="S24.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is the trivial bundle with fiber <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\oplus Q_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\oplus Q_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Kuranishi section is</p>
<table id="S24.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\phi,u,F,e)=(e,\lambda_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\phi,u,F))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\phi,u,F,e)=(e,\lambda_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\phi,u,F)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The obtained topological Kuranishi space is denoted by</p>
<table id="S24.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=(G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=(G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">which also has a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action commuting with <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notice that there is a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant evaluation map</p>
<table id="S24.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}:V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X^{{\sf p}+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}:V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X^{{\sf p}+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.6" class="ltx_p">at the markings, where <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the first <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by permutation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One still have the relative smoothness of the forgetful map</p>
<table id="S24.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">One can then also follow the approach of Section <a href="#S16" title="16. Vertical Normal Complex Structures and orientations ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> (which is much simplified here) to equip the vertical tangent bundle an NC structure. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Section 6.2]</cite> for related setups in Gromov–Witten theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since we are working with a single space without boundary or corners, one can apply the smoothing strategy of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> to obtain a stable <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-smoothing of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the stabilization factor needed for the smoothing can be absorbed into the thickening space <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, one can make the evaluation map <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ev</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth submersion at the expense of introducing further stabilization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then eventually one can use the stable complex structure of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the NC structure on vertical tangent bundles to construct a <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant NC structure on <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one obtains an object of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>NC</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> written as</p>
<table id="S24.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}=({{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}=({{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\times G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},E_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}},S_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">with an equivariant smooth evaluation map</p>
<table id="S24.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}:V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X^{{\sf p}+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}:V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X^{{\sf p}+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S24.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">24.3.3. </span>The Kuranishi and derived orbifold multimodule</h5>

<div id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we will insert Morse constraints and couple with the diagonal bimodule of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For critical points <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}_{1},\ldots,\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}_{1},\ldots,\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w,w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w,w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{{{\mathbb{E}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{{{\mathbb{E}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider</p>
<table id="S24.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm ev}^{-1}(W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}}^{\rm us}\times\cdots\times W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}}^{\rm us}\times W_{x^{\prime}}^{\rm st})\times\mathring{M}_{w;w^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{E}}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ev</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>us</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>us</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>st</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}^{-1}(W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}}^{\rm us}\times\cdots\times W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}}^{\rm us}\times W_{x^{\prime}}^{\rm st})\times\mathring{M}_{w;w^{\prime}}^{{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{\mathbb{E}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}}^{\rm us}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>us</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}}^{\rm us}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>st</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm st}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uncompactified unstable resp. stable manifold and <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{M}_{w;w^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{E}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{M}_{w;w^{\prime}}^{\mathbb{\Delta}^{{\mathbb{E}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the uncompactified moduli space of parametrized trajectories in <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we compactify by allowing breaking as follows: at negative ends, the breakings at different gradient rays/lines are independent; at the positive end, the breaking is regarded as breaking in the product space <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, therefore “synchronized.” Let the compactified space be</p>
<table id="S24.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime},w^{\prime}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime},w^{\prime})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then one obtains a stratified smooth Kuranishi space</p>
<table id="S24.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime},w^{\prime}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime},w^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">whose symmetry group is reduced to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define a lift of the Steenrod multimodule to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>NC</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given objects <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=(a_{i},\underline{\smash{x}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=(a_{i},\underline{\smash{x}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}\in{\rm crit}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}\in{\rm crit}f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=(a^{\prime},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=(a^{\prime},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\Omega(a^{\prime}-a_{1}-\cdots-a_{\sf p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\Omega(a^{\prime}-a_{1}-\cdots-a_{\sf p})</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S24.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{x_{1}\cdots x_{{\sf p}}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime}))\in\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>NC</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x_{1}\cdots x_{{\sf p}}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{QSt}}:=K_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}(\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime}))\in\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}^{\rm NC}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The multimodule structural maps are obtained in a very simple way as we only need to consider broken Morse trajectories. Hence one obtains a multimodule <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}},\ldots,{\mathbb{M}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}},\ldots,{\mathbb{M}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>. One is readily to verify that it is a lift of <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies the expected equivariance property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally one takes group quotient by <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{d}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and obtains a lift to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>NC</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can do the outercollaring as the last step, providing the desired AMS construction claimed in Theorem <a href="#S24.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 24.2 (The AMS construction for quantum Steenrod operation). ‣ 24.1.1. The flow categories and bimodules associated to quantum Steenrod operation ‣ 24.1. Geometric setup ‣ 24. Quantum Steenrod Operations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, if we make a different set of choices, then the trivial homotopy from <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself can be lifted to <math id="S24.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> in the equivariant way, between the AMS lifts of two different choices. This finishes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S24.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 24.2 (The AMS construction for quantum Steenrod operation). ‣ 24.1.1. The flow categories and bimodules associated to quantum Steenrod operation ‣ 24.1. Geometric setup ‣ 24. Quantum Steenrod Operations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S24.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">24.4. </span>Comparing to Seidel–Wilkins’ definition in semi-positive case</h4>

<div id="S24.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S24.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-positive, Seidel–Wilkins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> defined their quantum Steenrod operations using the classical approach. We prove that our definition agrees with theirs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 24.6</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S24.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-positive, the quantum Steenrod operation defined above agrees with the definition of Seidel–Wilkins.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 24.7</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Strictly speaking, we are referring to the operations denoted by <math id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\Sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\Sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, where the latter is featured more in recent studies of arithmetic properties of quantum connections on semi-positive symplectic manifolds (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>). We can extend our <math id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QSt_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QSt_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> to define analogs of <math id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\Sigma_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\Sigma_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> using our framework and prove that it reduces to the Seidel–Wilkins operation when <math id="S24.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is semi-positive. Moreover, such an extension is covariantly constant under the quantum connection defined using FOP perturbations. We will not discuss such extensions in this manuscript.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S24.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">24.4.1. </span>The construction of Seidel–Wilkins</h5>

<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The quantum Steenrod operation defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> is based on the classical transversality via adding <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant inhomogeneous terms parametrized by <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. We extend the perturbation to <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider inhomogeneous terms</p>
<table id="S24.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,x}^{\rm eq}:T_{z}S^{2}\to T_{x}X\ {\rm for\ }(\eta,z,x)\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}\times S^{2}\times X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>for</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,x}^{\rm eq}:T_{z}S^{2}\to T_{x}X\ {\rm for\ }(\eta,z,x)\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}\times S^{2}\times X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which satisfies</p>
<table id="S24.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{S^{2},\sigma(\eta),z,x}^{\rm eq}=\nu_{S^{2},\eta,\sigma(z),x}^{\rm eq}\circ D\sigma:T_{z}S^{2}\to T_{x}M\ \ \forall\sigma\in{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{S^{2},\sigma(\eta),z,x}^{\rm eq}=\nu_{S^{2},\eta,\sigma(z),x}^{\rm eq}\circ D\sigma:T_{z}S^{2}\to T_{x}M\ \ \forall\sigma\in{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then one can consider the moduli space of maps <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:S^{2}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:S^{2}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the perturbed Cauchy–Riemann equation. More precisely, consider pairs <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,u)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:S^{2}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:S^{2}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S24.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{J}u(z)+\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,u(z)}^{\rm eq}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{J}u(z)+\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,u(z)}^{\rm eq}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We modify Seidel–Wilkins’ formulation slightly using the Morse model. For a comprehensive comparison with the original Seidel–Wilkins’ setup, which is based on finding explicit cycles representing generators if <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(B{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};{\mathbb{F}}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(B{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};{\mathbb{F}}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Appendix A]</cite>. Recall that one has identified critical points of <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pair of critical points <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w,w^{\prime}\in{\rm crit}f^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>crit</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w,w^{\prime}\in{\rm crit}f^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote</p>
<table id="S24.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}:=\Big\{\eta:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}\ |\ \eta^{\prime}(s)=\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(\eta(s))=0,\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}\eta(s)=w,\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}\eta(s)=w^{\prime}\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}:=\Big\{\eta:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}\ |\ \eta^{\prime}(s)=\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(\eta(s))=0,\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}\eta(s)=w,\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}\eta(s)=w^{\prime}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then elements of <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by its evaluation at <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, one has</p>
<table id="S24.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}=\bigsqcup_{w,w^{\prime}}\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}=\bigsqcup_{w,w^{\prime}}\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then given critical points <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{x}}_{1},\ldots,\underline{\smash{x}}_{{\sf p}},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{x}}_{1},\ldots,\underline{\smash{x}}_{{\sf p}},\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space</p>
<table id="S24.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{{\sf p}};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>SW</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{{\sf p}};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which consists of pairs <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:S^{2}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:S^{2}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth map and <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S24.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{J}u(z)+\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,u(z)}^{\rm eq}=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{J}u(z)+\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,u(z)}^{\rm eq}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S24.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(z_{i})\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}},\ u(z_{\infty})\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>us</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>st</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(z_{i})\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}},\ u(z_{\infty})\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Seidel–Wilkins proved that for a generic inhomogeneous term <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{S^{2}}^{\rm eq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>eq</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{S^{2}}^{\rm eq}</annotation></semantics></math>, whenever the expected dimension of the above moduli space is zero or one, the defining perturbed <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic map equation is transverse.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 24.8</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, Lemma 4.1]</cite>) For a generic <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structure <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant inhomogeneous term <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,x}^{\rm eq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>eq</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{S^{2},\eta,z,x}^{\rm eq}</annotation></semantics></math>, any moduli space <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>SW</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse provided that its expected dimension is at most <math id="S24.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover,</span></p>
<ol id="S24.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S24.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S24.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When the expected dimension is zero, </span><math id="S24.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>SW</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S24.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> consists of finitely many points.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S24.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S24.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When the expected dimension is one, </span><math id="S24.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mi>SW</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathcal{M}}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{1}\cdots\underline{\smash{x}}_{\sf p};\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}^{\rm SW}(a;\mathring{\Delta}_{w;w^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S24.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> admits a natural compactification which is a compact 1-dimensional manifold with boundary.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The only difference between our case and the case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> is that there is no requirement for <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-symmetry in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. However, this can be achieved as the <math id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is free.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then the chain-level quantum Steenrod operation can be defined using the count of zero-dimensional moduli spaces. One can use the standard argument to show that up to chain homotopy the chain-level map is well-defined.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S24.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">24.4.2. </span>The AMS lifts and FOP perturbations with an inhomogeneous term</h5>

<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we define a perturbation of the quantum Steenrod multimodule <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> by using the inhomogeneous term. Notice that each <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds uniquely to a smooth parametrized Morse flow line <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for objects <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{\sf p},w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{\sf p},w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space</p>
<table id="S24.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{M}_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}=\left\{(u,\eta)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X,\ \eta\in W^{\rm us}_{w}\cap W^{\rm st}_{w^{\prime}}\subseteq\widehat{S}^{\infty},\\
\overline{\partial}_{J}u+\nu=0,\\
u(z_{i})\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}},\ u(z_{\infty})\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>us</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>W</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>st</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>us</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>st</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{M}_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}=\left\{(u,\eta)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to X,\ \eta\in W^{\rm us}_{w}\cap W^{\rm st}_{w^{\prime}}\subseteq\widehat{S}^{\infty},\\
\overline{\partial}_{J}u+\nu=0,\\
u(z_{i})\in W^{\rm us}_{\underline{\smash{x}}_{i}},\ u(z_{\infty})\in W^{\rm st}_{\underline{\smash{x}}^{\prime}}\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It can be compactified in a natural way, which is denoted by</p>
<table id="S24.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S24.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These moduli spaces form a multimodule <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{M}},\ldots,\mathbb{M},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{M}}\#{{\mathbb{E}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{M}},\ldots,\mathbb{M},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{M}}\#{{\mathbb{E}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a variant of the <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0</annotation></semantics></math> case considered previously, which is denoted by <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. After outercollaring, using the same method as proving Theorem <a href="#S24.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 24.2 (The AMS construction for quantum Steenrod operation). ‣ 24.1.1. The flow categories and bimodules associated to quantum Steenrod operation ‣ 24.1. Geometric setup ‣ 24. Quantum Steenrod Operations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.2</span></a>, one can construct an AMS lift <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inhomogeneous term <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> can be dealt with by considering the graph <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{graph}(u):\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>graph</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{graph}(u):\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\to\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X</annotation></semantics></math>, where the product <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{QSt}}\times X</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with the sheared almost complex structure (e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Lemma 6.18]</cite>) so that we can go back to the standard situation. The resulting lift in <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 24.9</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When the virtual dimension is zero, the original section is FOP transverse.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the monotonicity condition and the classical transversality condition, when the virtual dimension is zero, the moduli space <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathring{M}_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi>w</mi><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{M}_{x_{1}\cdots x_{\sf p}w;(x^{\prime},w^{\prime})}^{\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with its compactification and there are no orbifold points in the moduli space. The Kuranishi section on the thickening is transverse to the zero section because the Cauchy–Riemann equation is transversely cut out. Then by the <span id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Classical Transversality)</span> property of FOP transverse perturbations, the original section is FOP transverse.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S24.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 24.10</span></span><span id="S24.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S24.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an FOP transverse perturbation of <math id="S24.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for moduli spaces of dimension at most <math id="S24.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbation agrees with the original Kuranishi section.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from the same proof of Lemma <a href="#S13.Thmthm13" title="Lemma 13.13. ‣ 13.8. Proof of Theorem ‣ 13. Proof of the Main Theorems ‣ Part II GEOMETRIC FLOW CATEGORIES, BIMODULES, AND HOMOTOPIES ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.13</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, there is a clear homotopy <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕏</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding an additional parameter <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and changing <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\nu</annotation></semantics></math> in the equation. One can further construct an AMS lift <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a homotopy from <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, one can extend the FOP transverse perturbations <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{0}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}_{1}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to an FOP transverse perturbation on <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S24.SS4.SSS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{H}}^{\mathbb{QSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, in an equivariant way. This leads to a chain homotopy between the chain maps defined with or without the inhomogeneous term. This concludes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S24.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 24.6. ‣ 24.4. Comparing to Seidel–Wilkins’ definition in semi-positive case ‣ 24. Quantum Steenrod Operations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S25" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">25. </span>The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma9" title="Theorem I (Equivariant Floer cohomology). ‣ 1.2.2. Equivariant Floer cohomology ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a></h3>

<div id="S25.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we construct the equivariant Floer theory, including equivariant Floer cohomology, equivariant continuation maps, and equivariant PSS/SSP maps, induced from a prime iteration of a Hamiltonian. Let <math id="S25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime.</p>
</div>
<section id="S25.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">25.1. </span>Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling</h4>

<section id="S25.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.1.1. </span>Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories</h5>

<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a notion called <span id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">coupling</span> of flow categories. This notion cannot be defined on the abstract level but rely on the geometric setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Floer flow category on <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a pair <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Morse flow category on another manifold <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a flow category as follows. The set of objects is</p>
<table id="S25.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}({\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}})={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}({\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}})={\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">whose elements are denoted by <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>. For the morphism space <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{pq}^{{\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the moduli space of pairs <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to the Floer equation connection <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:{\mathbb{R}}\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:{\mathbb{R}}\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a Morse trajectory connection <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>; two pairs <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent if there exists <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S25.EGx78" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ u(\cdot,\cdot)=u^{\prime}(\cdot+s_{0},\cdot),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ u(\cdot,\cdot)=u^{\prime}(\cdot+s_{0},\cdot),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ v(\cdot)=v^{\prime}(\cdot+s_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ v(\cdot)=v^{\prime}(\cdot+s_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">One then compactifies this moduli spaces by allowing “synchronized” breakings (as well as sphere bubblings).</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now we describe the equivariant Floer flow category as a special coupling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 25.1</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a 1-periodic Hamiltonian on <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate. Let <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible almost complex structure. Define the <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-equivariant Floer flow category</span> to be the coupling</p>
<table id="S25.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:={\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{E}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:={\mathbb{F}}\#{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Morse flow category on <math id="S25.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> there is an action</p>
<table id="S25.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}=({\mathcal{A}}_{1},{\mathcal{A}}_{2}):{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}=({\mathcal{A}}_{1},{\mathcal{A}}_{2}):{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S25.EGx79" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{1}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}})={\mathcal{A}}_{H^{\natural p}}(p^{\mathbb{F}}),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{1}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}})={\mathcal{A}}_{H^{\natural p}}(p^{\mathbb{F}}),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{2}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}})=f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {\mathcal{A}}_{2}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}})=f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The grading is given by</p>
<table id="S25.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm deg}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}}):={\rm deg}(p^{\mathbb{F}})+{\rm deg}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm deg}(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{{\mathbb{E}}}):={\rm deg}(p^{\mathbb{F}})+{\rm deg}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Moreover, there is a free action on <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> the twisted Novikov group</p>
<table id="S25.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{\Pi}}_{\sf p}:=\Pi\times{\mathbb{Z}}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}={\mathbb{\Pi}}\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{\Pi}}_{\sf p}:=\Pi\times{\mathbb{Z}}\times{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}={\mathbb{\Pi}}\times{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For different Hamiltonians one can also define an equivariant continuation bimodule. Let <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be pairs as above for <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a Floer data on the infinite cylinder connecting <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration as a Floer daum on the infinite cylinder connecting <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By coupling the bimodule induced from <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the diagonal bimodule <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{{\mathbb{E}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Δ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{\Delta}}^{{{\mathbb{E}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov bimodule over <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.2</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a <math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov flow category enriched in <math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov flow bimodule over <math id="S25.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{F}_{0}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{F}_{0}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Now we state the technical theorem regarding equivariant AMS lifts of <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 25.3</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equivariant AMS lifts)<span id="S25.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S25.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S25.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S25.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant Floer flow category defined in Definition </span><a href="#S25.Thmthm1" title="Definition 25.1. ‣ 25.1.1. Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25.1. Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.1</span></a><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Fix the outercollaring width and an integral action on </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. There is a </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant lift </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of the outercollaring of </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to the category </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, called the </span><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equivariant AMS lift</span><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (subject to the outercollaring width and the integral action).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S25.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S25.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Choose integral actions on </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (not necessarily compatible with respect to the interpolating Floer data). Given equivariant AMS lifts of </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> resp. </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> resp. </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> subject to the outercollaring width and the chosen integral actions, there exists a </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant lift of the outercollaring of </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as a </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-equivariant bimodule over </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">See next Subsection.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Now assuming this theorem, one can define the equivariant Floer chain complex. Since the <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is free, by Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a>, one can find a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant FOP perturbation on <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by taking the isotropy free part of the perturbed zero locus, one obtains a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded Novikov flow category enriched in <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌𝐚𝐧</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{{\bf\Psi}{\bf Man}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that there is still the <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action which preserves all structures. Then applying the abstract construction in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, one obtains a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded cochain complex</p>
<table id="S25.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}},J^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi^{\natural{\sf p}}):=C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}},J^{\natural{\sf p}},\Xi^{\natural{\sf p}}):=C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">which is a graded <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>-module with a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action. The <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed part, denoted by</p>
<table id="S25.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">is called the (Tate) <span id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">equivariant Floer complex</span> of <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, suppose one has two such equivariant complexes</p>
<table id="S25.EGx80" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ CF^{*}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ CF^{*}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ CF^{*}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ CF^{*}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">one can extend, by Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a>, the <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant FOP perturbation over <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which induces a <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant chain map</p>
<table id="S25.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:CF^{*}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}})\to CF^{*}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:CF^{*}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}})\to CF^{*}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">Then, upon taking <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-fixed part, one has the continuation maps</p>
<table id="S25.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{01}:CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>01</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{01}:CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">One can proceed in the same way as proving Theorem <a href="#Thmthma1" title="Theorem A (Floer chain complex). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmthma2" title="Theorem B (Continuation Map). ‣ 1.1.1. Floer complexes and Floer homology ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Therefore, one obtains a well-defined chain homotopy class of cochain complexes of <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, hence a well-defined symplectic invariant</p>
<table id="S25.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">which is a module over <math id="S25.SS1.SSS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">25.2. </span>AMS construction for coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories</h4>

<div id="S25.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we carry out the AMS construction for the coupled flow category.</p>
</div>
<section id="S25.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.2.1. </span>The domain flow category</h5>

<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We modify the previous construction of AMS lifts for non-equivariant Floer theory. We do not consider the monotone flow category of domains as we have to couple it with the Morse flow category. Instead, one describes a flow category <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> with objects the same as those of <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First we choose an integral action <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}}^{\Omega}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{{\mathbb{F}}}^{\Omega}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Floer flow category associated to <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that from the construction in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S15.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 15.4. ‣ 15.1. Integral modification of topological energy ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.4</span></a>, the integral action can be chosen to be <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant. Then for each pair of objects <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{\mathbb{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=(p^{\mathbb{F}},p^{\mathbb{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(q^{\mathbb{F}},q^{\mathbb{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(q^{\mathbb{F}},q^{\mathbb{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>, denote</p>
<table id="S25.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{pq}:={\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}(q^{{\mathbb{F}}})-{\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}(p^{\mathbb{F}})\in{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{pq}:={\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}(q^{{\mathbb{F}}})-{\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}(p^{\mathbb{F}})\in{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We describe an object</p>
<table id="S25.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}=({\mathcal{G}}_{pq},B_{pq},C_{pq})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}=({\mathcal{G}}_{pq},B_{pq},C_{pq})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">as follows. Define <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{xy}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{xy}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math> as considered before. Consider the moduli space of pairs</p>
<table id="S25.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d_{pq}},\ v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d_{pq}},\ v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic of degree <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a flow line connection <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\mathbb{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\mathbb{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Two pairs <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{\prime},v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{\prime},v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent if they differ by a common translation in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we compactify this space in the usual way, requiring the positive constraint</p>
<table id="S25.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(z_{+})=[0,\ldots,0,1]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d_{pq}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(z_{+})=[0,\ldots,0,1]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d_{pq}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and requiring the image of <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in any hyperplane. The space we obtain is denoted by <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose dimension is</p>
<table id="S25.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}B_{pq}=2d_{pq}(d_{pq}+1)+{\rm index}(q^{{\mathbb{E}}})-{\rm index}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}})-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dim</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm dim}_{\mathbb{R}}B_{pq}=2d_{pq}(d_{pq}+1)+{\rm index}(q^{{\mathbb{E}}})-{\rm index}(p^{{\mathbb{E}}})-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{pq}\to B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{pq}\to B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve whose fiber at <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding rational curve in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant family, hence one obtains an object <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that the space <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified by the following poset</p>
<table id="S25.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{pq}=\left\{\Big((d_{0},\ldots,d_{l}),p^{{\mathbb{E}}}r_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots r_{l}^{{\mathbb{E}}}q^{{\mathbb{E}}}\Big)\ \left|\begin{array}[]{c}\ d_{i}\geq 0,\ \displaystyle\sum_{i}d_{i}=d,\\
p^{{\mathbb{E}}}=r_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq r_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq\cdots\leq r_{l}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq r_{l+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=q^{{\mathbb{E}}},\\
d_{i}=0\Longrightarrow r_{i}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\neq r_{i+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pq}=\left\{\Big((d_{0},\ldots,d_{l}),p^{{\mathbb{E}}}r_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots r_{l}^{{\mathbb{E}}}q^{{\mathbb{E}}}\Big)\ \left|\begin{array}[]{c}\ d_{i}\geq 0,\ \displaystyle\sum_{i}d_{i}=d,\\
p^{{\mathbb{E}}}=r_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq r_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq\cdots\leq r_{l}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq r_{l+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=q^{{\mathbb{E}}},\\
d_{i}=0\Longrightarrow r_{i}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\neq r_{i+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that there is a free <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the object <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by the domain rotation and the action on <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>: for <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in B_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in B_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S25.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\cdot\phi=[u\circ\gamma^{-1},\gamma\cdot v]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\cdot\phi=[u\circ\gamma^{-1},\gamma\cdot v]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\circ\gamma^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\circ\gamma^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the composition with the domain rotation and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\cdot v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\cdot v</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. This action commutes with the action of <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> for the following reason: the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>-action comes from the geometry of the target space <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by reparametrizing the maps. On the other hand, there is also an action by <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the corresponding action on <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using the construction of Section <a href="#S14" title="14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, one can define the following morphisms</p>
<table id="S25.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta_{prq}:{\mathcal{C}}_{pr}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{rq}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta_{prq}:{\mathcal{C}}_{pr}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{rq}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">in the category <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From the explicit construction of the domain curves, this morphism is <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant. Hence one obtains a flow category enriched in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.4</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any outercollaring of <math id="S25.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S25.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant lift to <math id="S25.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The rigidification can be constructed in the same way as in Section <a href="#S14" title="14. The Flow Categories, Multimodules, and Homotopies of Domains ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>. The freeness of the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-action allows us to achieve the equivariance easily.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.2.2. </span>Thickenings and group reductions</h5>

<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the construction of Section <a href="#S15" title="15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. We can write down the flow category</p>
<table id="S25.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}^{\mathbb{fr}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}^{\mathbb{fr}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which gives each pair of objects <math id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> an object</p>
<table id="S25.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">of framed maps. This flow category is canonically induced from previous steps and still carries the same symmetry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25.5</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist <math id="S25.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant group reduction schemes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">This can be constructed in the same way as in Proposition <a href="#S15.Thmthm9" title="Proposition 15.9. ‣ 15.2.6. Group reductions ‣ 15.2. Framed maps ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.9</span></a>. The freeness of the action allows us to maintain the symmetry.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hence one obtains a compatible system of maps</p>
<table id="S25.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{pq}:{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to Q_{pq}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Map</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{pq}:{\rm Map}_{pq}^{\rm fr}\to Q_{pq}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next we choose thickening data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25.6</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a <math id="S25.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant and transverse thickening datum on <math id="S25.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The same argument as in Proposition <a href="#S15.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 15.19. ‣ 15.3.3. Transversality ‣ 15.3. The topological Kuranishi lifts ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.19</span></a> applies. The freeness of the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-action allows us to maintain the symmetry.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, we obtain a compatible system of maps</p>
<table id="S25.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{pq}:W_{pq}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{pq}:W_{pq}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{pq}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{pq}/B_{pq}}^{0,1}\otimes TX).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then similar to Definition <a href="#S15.Thmthm14" title="Definition 15.14. ‣ 15.3.2. The thickenings ‣ 15.3. The topological Kuranishi lifts ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.14</span></a>, one can write down the thickened moduli spaces</p>
<table id="S25.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{pq}=(G_{xy},V_{xy},E_{xy},S_{xy})\in{\rm Ob}{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mmultiscripts><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>rig</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{pq}=(G_{xy},V_{xy},E_{xy},S_{xy})\in{\rm Ob}{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">and obtain a lift of the outercollaring of <math id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S25.SS2.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.2.3. </span>Stable smoothing and NC structure</h5>

<div id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, the stable smoothing procedure works in a similar way. Indeed, the projection map <math id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{xy}\to{}_{\lfloor}B_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{xy}\to{}_{\lfloor}B_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> has a rel-<math id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty,1}</annotation></semantics></math>-structure, allowing us to inductively perform stable smoothing. We omit the details. After taking quotient, one obtains a lift to <math id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction of stable complex structure is also the same. Hence one obtains</p>
<table id="S25.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">enriched in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof of (1) of Theorem <a href="#S25.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 25.3 (Equivariant AMS lifts). ‣ 25.1.1. Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25.1. Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.2.4. </span>Equivariant continuation maps</h5>

<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove (2) of Theorem <a href="#S25.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 25.3 (Equivariant AMS lifts). ‣ 25.1.1. Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25.1. Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.3</span></a>, i.e., the lift of the equivariant continuation bimodule, we also modify the previous non-equivariant case. Let <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{1}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the two equivariant flow categories associated to the (iterations of) Hamiltonian <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0},H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0},H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the equivariant continuation bimodule associated to the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration of a flow data on the cylinder interpolating between <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first define a collection of objects</p>
<table id="S25.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}=({\mathcal{G}}_{x_{0};x_{1}},B_{x_{0};x_{1}},C_{x_{0};x_{1}})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}=({\mathcal{G}}_{x_{0};x_{1}},B_{x_{0};x_{1}},C_{x_{0};x_{1}})\in{\rm Ob}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using the same integral modification of the symplectic action <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=(x_{0}^{\mathbb{F}},x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=(x_{0}^{\mathbb{F}},x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, consider solutions <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:{\mathbb{R}}\to S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:{\mathbb{R}}\to S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic of degree <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{x_{0}}-d_{x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{x_{0}}-d_{x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a flow line connecting <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We impose the negative constraint</p>
<table id="S25.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(z_{-})=[1,0,\ldots,0]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(z_{-})=[1,0,\ldots,0]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We compactify this space by allowing sphere bubbles and breakings at both positive and negative infinity. Moreover, breakings on either side are treated in the “synchronized” fashion. In this compactification, we also remove those solutions whose images in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in some hyperplane. Then one obtains the moduli space <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> over which there is the universal curve <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{x_{0};x_{1}}\to B_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{x_{0};x_{1}}\to B_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that the space <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is stratified by the poset</p>
<table id="S25.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{x_{0};x_{1}}:=\left\{\Big((d_{-r}\cdots d_{-1};d_{0};d_{1}\cdots d_{s}),w_{-r}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots w_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}};w_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots w_{s+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\Big)\left|\begin{array}[]{c}d_{i}\geq 0,\ \displaystyle\sum_{i=-r}^{s}d_{i}=d_{x_{0}}-d_{x_{1}}\\
x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=w_{-r}^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=w_{s+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}},\\
d_{i}=0,\ i\neq 0,\Longrightarrow w_{i}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\neq w_{i+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x_{0};x_{1}}:=\left\{\Big((d_{-r}\cdots d_{-1};d_{0};d_{1}\cdots d_{s}),w_{-r}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots w_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}};w_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\cdots w_{s+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\Big)\left|\begin{array}[]{c}d_{i}\geq 0,\ \displaystyle\sum_{i=-r}^{s}d_{i}=d_{x_{0}}-d_{x_{1}}\\
x_{0}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=w_{-r}^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ x_{1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}=w_{s+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}},\\
d_{i}=0,\ i\neq 0,\Longrightarrow w_{i}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\neq w_{i+1}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{x_{0};x_{1}}^{\Omega}\subseteq A_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x_{0};x_{1}}^{\Omega}\subseteq A_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset of tuples such that every <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>-area of some sphere class. Redefine <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the restriction to this open subset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can also do the outercollaring and rigidification construction in the equivariant way. We have the following analogue of Proposition <a href="#S25.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 25.4. ‣ 25.2.1. The domain flow category ‣ 25.2. AMS construction for coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.7</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The collection of objects <math id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a bimodule <math id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup></msup></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose outercollaring admits an enrichment in <math id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S25.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Following Section <a href="#S15" title="15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, we can then construct a bimodule</p>
<table id="S25.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}}^{\mathbb{fr}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mi>𝕒</mi><mi>𝕡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}}^{\mathbb{fr}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">over <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}^{\mathbb{fr}},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{1}}^{\mathbb{fr}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝕄</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕒</mi><msubsup><mi>𝕡</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝕄</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝕒</mi><msubsup><mi>𝕡</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}^{\mathbb{fr}},{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{1}}^{\mathbb{fr}})</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Map}}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐌𝐚𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Map}}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the freeness of the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, we learn that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25.8</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist <math id="S25.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant group reduction schemes lifting <math id="S25.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}}^{\mathbb{fr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕡</mi><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝕗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕣</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Map}_{\mathbb{B}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}_{01}}^{\mathbb{fr}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a flow bimodule enriched in <math id="S25.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf map}}}{}^{\rm fr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐦𝐚𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{{\bf map}}}{}^{\rm fr}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, following Proposition <a href="#S15.Thmthm19" title="Proposition 15.19. ‣ 15.3.3. Transversality ‣ 15.3. The topological Kuranishi lifts ‣ 15. Relatively Smooth AMS lifts ‣ Part III THE AMS CONSTRUCTION ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.19</span></a>, the freeness of the <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-action guarantees the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25.9</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a <math id="S25.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant and transverse thickening datum on <math id="S25.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>𝕠</mi><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a transverse lift to <math id="S25.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Thick}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐡𝐢𝐜𝐤</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>rig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Thick}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the above statements ensure that <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔹</mi><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a lift over <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}{}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Kur}}{}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. The constructions of normal complex structures and coherent smoothings follow from the recurring discussions of this paper. Therefore, by taking the quotient, we get</p>
<table id="S25.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>01</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{B}}_{01}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">enriched in <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant bimodule over <math id="S25.SS2.SSS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{0}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S25.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 25.3 (Equivariant AMS lifts). ‣ 25.1.1. Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25.1. Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.3</span></a> (2).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">25.3. </span>Equivariant PSS and SSP maps</h4>

<div id="S25.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One uses spiked disks used to define the ordinary PSS and SSP maps. Here we would like to define equivariant analogues, which are maps</p>
<table id="S25.EGx81" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \widehat{\Phi}_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{MF}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\to HF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}^{*}(X),\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \widehat{\Phi}_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{MF}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\to HF_{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}^{*}(X),\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{FM}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \Phi_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{\mathbb{FM}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To define these maps, we need to describe certain bimodules, denoted by <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Consider the Morse-to-Floer map first. Choose a Morse flow category <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the symplectic manifold <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\mathbb{M}},g^{\mathbb{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\mathbb{M}},g^{\mathbb{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the trivial product of <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the coupled flow category</p>
<table id="S25.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}:=\mathbb{M}\#{{\mathbb{E}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}:=\mathbb{M}\#{{\mathbb{E}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The equivariant Morse-to-Floer bimodule is a bimodule <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S25.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider spiked disks equipped with a <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant Floer data. Regard <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\cong{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\cong{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a surface with cylindrical end which has a natural <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action which rotate the cylindrical coordinates by multiples of <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2\pi}{{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2\pi}{{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can choose the Floer datum <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant, such that near <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> it is equal to <math id="S25.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}dt</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now we describe the moduli spaces. Choose a pair of objects <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x^{\mathbb{M}},x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}})\in{\rm Ob}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x^{\mathbb{M}},x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}})\in{\rm Ob}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y^{\mathbb{F}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y^{\mathbb{F}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the set of solutions <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma:(-\infty,0]\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:(-\infty,0]\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:{\mathbb{R}}\to S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:{\mathbb{R}}\to S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth maps solving</p>
<table id="S25.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{\mathbb{M}}(\gamma(s))=0,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0,\ v^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(v(s))=0,\ \gamma(0)=u(0)\in X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{\mathbb{M}}(\gamma(s))=0,\ \overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{\mathbb{MF}}}u=0,\ v^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(v(s))=0,\ \gamma(0)=u(0)\in X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and subject to the following constraint</p>
<table id="S25.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{s\to-\infty}\gamma(s)=x^{\mathbb{M}},\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}v(s)=x^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}v(s)=y^{{\mathbb{E}}},\lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)=y^{\mathbb{F}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{s\to-\infty}\gamma(s)=x^{\mathbb{M}},\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}v(s)=x^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}v(s)=y^{{\mathbb{E}}},\lim_{s\to+\infty}u(s,t)=y^{\mathbb{F}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">We compactify the space of such solutions <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> by allowing synchronized breakings of Morse trajectories in <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> at the negative end and synchronized breakings of Floer trajectories in <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> coupled with Morse trajectories in <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> at the positive end. The compactified space is denoted by</p>
<table id="S25.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.4" class="ltx_p">This space is stratified by the poset</p>
<table id="S25.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:=\Big\{(x_{-r}\cdots x_{-1};y_{1}\cdots y_{s})\ |\ x_{-r}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{-1},\ y_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;y_{s}\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:=\Big\{(x_{-r}\cdots x_{-1};y_{1}\cdots y_{s})\ |\ x_{-r}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{-1},\ y_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;y_{s}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.5" class="ltx_p">There are also natural structural maps</p>
<table id="S25.EGx82" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \iota_{x_{1}x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:M_{x_{1}x_{0}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times M_{x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x_{1};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}},\ \ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \iota_{x_{1}x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:M_{x_{1}x_{0}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times M_{x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x_{1};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}},\ \ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ \iota_{x;y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:M_{x;y_{0}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times M_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x;y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ \iota_{x;y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:M_{x;y_{0}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times M_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x;y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p4.6" class="ltx_p">satisfying obvious associativity conditions. Hence one obtains a bimodule <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S25.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an outercollaring width and let the outercollaring of the involved flow categories be</p>
<table id="S25.EGx83" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝕄</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex40.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ {}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The source flow category is already enriched in both <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>. The target flow category <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an AMS lift <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from a lift <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Theorem <a href="#S25.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 25.3 (Equivariant AMS lifts). ‣ 25.1.1. Coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25.1. Equivariant Floer flow category as a coupling ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.3</span></a>). Here we will establish the lift of the bimodule <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>𝔹</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S25.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.10</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an equivariant AMS lift <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a class of lifts of the outercollaring of <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, called an equivariant AMS lift, which is a <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov flow category over <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝕄</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, two different equivariant AMS lifts are homotopic via a <math id="S25.Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov homotopy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this proposition is given momentarily.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now using FOP perturbations one can define the equivariant PSS map on the chain level. Choose an FOP perturbation <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has the associated cochain complex</p>
<table id="S25.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">whose <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part is the equivariant Floer cochain complex</p>
<table id="S25.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as its own FOP perturbation, by the multimodule case of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a>, one can extend the FOP perturbation <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to an FOP perturbation <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔹</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one obtains a cochain map</p>
<table id="S25.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}})\to C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}})\to C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS3.p7.4" class="ltx_p">which is <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear and <math id="S25.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25.11</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant chain homotopy class of <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Phi}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of choices. Moreover, if <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{M}}_{1},{\mathbb{M}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{M}}_{1},{\mathbb{M}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are two Morse flow category associated to two Morse–Smale pair <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},g_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},g_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{2},g_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{2},g_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1},{\mathbb{F}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are Floer flow categories associated to <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the following diagram commutes</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S25.Ex44.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="68.87" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-56.61029744415473px" version="1.1" viewBox="38.07 56.61 209.21 68.87" width="209.21"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 38.07199831010231 68.86534099016708) translate(0,3.54) translate(38.07,0)"><g transform="translate(-38.07,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="67.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S25.Ex44.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}_{1}\#{\mathbb{E}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}_{1}\#{\mathbb{E}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 38.07199831010231 -0.27673578005007266 L 38.07199831010231 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(112.79,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 38.07199831010231 -0.27673578005007266 L 112.79179905730031 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 112.79179905730031 -0.27673578005007266 L 112.79179905730031 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-34.47)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -34.47109722276273 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -34.47109722276273 L 0.27673578005007266 -34.47109722276273" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(112.79,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.84em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.1" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.64)" width="50.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S25.Ex44.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{1}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 141.6890572030234 -11.14645356605438 L 142.24252876312352 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(141.97,0) translate(0,-33.21)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 141.6890572030234 -33.208800332088 L 142.24252876312352 -11.14645356605438" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 141.6890572030234 -33.208800332088 L 142.24252876312352 -33.208800332088" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-38.07,0) translate(0,-45.46) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="67.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S25.Ex44.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}_{2}\#{\mathbb{E}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕄</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C^{*}({\mathbb{M}}_{2}\#{\mathbb{E}})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 38.07199831010231 -45.74057965815042 L 38.07199831010231 -45.18710809805028" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(112.79,0) translate(0,-45.46)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 38.07199831010231 -45.74057965815042 L 112.79179905730031 -45.18710809805028" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 112.79179905730031 -45.74057965815042 L 112.79179905730031 -45.18710809805028" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(112.79,0) translate(0,-45.46) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.84em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.1" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.64)" width="50.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S25.Ex44.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C^{*}(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}_{2}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">up to <math id="S25.Thmthm11.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant chain homotopy. Here the horizontal arrows are the chain-level PSS maps and the vertical arrows are induced from Morse or Floer continuation maps.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, after restricting to the <math id="S25.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part, one obtains a canonical <math id="S25.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map</p>
<table id="S25.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In a similar way, one can construct a chain-level Floer-to-Morse (SSP) map, which induces a well-defined <math id="S25.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map</p>
<table id="S25.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\langle\uptheta\rangle)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(X)\to H^{*}(X;\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\langle\uptheta\rangle).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Just as in the nonequivariant case, we note that the maps <math id="S25.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 25.12</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The maps <math id="S25.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\mathbb{FM},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms on cohomology.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S25.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">On the chain level, we can turn on a exhaustive, complete, and decreasing filtration on both <math id="S25.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}^{*}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}})^{\mathbb{Z}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>#</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{*}(\mathbb{M}\#{\mathbb{E}})^{\mathbb{Z}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> by tracking the degree in <math id="S25.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathtt{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathtt{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uptheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uptheta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then both the equivariant diffferentials and equivariant PSS/SSP maps respect the filtration. On the other hand, geometrically, the constructions in Section <a href="#S25.SS2" title="25.2. AMS construction for coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.2</span></a> can be performed such that the induced differentials and chain maps on the associated graded agrees with the ones coming from the ordinary Morse cohomology, the ordinary Hamiltonian Floer cohomology, and the ordinary PSS/SSP maps. Therefore, based on Theorem <a href="#Thmthma6" title="Theorem F. ‣ 1.1.4. PSS map and isomorphism ‣ 1.1. Main results on Floer complexes ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, we conclude the theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">25.4. </span>AMS construction for equivariant PSS and SSP maps</h4>

<div id="S25.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide detailed construction for the PSS map. The case for SSP map is very similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the non-equivariant case, we first choose an integral action <math id="S25.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}^{\Omega}:{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\to{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with respect to the bimodule <math id="S25.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or more precisely, compatible with the Floer data).</p>
</div>
<section id="S25.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.4.1. </span>The domain bimodule for the AMS construction</h5>

<div id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first describe the analogue of the space of domains, i.e., a bimodule enriched in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the AMS construction for <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Subsection <a href="#S25.SS2" title="25.2. AMS construction for coupling of Floer and Morse flow categories ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.2</span></a>) provides a flow category <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ℂ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}^{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are the same as those of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the flow category <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not yet have such a corresponding flow category enriched in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define such a flow category as follows. For each pair <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0},x_{1}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0},x_{1}\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S25.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}=(G,B_{x_{0}x_{1}},C)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}=(G,B_{x_{0}x_{1}},C)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the trivial group, <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{x_{0}x_{1}}=M_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{x_{0}x_{1}}=M_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of flow lines between <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, regarded as a family of empty curves over <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{x_{0}x_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{x_{0}x_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. After outercollaring, we denote this rather trivial flow category enriched in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S25.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As the concatenation of Morse flow lines does not involve stabilization and the Lie groups do not change, this flow category is automatically enriched in the rigidified version <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>. However we do not consider stable complex structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we describe a bimodule over <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a pair of objects</p>
<table id="S25.EGx84" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S25.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ x=(x^{\mathbb{M}},x^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ x=(x^{\mathbb{M}},x^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S25.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ y=(y^{\mathbb{F}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ y=(y^{\mathbb{F}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> determines an integer <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the difference of the symplectic area of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the integrally modified symplectic action of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the the space of triples <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\gamma,\phi,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\gamma,\phi,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma:(-\infty,0]\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:(-\infty,0]\to X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{\mathbb{R}}\times S^{1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth maps, solving the following equation</p>
<table id="S25.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{\mathbb{M}}(\gamma(s))=0,\ \overline{\partial}\phi=0,\ \eta^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(\eta(s))=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{\mathbb{M}}(\gamma(s))=0,\ \overline{\partial}\phi=0,\ \eta^{\prime}(s)+\nabla f^{{\mathbb{E}}}(\eta(s))=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">satisfying the following asymptotic constraints</p>
<table id="S25.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{s\to-\infty}\gamma(s)=x^{\mathbb{M}},\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}\eta(s)=x^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}\eta(s)=y^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝕄</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{s\to-\infty}\gamma(s)=x^{\mathbb{M}},\ \lim_{s\to-\infty}\eta(s)=x^{{\mathbb{E}}},\ \lim_{s\to+\infty}\eta(s)=y^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and the constraint</p>
<table id="S25.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(+\infty)=[0,\ldots,0,1]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(+\infty)=[0,\ldots,0,1]\in\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Notice that there is no requirement for <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(-\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(-\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. We compactify this space by allowing sychrnoized breakings in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\times\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> at the negative end and synchronized breakings of holomorphic cylinders in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and Morse trajectories in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we remove those elements whose images in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{CP}^{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in any hyperplane. Denote this space by</p>
<table id="S25.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{x;y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{x;y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p">Then this is a smooth stratified manifold with a <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{d_{x;y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action. On top of it there is the universal curve</p>
<table id="S25.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{x;y}\to B_{x;y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{x;y}\to B_{x;y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.6" class="ltx_p">Then the triple</p>
<table id="S25.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x;y}:=({\mathcal{G}}_{x;y},B_{x;y},C_{x;y})\ {\rm where}\ {\mathcal{G}}_{x;y}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{x;y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x;y}:=({\mathcal{G}}_{x;y},B_{x;y},C_{x;y})\ {\rm where}\ {\mathcal{G}}_{x;y}={\mathcal{G}}_{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.7" class="ltx_p">is an object of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐂𝐮𝐫𝐯𝐞</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Curve}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can also define structural maps</p>
<table id="S25.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{1};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}},\ {\mathcal{C}}_{x;y_{0}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{x;y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{1};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{x_{0};y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}},\ {\mathcal{C}}_{x;y_{0}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times{\mathcal{C}}_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{x;y_{1}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.8" class="ltx_p">This provide a flow bimodule <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕠</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕞</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Dom}^{{\mathbb{M}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}};\mathbb{Dom}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. One can check that this bimodule is <math id="S25.SS4.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">25.4.2. </span>Thickening</h5>

<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can consider the space of framed maps <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Map</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> whose elements are <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,u,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,u,F)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in B_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in B_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:C_{\phi}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:C_{\phi}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an admissible map, and <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a frame determined by <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and the integral symplectic form <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> carries the information of a flow ray in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and we do not require that the evaluation of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the value of the flow ray at <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. One chooses an equivariant group reduction scheme</p>
<table id="S25.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{x;y}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X)\to Q_{x;y}:=Q_{d_{x;y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>Map</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mi>fr</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x;y}:{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X)\to Q_{x;y}:=Q_{d_{x;y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which extends the group reduction scheme chosen for the AMS construction for <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, we also choose thickening data</p>
<table id="S25.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{x;y}:W_{x;y}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{x;y}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{x,y}/B_{x;y}}^{0,1}\otimes TX)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{x;y}:W_{x;y}\to\Gamma_{c}(\mathring{C}_{x;y}\times X,\Lambda_{\mathring{C}_{x,y}/B_{x;y}}^{0,1}\otimes TX)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">extending the thickening data chosen for the AMS construction for <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the <span id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">thickening</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\check{V}_{x;y}:=\left\{(\phi,u,F,e)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}(\phi,u,F)\in{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X),\ e\in W_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\\
\overline{\partial}u+\nu_{x;y}(e)=0,\ \phi=\phi_{F}\end{array}\right.\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="2.0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>Map</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>fr</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{V}_{x;y}:=\left\{(\phi,u,F,e)\ \left|\ \begin{array}[]{c}(\phi,u,F)\in{}_{\lfloor}{\rm Map}_{x;y}^{\rm fr}(X),\ e\in W_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\\
\overline{\partial}u+\nu_{x;y}(e)=0,\ \phi=\phi_{F}\end{array}\right.\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The obstruction bundle <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the trivial bundle with fiber <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{x;y}\oplus Q_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{x;y}\oplus Q_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Kuranishi section is</p>
<table id="S25.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{x;y}(\phi,u,F,e)=(e,\lambda_{x;y}(\phi,u,F))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{x;y}(\phi,u,F,e)=(e,\lambda_{x;y}(\phi,u,F)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the construction so far in the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.13</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The collection of Kuranishi spaces</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{K}_{x;y}:=(G_{x;y},\check{V}_{x;y},E_{x;y},S_{x;y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{K}_{x;y}:=(G_{x;y},\check{V}_{x;y},E_{x;y},S_{x;y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">together with the structural maps</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}M_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times K_{x_{1};y}\to K_{x_{0};y}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕄</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}M_{x_{0}x_{1}}^{\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\times K_{x_{1};y}\to K_{x_{0};y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{x;y_{0}}\times K_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to K_{x;y_{1}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x;y_{0}}\times K_{y_{0}y_{1}}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to K_{x;y_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">define a bimodule <math id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝕄</mi><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>;</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}\mathbb{M}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S25.Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>rig</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a lift of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as we did not impose the matching condition in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We then construct the vertical NC structure. Similar to the case for <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction can be carried out in a <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant fashion. Hence one obtains a bimodule enriched in <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>rel</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf S^{\rm rel}Kur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We still apply the same smoothing strategy to obtain a stable smoothing of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbb{M}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbb{M}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To simplify notations, we still denote by the same symbol. Moreover, we may require that the natural map</p>
<table id="S25.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm ev}:\check{V}_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to X\times X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ev</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm ev}:\check{V}_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to X\times X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">is a submersion, hence transverse to the diagonal. Define</p>
<table id="S25.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:={\rm ev}^{-1}(\Delta_{X})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ev</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x;y}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}:={\rm ev}^{-1}(\Delta_{X})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">and define <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction of <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{K}_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{K}_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> to this closed submanifold. Finally, incoporating the stable complex structure on the domain bimodule, one obtains an NC structure. We summarize the construction as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S25.Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 25.14</span></span><span id="S25.Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S25.Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The collection of Kuranishi spaces <math id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{x;y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{x;y}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the restriction of the structural morphisms of Proposition <a href="#S25.Thmthm13" title="Proposition 25.13. ‣ 25.4.2. Thickening ‣ 25.4. AMS construction for equivariant PSS and SSP maps ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.13</span></a> define a lift of <math id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐒𝐊𝐮𝐫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf SKur}}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a bimodule over <math id="S25.Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝕄</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({}_{\lfloor}{\mathbb{M}}\#{\mathbb{E}};\hat{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, by taking the quotient by the Lie group actions, we obtain <math id="S25.SS4.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}^{\mathbb{MF},{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can further follow the machinery of this paper to address the invariance property. This concludes Proposition <a href="#S25.Thmthm10" title="Proposition 25.10. ‣ 25.3. Equivariant PSS and SSP maps ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S26" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">26. </span>The Equivariant Pair-of-Pants—Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a></h3>

<div id="S26.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The equivariant pair-of-pants product can be viewed as a Floer-theoretic analogue of the quantum Steenrod operation. It is defined via a multimodule associated to a Floer domain having a <math id="S26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-symmetry. The main results regarding such a construction is stated in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. Given the recurring nature of our arguments, we highlight the distinctive features.</p>
</div>
<section id="S26.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">26.1. </span>The equivariant pair-of-pants multimodule</h4>

<div id="S26.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,\omega,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,\omega,J)</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact almost Kähler manifold. Again, we do not discuss the dependence on <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, which could be done without new difficulty. Let <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Floer flow category of a Hamiltonian <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the coupling of the Floer flow category of the <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the Morse flow category <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote27" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">27</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">27</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">27</span>
                
                
                
              In general one could allow the <math id="footnote27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-fold iteration of a different Hamiltonian.</span></span></span> We describe a multimodule <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> appears <math id="S26.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> times in the source.</p>
</div>
<section id="S26.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.1.1. </span>The Floer domain and the equation</h5>

<div id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\ldots,z_{{\sf p}}\in{\mathbb{C}}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\ldots,z_{{\sf p}}\in{\mathbb{C}}\subseteq\mathbb{CP}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be all the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th roots of unity. Let</p>
<table id="S26.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma:=\mathbb{CP}^{1}\setminus\{z_{1},\ldots,z_{{\sf p}},z_{\infty}=\infty\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma:=\mathbb{CP}^{1}\setminus\{z_{1},\ldots,z_{{\sf p}},z_{\infty}=\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">be the punctured sphere, which has a holomorphic <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action permuting the finite punctures. Choose a <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant Floer datum <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction on the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> negative ends are <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Hdt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Hdt</annotation></semantics></math> and whose restriction on the positive end is the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}dt</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, one can choose different <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> for negative and positive ends as in the setup of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>, but we spell out the argument for <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-}=H_{+}=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-}=H_{+}=H</annotation></semantics></math> to simplify the notations. Solutions to the Floer equation</p>
<table id="S26.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}u=0,\ u:\Sigma\to X" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}u=0,\ u:\Sigma\to X</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">converge to periodic orbits of <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> at negative ends and periodic orbits of <math id="S26.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the positive end.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.1.2. </span>The moduli spaces and the multimodule</h5>

<div id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the morphism spaces of the multimodule <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a collection of objects</p>
<table id="S26.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},\ldots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}},x^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in\underbrace{{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}}_{{\sf p}}\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{E}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Ob</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},\ldots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}},x^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in\underbrace{{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}}_{{\sf p}}\times{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{E}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and an object <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=(y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Ob</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=(y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}},y^{{\mathbb{E}}})\in{\rm Ob}{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the flow category associated with the Hamiltonian <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we describe a moduli space as follows. Consider pairs</p>
<table id="S26.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Sigma\to X,\ v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Sigma\to X,\ v:{\mathbb{R}}\to\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\partial}_{\sigma^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}}u=0</annotation></semantics></math> converging to <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th negative cylindrical ends, <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\mathbb{F}^{\natural{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math> at the positive cylindrical end, and where <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is a Morse flow line of <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> at negative and positive ends respectively. We compactify the space of such solutions in the following way: while allowing sphere bubbling, the breakings at negative ends are treated as independent breaking while the breakings at positive ends are synchronized. Let the compactified space be <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are obvious inclusions given by breakings of the domains. For <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{{\mathbb{F}}}\leq w_{i}^{{\mathbb{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{{\mathbb{F}}}\leq w_{i}^{{\mathbb{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th source flow category, there is a map</p>
<table id="S26.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}w_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}}^{{\mathbb{F}}}\times M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{i-1}^{\mathbb{F}}w_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}x_{i+1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>w</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}w_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}}^{{\mathbb{F}}}\times M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{i-1}^{\mathbb{F}}w_{i}^{\mathbb{F}}x_{i+1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq w^{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{{\mathbb{E}}}\leq w^{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a map</p>
<table id="S26.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x^{{\mathbb{E}}}w^{{\mathbb{E}}}}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\times M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}w^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathfrak{FSt}}};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔱</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x^{{\mathbb{E}}}w^{{\mathbb{E}}}}^{{\mathbb{E}}}\times M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}w^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathfrak{FSt}}};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\leq y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}^{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a map</p>
<table id="S26.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};w}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};w}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}\times M_{wy}^{\mathbb{F}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}\to M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{\mathbb{X}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">These maps satisfy the conditions required for a multimodule. Hence one obtains a multimodule <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{F},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{F},\ldots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> enriched in <math id="S26.SS1.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐓𝐨𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\bf Top}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.1.3. </span>The symmetry</h5>

<div id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the Novikov monoid</p>
<table id="S26.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}:=\underbrace{\Pi\times\cdots\times\Pi}_{{\sf p}}\times{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>𝗉</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}:=\underbrace{\Pi\times\cdots\times\Pi}_{{\sf p}}\times{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">There is a <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}</annotation></semantics></math> by isomorphisms of Novikov monoids given by permuting the first <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math> factors in the above product. Then we can form the semi-direct product <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}\rtimes{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}\rtimes{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can view the multimodule <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a weak bimodule over <math id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{F}}\times{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{F}}\times{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>. On the source weak flow category, it has the action</p>
<table id="S26.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},\ldots,x^{\mathbb{F}}_{\sf p},x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}})=({\mathcal{A}}_{H}(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}),\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}),{\mathcal{A}}_{{{\mathbb{E}}}}(x^{{\mathbb{E}}}))\in{\mathbb{R}}^{{\sf p}+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝔽</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}},\ldots,x^{\mathbb{F}}_{\sf p},x^{{{\mathbb{E}}}})=({\mathcal{A}}_{H}(x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}),\ldots,{\mathcal{A}}_{H}(x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}),{\mathcal{A}}_{{{\mathbb{E}}}}(x^{{\mathbb{E}}}))\in{\mathbb{R}}^{{\sf p}+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S26.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S26.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 26.1</span></span><span id="S26.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S26.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The collection of moduli spaces <math id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔽</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x_{1}^{\mathbb{F}}\cdots x_{\sf p}^{\mathbb{F}}x^{{\mathbb{E}}};y}^{{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}}</annotation></semantics></math> define a Novikov multimodule over <math id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{F}},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}},\cdots,{\mathbb{F}},{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is equivariant with respect to the natural morphism</span></p>
<table id="S26.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}\to\mathbb{\Pi},\ (a_{1},\ldots,a_{\sf p},l)\mapsto(a_{1}+\cdots+a_{\sf p},l)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathbb{\Pi}^{({\sf p})}\to\mathbb{\Pi},\ (a_{1},\ldots,a_{\sf p},l)\mapsto(a_{1}+\cdots+a_{\sf p},l)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of Novikov monoids. Moreover, as a weak bimodule over <math id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{F}}\times{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>;</mo><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{F}}\times\cdots\times{\mathbb{F}}\times{{\mathbb{E}}};{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it is equivariant with respect to <math id="S26.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.SS1.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Straightforward.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.1.4. </span>Regularization and the equivariant pair-of-pants product</h5>

<div id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an outercollaring width and a compatible choice of integral actions. Recall that the outercollarings of <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔽</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> both admit AMS lifts to <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}{}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}{}_{\rm rig}^{\rm NC}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S26.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 26.2</span></span><span id="S26.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S26.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a lift of the outercollaring of <math id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐝𝐎𝐫𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>NC</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>rig</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\lfloor}\underline{\smash{\bf dOrb}}^{\rm NC}_{\rm rig}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a <math id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant Novikov multimodule over <math id="S26.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\tilde{\mathbb{F}},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}},{}_{\lfloor}{{\mathbb{E}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝔼</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>;</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\mathbb{F}},\ldots,\tilde{\mathbb{F}},{}_{\lfloor}{{\mathbb{E}}};\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The construction is analogous to the AMS construction for a general multimodule induced from a smooth Floer domain, except that the moduli spaces curves now has the parametrization factor from the moduli spaces of Morse trajectories in <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Section <a href="#S25.SS4.SSS1" title="25.4.1. The domain bimodule for the AMS construction ‣ 25.4. AMS construction for equivariant PSS and SSP maps ‣ 25. The Equivariant Floer theory for iterations—Proof of Theorem ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25.4.1</span></a>. One can carry out the construction almost verbatim; the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariance condition can be achieved because the action is free.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be an FOP transverse perturbation on <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant FOP transverse perturbation on <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF(H)</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the associated complex over <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. over <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part of <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the equivariant complex <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math>. By the multimodule case of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma14" title="Theorem N. ‣ 8.2. FOP perturbations on flow categories, multimodules, and homotopies ‣ 8. FOP Perturbations ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">N</span></a>, one can extend these FOP transverse perturbations to a <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant FOP transverse perturbation <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmthm18" title="Proposition 7.18. ‣ 7.2.3. Chain maps and homotopies ‣ 7.2. Algebraic structures without symmetry ‣ 7. Algebraic Structures ‣ Part I FORMAL NOTIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>, it induces a <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf F}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf F}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear chain map</p>
<table id="S26.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{P}:\overbrace{CF(H)\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}\cdots\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CF(H)}^{{\sf p}}\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}C({{\mathbb{E}}})\to C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{P}:\overbrace{CF(H)\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}\cdots\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CF(H)}^{{\sf p}}\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}C({{\mathbb{E}}})\to C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariance condition on the multimodule <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕥</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbb{X}}^{\mathbb{FSt}}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\rm FSt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>FSt</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\rm FSt}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map</p>
<table id="S26.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{P}:\left(\overbrace{CF(H)\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}\cdots\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CF(H)}^{{\sf p}}\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CM(f^{{\mathbb{E}}})\right)\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}^{({\sf p})}}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\to C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mi>𝗉</mi></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐅</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝗉</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></munderover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>𝔼</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mo>⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msubsup></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{P}:\left(\overbrace{CF(H)\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}\cdots\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CF(H)}^{{\sf p}}\widehat{\underset{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}{\otimes}}CM(f^{{\mathbb{E}}})\right)\underset{\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{{\mathbb{\Pi}}^{({\sf p})}}}{\otimes}\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}\to C(\mathring{\mathbb{F}}^{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which is <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant and <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck">Π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{{\bf F}_{\sf p}}^{\mathbb{\Pi}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Restricting to the <math id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part, one obtains the chain-level equivariant pair-of-pants product</p>
<table id="S26.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:C_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to CF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S26.SS1.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">hence a map on the cohomology level (denoted by the same notation)</p>
<table id="S26.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S26.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:H_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(CF(H)^{\otimes{\sf p}})\to HF_{{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}}(H^{\natural{\sf p}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">26.2. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a></h4>

<div id="S26.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As above, we write down the proof for <math id="S26.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-}=H_{+}=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-}=H_{+}=H</annotation></semantics></math> to simplify the notations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S26.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.2.1. </span>Independence of choices</h5>

<div id="S26.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S26.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 26.3</span></span><span id="S26.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S26.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is well-defined up to <math id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant chain homotopies. Therefore, restricting to the <math id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant part, <math id="S26.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined up to chain homotopies.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Two concrete chain level constructions can be connected by a homotopy: we can carry out the construction of multimodule homotopies in the <math id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant setting using families of curves with parametrization having the additional factor from the moduli spaces of Morse trajectories in <math id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{S}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{S}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariance can be achieved due to the freeness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, following the definition of <math id="S26.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it FSt}_{{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐹𝑆𝑡</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it FSt}_{{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>, we see that the Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation does not depend on any choices after passing to cohomology. This finishes proving Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a> (1) and (3).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.2.2. </span>Isomorphism on cohomology</h5>

<div id="S26.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a> (2) follows from a stronger assertion in the quantitative setting, Corollary <a href="#S27.Thmthm10" title="Corollary 27.10. ‣ 27.4. Equivariant pair-of-pants product ‣ 27. Quantitative Results about Equivariant Floer Theory ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27.10</span></a>, which is proven in the next Section. The idea is that instead of directly constructing a chain-homotopy inverse of <math id="S26.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> by dualizing the equivariant pair-of-pants product, we turn on the action filtration on the (equivariant) Floer chain complexes, which the operation <math id="S26.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> respects, so that the induced operation on the associated graded reduces to the equivariant pants product on local Floer cohomology. Then, by appealing to the known isomorphism property in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> in the aspherical setting, which, in particular, applies to local Floer theory, we conclude that <math id="S26.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism on cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S26.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 26.4</span></span><span id="S26.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S26.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S26.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using Proposition <a href="#S23.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 23.3. ‣ 23.1.1. The algebraic construction ‣ 23.1. Tate Equivariant Cohomology ‣ 23. Equivariant Morse and Floer Cohomology ‣ Part IV EQUIVARIANT FLOER THEORY AND STEENROD OPERATIONS ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23.3</span></a>, we further see that <math id="S26.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it FSt}_{{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐹𝑆𝑡</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it FSt}_{{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. This is the generalization of the localization results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> for general compact symplectic manifolds in Hamiltonian Floer theory. We expect that the method in this paper can also be used for proving an analogous result for the fixed point Floer cohomology associated with a general symplectic automorphism, though one needs a slight different regularization procedure.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S26.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">26.2.3. </span>The multimodule homotopy</h5>

<div id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we prove the commutativity of Diagram (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In item 4 ‣ Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>) in Theorem <a href="#Thmthma10" title="Theorem J (Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation). ‣ 1.2.3. Floer-theoretic Steenrod operation ‣ 1.2. Main results on equivariant Floer theory and Steenrod operations ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Integral Hamiltonian Floer Theory: Foundations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a> (4). The core of the proof is the following chain-level assertion. As we only need the cohomological level conclusion, one fixes a Hamiltonian <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=H_{-}=H_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=H_{-}=H_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\natural{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">♮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\natural{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate. Moreover, fix a Morse–Smale pair <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f,g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with associated Morse flow category <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝕄</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\smash{\mathbb{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (whose cohomology is isomorphic to <math id="S26.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X;\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X;\Lambda_{\mathbb{Z}}^{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S26.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S26.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 26.5</span></span><span id="S26.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S26.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S26.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S26.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following diagram commutes up to <math id="S26.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbb{Z}}_{\sf p}}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant homotopy.</span></p>
